insert into public.gov_exams (category,name,summary,content,links,display_order) values ('BPSC','BPSC AE','BPSC Assistant Engineer Examination','[{"heading":"Overview","body":["BPSC Assistant Engineer Examination"]},{"heading":"The Philosophy Behind the Examination","body":["The BPSC AE exam is designed to select candidates who not only possess strong theoretical knowledge but also have the aptitude to apply it to practical, state-specific challenges. The core philosophy is to identify individuals with:","Core Technical Proficiency: A deep and clear understanding of the fundamental principles of their engineering discipline.","Analytical and Problem-Solving Skills: The ability to analyze technical problems and devise efficient, feasible solutions within the administrative framework.","General Awareness: A solid understanding of general studies, including the history, geography, and economy of Bihar, which is crucial for effective public service in the state.","Linguistic Competence: Basic proficiency in both Hindi and English, necessary for administrative communication."]},{"heading":"The Services and Disciplines","body":["The BPSC AE exam is primarily conducted for recruitment into three core engineering disciplines. Selected candidates are appointed as Assistant Engineers in various key government departments."]},{"heading":"The Selection Process","body":["The BPSC AE selection process is centered around a comprehensive written examination. Unlike some other engineering exams, all papers in the BPSC AE written test are of an objective (Multiple Choice Questions) nature.","Stage 1: Written Examination (Objective Type): This is the main stage of the selection process. It consists of six papers conducted over multiple days. Four of these are compulsory common papers for all disciplines, and two are optional papers specific to the candidate''s engineering branch. The performance in this written test is the primary determinant of the final merit list.","Stage 2: Evaluation of Experience/Weightage (If applicable): In some recruitment cycles, the BPSC may assign weightage for prior work experience in government or semi-government organizations. This is specified in the official notification.","Stage 3: Document Verification / Interview: Candidates who qualify in the written examination are called for document verification. Depending on the specific notification, an interview may or may not be part of the final selection process. Aspirants must carefully read the official advertisement for clarity on this stage.","The Final Verdict: Merit and Allocation","The final merit list is prepared based on the total marks obtained in the four compulsory and optional papers of the Written Examination (excluding the qualifying papers) . Some recruitments may also include marks from an interview or weightage for experience. Based on their final rank, category, and the vacancies available, candidates are recommended for appointment to various departments within the Bihar Government."]},{"heading":"Typical Timeline (Indicative)","body":["The timeline for the BPSC AE examination can be irregular and varies significantly from one recruitment cycle to the next. Aspirants must regularly check the official BPSC website for updates. The following is a generalized sequence of events.","Disclaimer: This timeline is purely indicative. BPSC notifications are not released on a fixed annual schedule. Always refer to the official BPSC website for actual dates.","Phase 1: Notification Release","The official notification is published on the BPSC website (bpsc.bih.nic.in). This is the most crucial document, containing vacancy details, eligibility, syllabus, and exam dates. This can happen at any time of the year.","Phase 2: Application Period","The online application window typically opens for about one month. Candidates must register and fill out the form carefully during this period.","Phase 3: Admit Card Release","Admit cards are usually made available for download on the BPSC website approximately 10-15 days before the scheduled examination dates.","Phase 4: Written Examination","The six papers of the written exam are conducted over two or more days. The schedule is detailed in the admit card.","Phase 5: Result of Written Examination","The BPSC declares the results of the written exam after a few months. A list of qualified candidates is published, who are then eligible for the next stage.","Phase 6: Document Verification / Interview","Qualified candidates are called for document verification and, if applicable, an interview. This phase usually occurs a few weeks after the written result declaration.","Phase 7: Final Result Declaration","The final merit list is published, containing the names and roll numbers of the candidates recommended for appointment."]},{"heading":"Eligibility Criteria","body":["Candidates must ensure they meet all the eligibility criteria set by the BPSC before applying. These criteria are strictly enforced. Eligibility is determined by Nationality, Age Limit, and Educational Qualification.","Note: The details provided here are based on general BPSC norms. Always refer to the specific official notification for the exact requirements of the recruitment cycle.","1. Nationality","A candidate must be a citizen of India.","2. Age Limit","The age of a candidate is calculated as of a specific cut-off date, typically the 1st of August of the notification year.","Minimum Age: 21 years .","Maximum Age (Unreserved Male): 37 years .","The BPSC provides relaxations in the upper age limit for various categories as per Bihar Government rules:","3. Educational Qualification","The minimum educational requirement is a regular degree in the relevant engineering discipline:","A candidate must possess a B.E./B.Tech degree in Civil, Mechanical, or Electrical Engineering from an AICTE-approved institution or a university recognized by the UGC.","Degrees obtained through distance learning are generally not considered valid for this post.","Candidates in their final year are usually eligible to apply, provided they can produce their final degree certificate by the time of document verification or a specified cut-off date mentioned in the notification.","4. Number of Attempts","There is no limit on the number of attempts a candidate can make for the BPSC AE examination, provided they are within the prescribed age limit."]},{"heading":"Examination Pattern & Scheme","body":["The BPSC AE examination consists of a written test comprising six papers. All papers are objective (Multiple Choice Questions - MCQ). The examination is designed to test language skills, general awareness, and deep technical knowledge.","Part A: Compulsory Papers (For all candidates)","There are four compulsory papers. The first two are qualifying in nature, while the next two are counted for merit.","Part B: Optional Papers (Discipline Specific)","Candidates must choose one of the engineering disciplines (Civil, Mechanical, or Electrical) and appear for the two corresponding papers.","Mode of Exam: All papers are conducted offline (pen-and-paper OMR based).","Question Type: All questions are Multiple Choice Questions (MCQs).","Negative Marking: Yes, there is a penalty for incorrect answers. Typically, 1/4th of the mark (0.25 marks) is deducted for each wrong answer.","Qualifying Papers: Marks obtained in General English and General Hindi are not counted for the final merit list. However, a candidate must score at least 30 marks out of 100 in each of these papers to be eligible for evaluation of their other papers.","Final Merit Calculation","The final merit list is prepared based on the cumulative score of the four merit-based papers.","General Studies: 100 Marks + General Engineering Science: 100 Marks + Optional Paper V: 100 Marks + Optional Paper VI: 100 Marks = Grand Total: 400 Marks"]},{"heading":"Application Process","body":["The application process for the BPSC Assistant Engineer examination is conducted entirely online through the official BPSC portal. Candidates must follow the steps precisely to ensure their application is submitted successfully.","Crucial Note: Always refer to the official notification for the exact application start/end dates, fee structure, and specific document requirements for the current recruitment cycle.","Step-by-Step Guide to Applying Online","Step 1: New Registration First-time applicants must register on the BPSC online portal to create their account. Visit the official BPSC website: bpsc.bih.nic.in or onlinebpsc.bihar.gov.in . Find the link for \"Online Application\" and select the relevant AE examination. Click on \"New Registration\" and fill in your basic details, a valid Email ID , and a Mobile Number . You will receive an OTP on your mobile/email for verification, followed by a Registration ID and Password.","First-time applicants must register on the BPSC online portal to create their account.","Visit the official BPSC website: bpsc.bih.nic.in or onlinebpsc.bihar.gov.in .","Find the link for \"Online Application\" and select the relevant AE examination.","Click on \"New Registration\" and fill in your basic details, a valid Email ID , and a Mobile Number .","You will receive an OTP on your mobile/email for verification, followed by a Registration ID and Password.","Step 2: Fill the Application Form Log in using the Registration ID and Password received. Fill in all required fields in the online application form with extreme care. Personal Details: Name, Father''s/Mother''s name, Address, Category (UR, EWS, BC, EBC, SC, ST), etc. Educational Details: Marks and passing year for 10th, 12th, and your B.E./B.Tech degree.","Log in using the Registration ID and Password received.","Fill in all required fields in the online application form with extreme care.","Personal Details: Name, Father''s/Mother''s name, Address, Category (UR, EWS, BC, EBC, SC, ST), etc.","Educational Details: Marks and passing year for 10th, 12th, and your B.E./B.Tech degree.","You will be required to upload scanned copies of your photograph, signature, and other relevant documents as per the specifications.","Recent Passport-sized Photograph.","Scanned Signature (in both Hindi and English).","Educational Certificates (10th, B.E./B.Tech degree).","Category Certificate (If applying for reservation).","Bihar Domicile Certificate (If claiming reservation).","Ensure all scans are clear and within the prescribed size (e.g., 20-50 KB) and format (e.g., .jpg) limits.","Step 4: Application Fee Payment After filling and verifying all details, proceed to the payment gateway to pay the application fee. The fee varies by category (e.g., General, SC/ST, Females of Bihar). Payment is accepted via online modes: Credit Card, Debit Card, Net Banking, or UPI . The application fee is generally non-refundable.","After filling and verifying all details, proceed to the payment gateway to pay the application fee.","The fee varies by category (e.g., General, SC/ST, Females of Bihar).","Payment is accepted via online modes: Credit Card, Debit Card, Net Banking, or UPI .","The application fee is generally non-refundable.","Step 5: Final Submission and Printout After successful payment, you must do a final submission of your application. Review your entire application form one last time using the \"Preview\" option before final submission. Click the \"Submit Application Form\" button. Download a copy of the filled application form (PDF) and the payment receipt. Keep these safe for all future reference.","After successful payment, you must do a final submission of your application.","Review your entire application form one last time using the \"Preview\" option before final submission.","Click the \"Submit Application Form\" button.","Download a copy of the filled application form (PDF) and the payment receipt. Keep these safe for all future reference."]},{"heading":"Syllabus","body":["The BPSC AE syllabus is extensive and covers a wide range of subjects, from general aptitude to core engineering disciplines. A detailed understanding of the syllabus is essential for effective preparation."]},{"heading":"Compulsory Papers Syllabus","body":["Detection of errors in sentences and phrases.","Filling of blanks with appropriate articles, prepositions, etc.","Vocabulary and Spellings.","Grammar: Tenses, Verbs, Nouns, Pronouns, Adjectives, Adverbs.","Sentence structure and completion.","Synonyms and Antonyms.","Idioms and Phrases.","हिन्दी व्याकरण (Grammar).","वाक्य संरचना और सुधार (Sentence Structure and Correction).","संज्ञा, सर्वनाम, विशेषण, क्रिया (Noun, Pronoun, Adjective, Verb).","मुहावरे और लोकोक्तियाँ (Idioms and Phrases).","विलोम और पर्यायवाची शब्द (Antonyms and Synonyms).","अपठित गद्यांश (Unseen Passage Comprehension).","रिक्त स्थानों की पूर्ति (Fill in the Blanks).","Current Affairs: National and International events, major appointments, awards, sports, and schemes.","Indian History & Culture: Ancient, Medieval, and Modern history, with special emphasis on the Indian freedom struggle and the role of Bihar.","Indian Polity: Constitution of India, Panchayati Raj, Public Policy, Rights Issues.","Indian Economy: Basics of Indian economy, Five Year Plans, and economic policies with a focus on Bihar''s economy.","Geography: Geography of India and Bihar, including physical, social, and economic geography.","General Science: Physics, Chemistry, and Biology based on everyday observation and experience.","Engineering Mechanics: Force systems, equilibrium, friction, centroids, moments of inertia, kinematics and kinetics of particles and rigid bodies.","Surveying and Measurement: Principles of surveying, leveling, contouring, theodolite traversing, basics of modern surveying techniques.","Mechanics of Solids: Simple stress and strain, elastic constants, shear force and bending moment diagrams, stresses in beams, torsion, columns and struts.","Engineering Materials and Construction: Properties of engineering materials, testing, concrete technology, construction practices.","Engineering Economy: Costing, depreciation, present worth, annual cost and break-even analysis.","Basics of Electrical Engineering: DC circuits, AC circuits fundamentals, transformers, basics of motors and generators.","Basics of Mechanical Engineering: Thermodynamics, fluid mechanics, heat transfer, IC engines."]},{"heading":"Optional Papers Syllabus (Discipline Specific)","body":["Structural Analysis: Determinate and indeterminate structures, ILD, Arches, Trusses, Moment distribution, Slope deflection methods.","Structural Design (Steel): Design of tension and compression members, beams, column bases, connections (bolted and welded), plate girders, and trusses.","Structural Design (RCC): Limit State and Working Stress methods of design, design of beams, slabs, columns, footings, retaining walls, and water tanks. Pre-stressed concrete principles.","Soil Mechanics and Foundation Engineering: Properties of soil, classification, permeability, consolidation, shear strength, earth pressure, stability of slopes, bearing capacity, shallow and deep foundations.","Hydrology and Water Resources: Hydrological cycle, precipitation, runoff, hydrographs, flood estimation and routing, irrigation engineering, dams, canals, groundwater hydrology.","Fluid Mechanics and Open Channel Flow: Fluid properties, fluid statics, kinematics, dynamics, flow through pipes, boundary layer, uniform and non-uniform flow in open channels, hydraulic jump.","Transportation Engineering: Geometric design of highways and railways, pavement design, traffic engineering, railway engineering components, airport planning basics.","Environmental Engineering: Water demand, quality, treatment, and distribution. Sewage collection, quality, treatment, and disposal. Air and noise pollution.","Thermodynamics: Laws of thermodynamics, properties of pure substances, thermodynamic cycles (Otto, Diesel, Rankine, Brayton), entropy, availability.","Fluid Mechanics: Fluid properties, fluid statics, kinematics, dynamics, Bernoulli''s equation, flow through pipes, boundary layer theory.","Heat Transfer: Conduction, convection, and radiation. Fins, heat exchangers, boiling and condensation.","I.C. Engines: SI and CI engines, performance, combustion, emissions and control.","Refrigeration and Air-Conditioning: Vapour compression cycle, refrigerants, psychrometry, cooling load calculations.","Theory of Machines: Kinematics of mechanisms, gears and gear trains, cams, governors, balancing, vibrations.","Machine Design: Design of joints, shafts, keys, couplings, bearings, gears, clutches, brakes, and springs against static and dynamic loading.","Strength of Materials: Stress-strain, SFD & BMD, bending and shear stresses, torsion, columns, springs.","Manufacturing Science: Casting, forming, welding, machining processes, machine tools, non-conventional machining, metrology.","Industrial Engineering: Production planning, inventory control, operations research (LP, transportation, assignment), PERT & CPM.","Electrical Circuits and Fields: Network analysis, Thevenin''s, Norton''s theorems, transient and steady-state response, two-port networks, three-phase circuits, electromagnetic theory, Maxwell''s equations.","Control Systems: Transfer functions, block diagrams, signal flow graphs, time and frequency response analysis, stability analysis (Routh-Hurwitz, Nyquist, Bode), PID controllers, state-space analysis.","Electrical Materials: Crystal structures, properties of conductors, semiconductors, insulators, magnetic and dielectric materials.","Measurements and Instrumentation: Bridges and potentiometers, measurement of voltage, current, power, energy, instrument transformers, transducers, oscilloscopes.","Electrical Machines: DC machines, transformers, three-phase induction motors, synchronous machines, special motors.","Power Systems: Power generation, transmission and distribution, fault analysis, stability analysis, load flow studies, power system protection, circuit breakers.","Power Electronics and Drives: Semiconductor devices (diodes, thyristors, MOSFETs, IGBTs), rectifiers, choppers, inverters, speed control of DC and AC motors.","Analog and Digital Electronics: Diode circuits, transistors, op-amps, logic gates, combinational and sequential circuits, A/D and D/A converters, microprocessors.","Frequently Asked Questions","Here are answers to some common questions about the BPSC Assistant Engineer Examination."]},{"heading":"Regarding Eligibility & Application","body":["Can final year engineering students apply for BPSC AE? Yes, generally. Final year students are often eligible to apply, but they must be able to produce their final degree/provisional certificate by a specified cut-off date, which is usually before the document verification stage. Always check the official notification for the exact condition.","Is a degree in Computer Science or IT eligible for this exam? No. The BPSC AE recruitment is typically conducted for core engineering branches only: Civil, Mechanical, and Electrical.","Is knowledge of Hindi compulsory? Yes. There is a compulsory qualifying paper in General Hindi. Although the marks are not added to the merit list, you must score a minimum of 30% to pass. Failure to do so will result in the disqualification of your candidature."]}]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,0) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.gov_exams (category,name,summary,content,links,display_order) values ('BPSC','BPSC CCE','BPSC Combined Competitive Examination','[{"heading":"Overview","body":["BPSC Combined Competitive Examination"]},{"heading":"About the Examination","body":["The Bihar Public Service Commission (BPSC) Combined Competitive Examination (CCE) is the state-level examination for recruitment to various civil services and posts within the Government of Bihar. It is a comprehensive, multi-stage process designed to select the state''s future administrative leaders. Regarded as one of the most prestigious state civil services exams in India, it serves as the gateway to a career in public service that directly impacts the governance and development of Bihar."]},{"heading":"The Philosophy Behind the Examination","body":["The BPSC CCE is engineered to assess more than just rote memorization. Its core philosophy is to identify individuals who possess a potent combination of intellectual depth, analytical acuity, ethical integrity, and a profound understanding of the state''s socio-economic landscape . The examination seeks to evaluate a candidate''s:","Critical Assimilation: The ability to understand complex information from diverse fields, filter out noise, and grasp the core issues relevant to the state and nation.","Balance of Judgment: The capacity to form reasoned opinions, consider multiple perspectives, and make sound, unbiased judgments.","Intellectual and Moral Integrity: A strong ethical compass and the courage to uphold constitutional and administrative values.","Leadership and Social Cohesion: The potential to lead and work collaboratively to solve pressing societal problems at the state level.","Awareness and Curiosity: A deep and abiding interest in local, state, national, and international affairs."]},{"heading":"The Services at Stake","body":["Successful candidates are allocated to various state-level services based on their rank and preferences. These services form the backbone of the state''s administrative machinery."]},{"heading":"Key Group ''A'' & ''B'' Services","body":["and several others, including posts like Municipal Executive Officer, Revenue Officer, Block Panchayat Raj Officer, etc."]},{"heading":"The Three-Tiered Selection Gauntlet","body":["The CCE is a multi-layered process where each stage serves as a progressively finer filter to identify the most suitable candidates. The entire cycle, from notification to the final result, spans over a year.","Stage 1: Preliminary Examination (The Filter): This is an objective-type (Multiple Choice Questions) screening test. Its primary role is to eliminate non-serious candidates and select a manageable number for the Mains exam. It tests the breadth of a candidate''s knowledge. Crucially, the marks from this stage are not counted for the final merit list. It is a pass/fail gateway to the next level.","Stage 2: Main Examination (The Crucible): Candidates who clear the Prelims undertake the Main Examination, a descriptive (written) exam that is the real test of intellectual depth and analytical skill. Spread over five papers (including qualifying ones), it evaluates a candidate''s ability to articulate nuanced arguments and structure comprehensive answers. The marks from the Main Examination are the most significant component of the final score.","Stage 3: Personality Test (The Final Appraisal): The final stage is an interview conducted by a board of distinguished experts at the BPSC headquarters in Patna. The board, having access to the candidate''s Detailed Application Form, assesses their personality, mental alertness, leadership qualities, and suitability for a high-stakes career in the state''s public administration.","The Final Verdict: Merit and Allocation","The final merit list is prepared based on the total marks obtained in the Main Examination (900 marks) and the Personality Test (120 marks) , for a grand total of 1020 marks. Based on their final rank, the preferences for services submitted by them, their category, and the vacancies available, candidates are allocated a service. This final allocation marks the successful culmination of one of the most demanding journeys in the Bihar state examination landscape."]},{"heading":"Typical Timeline (Indicative)","body":["The BPSC CCE schedule can vary, but it generally follows a year-long cycle. Understanding this timeline is crucial for strategic planning and sustained preparation. The following is a typical, indicative schedule based on recent trends.","Disclaimer: These timelines are based on past trends. Always refer to the official BPSC calendar and notifications for the specific year for exact dates.","Phase 1: Notification & Application (June - August)","June / July: The official notification for the Combined Competitive Examination is released by BPSC on its website (bpsc.bih.nic.in). This document contains all essential details: vacancy numbers, eligibility, syllabus, and exam rules.","July - August: The online application window opens and typically remains active for about a month. This process involves a one-time registration (OTR) and filling out the application form. Aspirants must be careful while filling in details and uploading documents.","Phase 2: Preliminary Examination (September - October)","September: Approximately 2-3 weeks before the exam date, BPSC releases the e-Admit Card. Candidates must download and print it.","Late September / Early October: The Preliminary Exam is conducted on a single day. It consists of one paper of General Studies.","Phase 3: Prelims Result & Mains Application (November - December)","November: The results of the Preliminary Examination are typically declared within 4-6 weeks of the exam date. BPSC releases a list of the roll numbers of qualified candidates.","November - December: Shortly after the Prelims result, the qualified candidates are required to fill out the Detailed Application Form (DAF) for the Mains exam. This form collects detailed information and is also where candidates indicate their optional subject.","Phase 4: Main Examination (January - February)","December / January: Admit cards for the Main Examination are released for candidates who have successfully submitted their DAF.","January / February: The Main Examination is a descriptive (written) test conducted over 3-4 days.","Phase 5: Mains Result & Interview Schedule (May - June)","May / June: The results of the Main Examination are usually announced about 3 to 4 months after the exam. A list of candidates who have qualified for the Personality Test (Interview) is published.","Phase 6: Personality Tests (Interviews) (July - August)","The Personality Tests (Interviews) typically commence a few weeks after the Mains results are declared.","Candidates are summoned to the BPSC office in Patna in batches. The interview schedule is spread over several weeks.","Phase 7: Final Result Declaration (August - September)","This is the culmination of the entire process. The final merit list, based on the combined scores of the Main Examination and the Personality Test, is declared, usually within a few weeks of the conclusion of the last interview."]},{"heading":"Eligibility Criteria","body":["Before embarking on the rigorous journey of BPSC preparation, every aspirant must meticulously verify their eligibility. The criteria set by the Bihar Public Service Commission are strict and non-negotiable. Eligibility is primarily determined by four key parameters: Nationality, Age Limit, Educational Qualification, and Number of Attempts.","Note: The details provided here are based on prevailing rules. These are subject to change, and the official BPSC notification for the current examination year is the ultimate authority.","1. Nationality","A candidate must be a citizen of India to be eligible for the BPSC CCE.","2. Age Limit","The age of a candidate is calculated as of a specific cut-off date, which is typically the 1st of August of the examination year. The age limits vary depending on the post and category.","Minimum Age: 20, 21, or 22 years , depending on the service/post. (e.g., 20 years for Dy. SP, 22 years for others like SDM).","Maximum Age: The upper age limit varies by category.","BPSC provides relaxations in the upper age limit for candidates from different categories:","Note: Candidates must refer to the official notification for post-specific age requirements and detailed relaxation norms.","3. Educational Qualification"]},{"heading":"The minimum educational requirement is straightforward","body":["A candidate must hold a Bachelor''s degree (Graduate) or an equivalent qualification from a recognized University.","Final-Year Students: Candidates who have appeared or intend to appear for their final degree examination and are awaiting results are also eligible to apply. However, they must have passed the exam by the time of filling the Mains application form.","4. Number of Attempts","The number of times a candidate can appear for the examination is limited. Appearing in the Preliminary Exam is counted as an attempt.","Note: The attempt limit policy has changed over time. As per the latest rules, there is no separate attempt limit for most categories; eligibility is determined by the age limit. Always verify with the latest notification."]},{"heading":"Stages, Exam Pattern & Marking Scheme","body":["The BPSC CCE is a meticulously designed three-stage process. Each stage has a distinct purpose and format. A thorough understanding of this pattern is the first step toward building a successful preparation strategy."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: Preliminary Examination (Objective Type)","body":["The primary purpose of the Prelims is to screen candidates for the Main Examination. The marks obtained in this stage are not counted for the final merit list.","Objective Format: The paper consists of Multiple Choice Questions (MCQs).","Negative Marking: There is a penalty for incorrect answers. For each wrong answer, one-third (1/3rd) of the marks assigned to that question will be deducted.","No CSAT Paper: Unlike UPSC, BPSC Prelims consists of only one General Studies paper.","Merit Calculation: The cut-off for qualifying for the Main Examination is determined solely based on the marks scored in this paper."]},{"heading":"Stage 2: Main Examination (Descriptive + Objective)","body":["This stage is the core of the selection process. The marks obtained here are the primary determinant of a candidate''s final rank. It consists of 5 papers.","Candidates must clear these papers to have their other Mains papers evaluated.","Failing to secure the qualifying marks in these papers will lead to disqualification.","The sum of marks from these three papers determines the written score for the final merit list."]}]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,1) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.gov_exams (category,name,summary,content,links,display_order) values ('SSC','IBPS PO','The Institute of Banking Personnel Selection (IBPS) Probationary Officer (PO) examination is a highly competitive, national-level recruitment process for the post of Probationary Officer/Management Trainee (Scale-I) in various Public Sector','[{"heading":"About the Examination","body":["The Institute of Banking Personnel Selection (IBPS) Probationary Officer (PO) examination is a highly competitive, national-level recruitment process for the post of Probationary Officer/Management Trainee (Scale-I) in various Public Sector Banks (PSBs) across India. It serves as a unified gateway for graduates aspiring to a prestigious management-level career in the banking sector, offering immense growth opportunities, job security, and a chance to contribute to the nation''s economy."]},{"heading":"The Philosophy Behind the Examination","body":["The IBPS PO exam is designed to select candidates who possess the right blend of aptitude, knowledge, and personality for a dynamic and demanding banking career. It goes beyond testing theoretical knowledge and focuses on assessing practical skills essential for a modern banker.","Speed and Accuracy: The time-bound nature of the online exams, with strict sectional timings, tests a candidate''s ability to solve complex problems quickly and accurately under immense pressure.","Analytical & Reasoning Skills: The exam evaluates logical thinking, problem-solving capabilities, and the ability to interpret complex data, which are crucial for making sound banking decisions related to credit, risk, and operations.","Language Proficiency: Strong command of the English language is assessed, as it is vital for customer interaction, internal communication, drafting official documents, and understanding policy circulars.","General & Banking Awareness: It tests a candidate''s awareness of the economy, the banking industry, financial regulations, and current affairs, ensuring they are well-informed professionals capable of understanding the broader economic context of their work.","Participating Public Sector Banks","Successful candidates are provisionally allotted to one of the participating PSBs based on their rank and preferences. The initial pay scale for a PO (Scale-I) is approximately ₹36,000 - ₹63,840, with the gross salary being significantly higher due to allowances.","(List is subject to change as per vacancies reported by banks for a given year)."]},{"heading":"The Three-Tiered Selection Gauntlet","body":["The IBPS PO selection is a rigorous, multi-stage process designed to meticulously filter candidates at each level.","Stage 1: Preliminary Examination (The Speed Test): An online objective test designed primarily to screen a large number of candidates. It tests fundamental skills in Reasoning, Quantitative Aptitude, and English within a highly constrained time limit. Marks from this stage are not considered for the final merit list; it is purely a qualifying hurdle to reach the main examination.","Stage 2: Main Examination (The Crucible of Knowledge): Candidates who clear the Prelims appear for the Main exam. This is a comprehensive online test comprising both objective and descriptive sections. It evaluates in-depth knowledge of reasoning, data interpretation, banking awareness, and English writing skills. The score from this stage is the most critical component for final selection.","Stage 3: Interview (The Final Appraisal): The final stage is a face-to-face interview conducted by panels of senior bankers and experts from participating banks. It assesses a candidate''s communication skills, confidence, banking knowledge, problem-solving attitude, and overall suitability for the PO role.","The Final Verdict: Merit and Allotment","The final merit list is created based on the combined scores of the Main Examination and the Interview . The marks are normalized to a scale of 100, with a weightage of 80% for the Main Examination and 20% for the Interview . Based on the final score and vacancies, candidates are provisionally allotted to one of the participating banks."]},{"heading":"Typical Timeline (Indicative)","body":["The IBPS PO recruitment cycle is an annual process that is typically completed within 9-10 months. The schedule is fairly consistent year after year, with the final result being famously declared on the 1st of April.","Disclaimer: These timelines are based on past trends. Always refer to the official IBPS notification for the specific year for exact dates.","Phase 1: Notification & Application (July - August)","Late July / Early August: The official detailed notification is released by IBPS on its website (ibps.in).","August: The online application window opens and remains active for about 20-22 days . Candidates must complete their registration and fee payment during this period.","Phase 2: Pre-Exam Training & Prelims Admit Card (September - October)","September: IBPS arranges Pre-Examination Training (PET) for candidates from SC/ST/Minority Communities at certain centres.","Late September / Early October: The call letters (Admit Cards) for the Preliminary Examination are released online for all registered candidates.","Phase 3: Preliminary Examination (October)","The online Preliminary Examination is conducted on multiple dates, usually over two weekends in October, to accommodate the large number of applicants.","Phase 4: Prelims Result & Mains Admit Card (November)","Early November: Results of the Preliminary Examination are declared, along with the scorecards and cut-offs.","Mid-November: Call letters for the Main Examination are released for candidates who have qualified in the Prelims.","Phase 5: Main Examination (November)","The online Main Examination (Objective + Descriptive) is conducted on a single day, typically in the last week of November.","Phase 6: Mains Result & Interview Call Letters (December - January)","December: The results of the Main Examination are announced, listing candidates shortlisted for the interview.","January: Shortlisted candidates can download their call letters for the Interview, which specify the date, time, and venue.","Phase 7: Interviews (January - February)","Interviews are conducted by the participating banks under the coordination of IBPS at select nodal centres across the country.","Phase 8: Final Result & Provisional Allotment (April)","1st April: IBPS declares the final combined result for the Main exam and Interview. A final merit list is published, and candidates are provisionally allotted to a bank based on their rank and preferences."]},{"heading":"Eligibility Criteria","body":["Candidates must fulfill the eligibility criteria prescribed by IBPS to apply for the PO examination. Eligibility is determined by Nationality, Age, and Educational Qualification.","Note: The official notification for the current examination year is the final authority on eligibility criteria.","1. Nationality / Citizenship","2. Age Limit","The age is calculated as of a cut-off date mentioned in the notification (usually the 1st of the month of notification, e.g., 01.08.2025).","Minimum Age: 20 years","Maximum Age: 30 years","This means a candidate must have been born not earlier than 02.08.1995 and not later than 01.08.2005 (both dates inclusive), for the 2025 exam as an example.","3. Educational Qualification","The minimum educational requirement is as follows (as on the last date of online registration):","A Degree (Graduation) in any discipline from a University recognised by the Govt. of India or any equivalent qualification recognized as such by the Central Government.","The candidate must possess a valid Mark-sheet / Degree Certificate that he/ she is a graduate on the day he / she registers and indicate the percentage of marks obtained in Graduation while registering online.","Final-Year Students: Candidates in their final year/semester are generally not eligible unless their result is declared and they have the degree certificate/final mark sheet on or before the cut-off date specified in the notification.","Computer Literacy: Working knowledge of computer systems is mandatory. Candidates should have a Certificate/Diploma/Degree in computer operations/language or should have studied Computer / Information Technology as one of the subjects in High School/College/Institute.","Language Proficiency: Verbal and written proficiency in the official language of the State/UT is desirable.","4. Number of Attempts","There is no limit on the number of attempts for the IBPS PO exam. Candidates can appear for the exam as many times as they wish, provided they meet the age and educational eligibility criteria."]},{"heading":"Stages, Exam Pattern & Marking Scheme","body":["The IBPS PO exam is conducted in three phases: Preliminary, Main, and Interview. A thorough understanding of the pattern, sectional timings, and marking scheme is vital for success."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: Preliminary Examination (Online Objective)","body":["This is a qualifying exam. Candidates must clear the sectional as well as the overall cut-off to be shortlisted for the Main exam.","Online Mode: The exam is conducted online on a computer.","Sectional Timing: There is a fixed time of 20 minutes for each section. Candidates cannot switch between sections before the time for the current section expires.","Negative Marking: For each incorrect answer, one-fourth (0.25) of the marks assigned to that question will be deducted as a penalty.","Qualifying Nature: Marks are not counted for the final merit list. The result is declared as ''Qualified'' or ''Not Qualified''."]},{"heading":"Stage 2: Main Examination (Online Objective + Descriptive)","body":["The marks obtained in the Main Examination are considered for shortlisting for the interview and for the final merit list.","This test is conducted immediately after the objective test on the same platform to assess writing skills.","Separate Timings: Both objective and descriptive tests have separate timings. All sections in the objective test also have individual sectional timings.","Negative Marking: Applicable for the objective test (0.25 marks deducted for each wrong answer).","Merit Calculation: Marks from both Objective and Descriptive tests are considered for shortlisting and final merit. The descriptive paper is only evaluated if the candidate clears the sectional cut-offs in the objective test."]},{"heading":"Stage 3: Interview","body":["Candidates who clear the Mains cut-off (approximately 3 times the number of vacancies) are called for a face-to-face interview.","Maximum Marks: 100 Marks","Minimum Qualifying Marks: Candidates must score a minimum of 40% (35% for SC/ST/OBC/PWBD) to pass the interview stage. Failing to do so will disqualify them from the final selection, regardless of their Main exam score.","Qualities Assessed: The interview assesses personality, communication skills, presence of mind, clarity of thought, leadership potential, and general and banking awareness.","Final Rank Calculation","The final merit list is prepared by giving a weightage of 80:20 to the Main Examination and the Interview, respectively.","Final Score = [ (Main Exam Score out of 225) * (80/225) ] + [ (Interview Score out of 100) * (20/100) ]","Application Workflow","The application process for IBPS PO is entirely online. Candidates must follow the steps carefully to avoid any errors."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: Pre-requisites for Applying Online","body":["Before starting the application, candidates must have the following documents ready as per the specified format:","A valid personal Email ID and Mobile Number, which must be kept active until the final declaration of results.","Scanned copy of a recent passport-style colour Photograph (4.5cm × 3.5cm, 20-50 kb).","Scanned copy of Signature (on white paper with Black Ink, 10-20 kb).","Scanned copy of Left Thumb Impression (on white paper with black or blue ink, 20-50 kb).","A Hand Written Declaration (on white paper with black ink, in English only, 50-100 kb), with the text specified in the notification.","Details/documents for online payment of the requisite application fee."]},{"heading":"Stage 2: Online Registration and Application","body":["Visit the official IBPS website: Go to www.ibps.in and click on the link for \"CRP PO/MT\".","New Registration: Click on \"CLICK HERE FOR NEW REGISTRATION\" and enter your basic information (Name, Mobile No., and Email ID). A provisional Registration Number and Password will be generated and sent via SMS and Email.","Fill the Form: Log in with the registration number and password to fill in personal, academic, and communication details. Validate your details at each step by clicking the ''Validate your details'' button and then ''Save & Next''.","Upload Documents: Upload the scanned copies of your Photograph, Signature, Left Thumb Impression, and Hand Written Declaration as per the specifications.","Bank Preferences: Candidates will be asked to indicate their preferences for participating banks in order of priority. This choice is crucial and cannot be changed later."]},{"heading":"Stage 3: Fee Payment","body":["Application Fee (Indicative): ₹ 175/- for SC/ST/PWBD candidates. ₹ 850/- for all others (General/OBC/EWS).","₹ 175/- for SC/ST/PWBD candidates.","₹ 850/- for all others (General/OBC/EWS).","Payment Mode: The fee can only be paid online using Debit Cards (RuPay/Visa/MasterCard/Maestro), Credit Cards, Internet Banking, IMPS, or Mobile Wallets."]}]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,2) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.gov_exams (category,name,summary,content,links,display_order) values ('SSC','IBPS RRB','The Institute of Banking Personnel Selection (IBPS) Common Recruitment Process (CRP) for Regional Rural Banks (RRBs) is a national-level banking exam conducted annually to recruit personnel for various posts in RRBs across India. This exami','[{"heading":"About the Examination","body":["The Institute of Banking Personnel Selection (IBPS) Common Recruitment Process (CRP) for Regional Rural Banks (RRBs) is a national-level banking exam conducted annually to recruit personnel for various posts in RRBs across India. This examination is a gateway for aspirants seeking a career in rural banking, which plays a crucial role in the economic development of rural areas."]},{"heading":"The Philosophy Behind the Examination","body":["The IBPS RRB exam aims to select candidates who are not only proficient in banking knowledge but also have an aptitude for working in and understanding the rural economy. The core philosophy is to identify individuals who demonstrate:","Quantitative & Reasoning Skills: The ability to solve problems, analyze data, and think logically, which is essential for day-to-day banking operations.","Language Proficiency: Strong command over English and/or Hindi, along with knowledge of the local language of the state, is crucial for customer interaction.","General & Banking Awareness: A good understanding of the Indian economy, financial systems, current affairs, and computer literacy.","Commitment to Rural Service: A mindset geared towards contributing to financial inclusion and the development of India''s rural sector."]},{"heading":"The Posts and Services at Stake","body":["The IBPS RRB exam recruits for Group ''A'' Officers and Group ''B'' Office Assistants."]},{"heading":"The Multi-Stage Selection Process","body":["The selection process varies depending on the post.","For Office Assistant (Multipurpose): This is a two-stage process. Preliminary Exam: An online screening test of Reasoning and Numerical Ability. It is qualifying in nature. Mains Exam: Candidates who clear the prelims appear for the mains exam. The final selection is based solely on the marks obtained in the Mains Exam . There is no interview.","Preliminary Exam: An online screening test of Reasoning and Numerical Ability. It is qualifying in nature.","Mains Exam: Candidates who clear the prelims appear for the mains exam. The final selection is based solely on the marks obtained in the Mains Exam . There is no interview.","For Officer Scale-I: This is a three-stage process. Preliminary Exam: An online screening test of Reasoning and Quantitative Aptitude, which is qualifying. Mains Exam: Shortlisted candidates appear for the mains exam. Marks from this stage are considered for final selection. Interview: Candidates who clear the mains exam are called for an interview. The final merit list is prepared based on the combined score of the Mains Exam and Interview.","Preliminary Exam: An online screening test of Reasoning and Quantitative Aptitude, which is qualifying.","Mains Exam: Shortlisted candidates appear for the mains exam. Marks from this stage are considered for final selection.","Interview: Candidates who clear the mains exam are called for an interview. The final merit list is prepared based on the combined score of the Mains Exam and Interview.","For Officer Scale-II & III: This is a two-stage process. Single Level Examination: A single online exam is conducted. There is no preliminary exam for these posts. Interview: Candidates qualifying in the single-level exam are shortlisted for an interview. The final merit list is based on the combined score of the exam and interview.","Single Level Examination: A single online exam is conducted. There is no preliminary exam for these posts.","Interview: Candidates qualifying in the single-level exam are shortlisted for an interview. The final merit list is based on the combined score of the exam and interview."]},{"heading":"Typical Timeline (Indicative)","body":["The IBPS RRB examination cycle typically spans about 7-8 months from notification to the final result. The following is a general, indicative schedule.","Disclaimer: These timelines are based on past trends. Always refer to the official IBPS calendar and notification for the specific year for exact dates.","Phase 1: Notification & Application (June - July)","June: The official notification for RRB CRP is released by IBPS on its website (ibps.in). It details vacancies, eligibility, exam pattern, and application guidelines.","June - July: The online application and fee payment window typically remains open for about three weeks .","Phase 2: Preliminary Examination (August)","July / August: Approximately 10-15 days before the exam , IBPS releases the call letters (admit cards) for the Preliminary Examination for Officer Scale-I and Office Assistant posts.","August: The Preliminary online examinations are conducted over multiple weekends.","Phase 3: Mains & Single Level Examination (September - October)","September: Results for the Preliminary exams are announced.","September: Call letters for the Mains Exam (for Officer Scale I & Office Assistant) and Single Level Exam (for Officer Scale II & III) are released.","September / October: The Mains and Single Level online examinations are conducted, usually on separate dates."]},{"heading":"Phase 4: Interview Process (November - December)","body":["October / November: Results for the Mains/Single Level exams are declared, and a list of candidates shortlisted for interviews (for all Officer posts) is released.","November / December: Interviews for Officer Scale-I, II, and III are conducted by participating RRBs, coordinated by NABARD and IBPS.","Phase 5: Provisional Allotment (January of next year)","1st January: IBPS releases the final result and provisional allotment list for all posts. This list specifies the RRB allotted to successful candidates based on merit, preference, and vacancies."]},{"heading":"Eligibility Criteria","body":["Candidates must ensure they fulfill the IBPS RRB eligibility criteria, which are mandatory. Eligibility is determined by Nationality, Age Limit, and Educational Qualification.","Note: The official IBPS notification for the current examination year is the ultimate authority on eligibility criteria.","1. Nationality","A candidate must be a citizen of India or a subject of Nepal/Bhutan, or a Tibetan Refugee who came to India before 1st January 1962, or a person of Indian origin who has migrated from specific countries with the intention of permanently settling in India.","2. Age Limit","The age is calculated as of a specific cut-off date, typically the 1st of June of the examination year. The age limits are:","For Officer Scale-III (Senior Manager): Above 21 years - Below 40 years.","For Officer Scale-II (Manager): Above 21 years - Below 32 years.","For Officer Scale-I (Assistant Manager): Above 18 years - Below 30 years.","For Office Assistant (Multipurpose): Between 18 years and 28 years.","3. Educational Qualification & Experience","Office Assistant (Multipurpose): Bachelor’s degree in any discipline from a recognized University. Proficiency in the local language as prescribed by the participating RRB is required.","Officer Scale-I (Assistant Manager): Bachelor’s degree in any discipline. Preference given to degrees in Agriculture, Horticulture, Forestry, Animal Husbandry, etc. Proficiency in the local language and working knowledge of computers is required.","Officer Scale-II (General Banking Officer): Bachelor’s degree in any discipline with a minimum of 50% marks in aggregate. Preference to specific degrees. Experience: Two years as an officer in a Bank or Financial Institution.","Officer Scale-II (Specialist Officers): IT Officer: Bachelor’s degree in Electronics/Communication/Computer Science/IT with 50% marks. Experience: One year in the relevant field. Chartered Accountant: Certified Associate (CA) from the Institute of Chartered Accountants of India. Experience: One year as a CA. Law Officer: Degree in Law with 50% marks. Experience: Two years as an advocate or should have worked as a Law Officer in Banks for a period of not less than two years.","IT Officer: Bachelor’s degree in Electronics/Communication/Computer Science/IT with 50% marks. Experience: One year in the relevant field.","Chartered Accountant: Certified Associate (CA) from the Institute of Chartered Accountants of India. Experience: One year as a CA.","Law Officer: Degree in Law with 50% marks. Experience: Two years as an advocate or should have worked as a Law Officer in Banks for a period of not less than two years.","Officer Scale-III (Senior Manager): Bachelor’s degree in any discipline with a minimum of 50% marks in aggregate. Preference to specific degrees/diplomas. Experience: Minimum 5 years’ experience as an Officer in a Bank or Financial Institution.","Note: A Language Proficiency Test (LPT) will be conducted for candidates who do not have the local language as a subject in their Class 8th or higher studies."]},{"heading":"Stages, Exam Pattern & Marking Scheme","body":["The IBPS RRB exam pattern varies for different posts. A key feature is the choice between English and Hindi in the Mains exam."]},{"heading":"Common Features","body":["Mode: All exams are conducted online (Computer-Based Test).","Question Type: All questions are Objective Type (MCQs).","Negative Marking: For every wrong answer, one-fourth (0.25) of the marks assigned to that question will be deducted.","Office Assistant (Multipurpose) & Officer Scale-I","Officer Scale-II & III (Single Level Examination)"]},{"heading":"Final Selection","body":["Office Assistant: Final selection is based on marks obtained in the Mains Examination.","Officer Scale-I, II, III: Final selection is based on the combined performance in the Mains/Single Level Examination and the Interview. The weightage ratio is typically 80:20 for the written test and interview, respectively.","Application Workflow","The application process for IBPS RRB is conducted entirely online through the official IBPS website (ibps.in) . Candidates must follow the steps carefully."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: Registration and Basic Information","body":["New Registration: Click on the link for \"CRP for RRBs\" on the IBPS homepage and select \"Click here for New Registration\".","Enter Basic Info: Provide your name, mobile number, and email address. A provisional Registration Number and Password will be generated and sent via SMS and email.","Save and Next: Use these credentials to log in and proceed with the application."]},{"heading":"Stage 2: Uploading Scanned Documents","body":["This is a critical step. Candidates must scan and upload their photograph, signature, left thumb impression, and a handwritten declaration as per the specifications given in the notification.","Photograph: Recent colour passport-style photograph (4.5cm × 3.5cm). Size: 20kb–50 kb.","Signature: On white paper with black ink. Size: 10kb–20 kb.","Left Thumb Impression: On white paper with black or blue ink. Size: 20kb–50 kb.","Handwritten Declaration: Written by the candidate in English on white paper with black ink. The text for the declaration is provided in the official notification. Size: 50kb–100 kb."]}]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,3) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.gov_exams (category,name,summary,content,links,display_order) values ('SSC','NABARD Grade A','National Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development - Assistant Manager Exam','[{"heading":"Overview","body":["National Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development - Assistant Manager Exam"]},{"heading":"About the Examination","body":["The National Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development (NABARD) Grade A Examination is a premier national-level exam for recruitment to the post of Assistant Manager. NABARD, as India''s apex development bank, is instrumental in shaping policy and providing credit for agriculture and rural development. For B.Tech graduates, this exam is a gateway to a dynamic techno-managerial career, offering a unique platform to apply analytical skills for grassroots-level impact and nation-building."]},{"heading":"The Philosophy Behind the Examination","body":["The NABARD Grade A exam is designed to select officers who can merge strong analytical abilities with a comprehensive understanding of India''s rural economy. The core philosophy is to identify individuals who demonstrate:","Quantitative & Analytical Skills: The ability to interpret complex data, assess projects financially, and solve problems logically—a core strength of engineers.","Technological Acumen: An understanding of how technology (FinTech, Agri-Tech) can be leveraged to drive financial inclusion and agricultural productivity.","Socio-Economic Awareness: A deep understanding of rural India''s challenges and opportunities, including infrastructure, climate change, and sustainable development.","Managerial Potential: The capacity to manage projects, lead diverse teams, and coordinate with stakeholders like government agencies, banks, and NGOs."]},{"heading":"The Posts and Services at Stake","body":["While NABARD announces specialist vacancies, the primary opportunity for graduates from any engineering discipline (CSE, Civil, Mechanical, etc.) is the Grade A (Assistant Manager) in Rural Development Banking Service (RDBS) - Generalist post.","The role of a Generalist Officer is multi-faceted and offers immense exposure:"]},{"heading":"The Three-Phased Selection Process","body":["The selection is a rigorous process where each stage serves a specific purpose in filtering and selecting the most competent candidates.","Stage 1: Phase I Examination (The Qualifying Filter): This is an objective-type, online screening test. Its primary role is to shortlist a reasonable number of candidates for the next stage. It tests a broad range of aptitudes, and only the marks in the merit sections (GA, ESI, ARD) are used for shortlisting.","Stage 2: Phase II Examination (The Merit Decider): This stage is the ultimate test of a candidate''s in-depth knowledge and writing skills. It consists of two papers, and performance in this phase is the most critical component of the final score.","Stage 3: Interview (The Personality Assessment): The final stage is a face-to-face interview to assess a candidate''s personality, communication skills, and suitability for a managerial role in the development sector.","The Final Verdict: Merit and Allocation","The final merit list is prepared based on the consolidated marks obtained in the Phase II (Main Examination) and the Interview . Based on their final rank, candidates are appointed as Assistant Managers in NABARD and are posted in its various offices across the country."]},{"heading":"Typical Timeline (Indicative)","body":["The NABARD Grade A examination cycle is relatively fast, typically spanning 5-6 months from notification to final result.","Disclaimer: These timelines are based on past trends. Always refer to the official NABARD notification for the specific year for exact dates.","Phase 1: Notification & Application (July - August)","July: The official notification is released on the NABARD website, containing all vital details like vacancies, eligibility, and syllabus.","July - August: The online application window opens and remains active for about three weeks .","Phase 2: Phase-I Examination (September)","Late August / Early September: Admit Cards (Call Letters) for the Phase-I Exam are made available for download around 10-15 days prior to the exam date.","Mid-September: The Phase-I online examination is typically conducted on a single day across various centers.","Phase 3: Phase-I Result & Phase-II Admit Card (October)","Early October: The results of the Phase-I Exam are declared, usually within 15-20 days. A list of shortlisted candidates for Phase-II is published.","Mid-October: Admit cards for the Phase-II Examination are released for all shortlisted candidates.","Phase 4: Phase-II Examination (November)","Early November: The Phase-II Examination (Mains) is conducted. This includes both objective and descriptive online tests.","Phase 5: Final Result and Joining (December - February)","December: The results of Phase-II are declared, and a list of candidates shortlisted for the interview is released.","December / January: Interviews are conducted by NABARD at its head office or regional offices.","January / February: The final merit list is published, and offer letters are sent to the selected candidates."]},{"heading":"Eligibility Criteria","body":["Aspirants must carefully verify their eligibility. The criteria set by NABARD are mandatory and checked during document verification.","Note: The details provided here are based on prevailing rules. The official NABARD notification for the current examination year is the ultimate authority.","1. Nationality","A candidate must be a citizen of India.","2. Age Limit","The age limit for the Generalist post is typically between 21 and 30 years . The age is calculated as of a specific cut-off date, usually the 1st of the notification month .","Example: For a notification on 01-07-2025, a candidate must have been born not earlier than 02-07-1995 and not later than 01-07-2004 (both days inclusive).","3. Educational Qualification (For Generalist Post)","This is the key criterion for B.Tech students.","Essential Qualification: A Bachelor’s Degree in any subject from a recognized University with a minimum of 60% marks in aggregate (or equivalent CGPA). For SC/ST/PWBD applicants, the requirement is 55% marks .","Direct Eligibility for Engineers: Your B.E./B.Tech degree in ANY branch (e.g., Computer Science, Mechanical, Civil, Electrical, Electronics) makes you fully eligible for the Generalist post, provided you meet the minimum percentage criteria.","Final-Year Students: Candidates who are in the final year of their B.Tech can also apply, but they must possess the final degree and marksheet on or before the cut-off date specified in the official notice.","4. Number of Attempts","There is no limit on the number of attempts for the NABARD Grade A examination. A candidate may appear for the examination as many times as they wish, provided they satisfy all other eligibility conditions, including the age limit."]},{"heading":"Stages, Exam Pattern & Marking Scheme","body":["Understanding the unique pattern of the NABARD exam is crucial for creating an effective preparation strategy.","Phase-I Examination (Screening Test)","An online objective test with a total of 200 questions, 200 marks, and a composite time of 120 minutes . It''s divided into two parts.","Question Type: Objective Type, Multiple Choice Questions (MCQs).","Negative Marking: There is a penalty of 0.25 marks for each incorrect answer.","Cut-offs: Candidates must clear sectional cut-offs in the Qualifying section and an overall cut-off in the Merit section to be shortlisted for Phase-II."]},{"heading":"Phase-II Examination (Merit Deciding Stage)","body":["This is the main examination, consisting of two papers conducted online.","Descriptive Test: The descriptive answers for both Paper-I and Paper-II must be typed using the keyboard.","Negative Marking: Applicable only to the objective section of Paper-II, with a penalty of 0.25 marks for a wrong answer.","Final Rank Calculation","The final merit is prepared by adding the marks from Phase-II and the Interview.","Final Score = Marks in Phase-II (Paper I + Paper II) out of 200 + Marks in Interview out of 50. Total = 250 Marks.","Application Workflow","The application process for NABARD Grade A is entirely online. It is crucial to follow the instructions carefully to avoid any issues."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: New Registration","body":["This is the first step for all new applicants.","Visit the Portal: Go to the official NABARD website and navigate to the \"Careers\" section.","Register: Click on the application link and select \"Click here for New Registration\". Enter your name, mobile number, and email ID.","Credentials: A provisional Registration Number and Password will be generated and sent to your registered mobile number and email ID. Keep these safe."]},{"heading":"Stage 2: Filling the Application Form and Document Upload","body":["After registration, log in and complete the detailed application form.","Fill Details: Enter your personal, academic, and contact details. Use the \"Save and Next\" button to proceed.","Document Upload: You will need to upload scanned copies of four documents as per the specified format: Photograph: Recent colour passport-size photo (20KB - 50KB). Signature: On white paper with black ink (10KB - 20KB). Left Thumb Impression: On white paper with black/blue ink (20KB - 50KB). Handwritten Declaration: The text for the declaration will be provided in the notification. It must be written by you in English on white paper with black ink (50KB - 100KB).","Photograph: Recent colour passport-size photo (20KB - 50KB).","Signature: On white paper with black ink (10KB - 20KB).","Left Thumb Impression: On white paper with black/blue ink (20KB - 50KB).","Handwritten Declaration: The text for the declaration will be provided in the notification. It must be written by you in English on white paper with black ink (50KB - 100KB)."]},{"heading":"Stage 3: Fee Payment and Final Submission","body":["Fee Payment: Amount: A non-refundable fee of approximately ₹800 for General/OBC/EWS candidates. Exemptions: SC/ST/PwBD candidates are usually exempted from the fee but must pay intimation charges of around ₹150 . Payment Modes: The fee must be paid online via Debit Card, Credit Card, or Internet Banking.","Amount: A non-refundable fee of approximately ₹800 for General/OBC/EWS candidates.","Exemptions: SC/ST/PwBD candidates are usually exempted from the fee but must pay intimation charges of around ₹150 .","Payment Modes: The fee must be paid online via Debit Card, Credit Card, or Internet Banking.","Final Submit: After successful payment, verify all details in the application preview and click on \"Final Submit\".","Print Application: Download and print the final application form and the e-receipt for your records.","Common Mistakes to Avoid","Incorrect Declaration: Writing the handwritten declaration in capital letters or having someone else write it will lead to rejection.","Photo/Signature Mismatch: Ensure the uploaded signature matches the one you will use during the exam. The photo should be recent and clear.","Last-Minute Rush: Avoid applying on the last day to prevent server issues or payment failures."]}]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,4) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.gov_exams (category,name,summary,content,links,display_order) values ('SSC','RBI Grade B','Reserve Bank of India - Grade B Officer (Direct Recruit - General)','[{"heading":"Overview","body":["Reserve Bank of India - Grade B Officer (Direct Recruit - General)"]},{"heading":"About the Examination","body":["The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) Grade B (DR - General) Examination is one of the most prestigious and competitive exams in the country for entry into India''s central banking institution. It recruits officers who will be at the forefront of managing the country''s monetary policy, financial stability, and currency management."]},{"heading":"The Philosophy: Why Engineers Excel","body":["While seemingly a finance and economics-focused role, the RBI Grade B exam is designed to select candidates with exceptional analytical, quantitative, and problem-solving skills—traits deeply ingrained in an engineering curriculum. The core philosophy is to identify individuals who demonstrate:","Analytical Prowess: The ability to dissect complex economic and financial data, identify patterns, and make informed judgments.","Quantitative Aptitude: Strong numerical and logical reasoning skills are crucial for policy analysis and financial supervision.","Problem-Solving Mindset: The capacity to develop structured solutions for multifaceted macroeconomic challenges.","Learning Agility: The ability to quickly grasp concepts in economics, finance, and management, even without a formal background.","The Role and Career Trajectory","Selected candidates join as Grade B Officers (Managers) and are posted in various departments of the RBI. The career progression is among the best in the public sector, offering immense learning opportunities and the chance to contribute to national economic policy."]},{"heading":"The Three-Phase Selection Gauntlet","body":["The RBI Grade B is a rigorous three-stage process designed to test a candidate''s aptitude, knowledge, and personality.","Phase-I: The Aptitude Filter: An objective-type, online screening test. It''s a high-speed, high-stakes exam designed to test your core aptitude in Reasoning, Quantitative Aptitude, English, and General Awareness. This stage is purely qualifying ; its marks are not counted for the final merit list.","Phase-II: The Knowledge Test: This is the main examination and the primary determinant of your selection. It consists of three papers that test your understanding of Economic & Social Issues, Finance & Management, and your English writing skills. It uses a mix of objective and descriptive (typed on computer) formats.","Phase-III: The Personality Assessment (Interview): Candidates who clear Phase-II are called for a personal interview. This stage assesses your personality, communication skills, clarity of thought, and suitability for a career in the RBI."]},{"heading":"The Final Verdict: Merit and Selection","body":["The final merit list is prepared by combining the marks obtained in the Phase-II Examination and the Interview . Based on this final score and available vacancies, candidates are offered an appointment as an RBI Grade B Officer."]},{"heading":"Typical Timeline (Indicative)","body":["The RBI Grade B examination cycle is relatively short and intense, typically spanning 4-5 months from notification to the final result.","Disclaimer: These timelines are based on past trends. Always refer to the official RBI notification for the specific year for exact dates.","Phase 1: Notification & Application (e.g., May)","May: The official notification is released on the RBI website (opportunities.rbi.org.in).","May - June: The online application window remains open for approximately 3-4 weeks .","Phase 2: Phase-I Examination (e.g., July)","Late June / Early July: Admit cards for the Phase-I Examination are released.","July: The Phase-I online examination is conducted on a single day across the country.","Mid-July: Results of Phase-I are typically declared within a week.","Phase 3: Phase-II Examination (e.g., July/August)","July: Admit cards for the Phase-II Examination are released shortly after the Phase-I result.","Late July / Early August: The Phase-II online examination is conducted, usually about 15-20 days after the Phase-I exam.","Phase 4: Interviews & Final Result (e.g., September - November)","September: Results of the Phase-II Examination are declared, and a list of shortlisted candidates for the interview is published.","October: Interviews are conducted for the shortlisted candidates at RBI centers.","November / December: The final result, based on combined Phase-II and Interview scores, is declared."]},{"heading":"Eligibility Criteria","body":["Candidates must strictly adhere to the eligibility criteria set by the RBI. The official notification is the final authority on these conditions.","1. Nationality","A candidate must be a citizen of India.","2. Age Limit (as on the date specified in the notification)"]},{"heading":"The standard age limit for the RBI Grade B exam is","body":["Minimum Age: 21 years","Maximum Age: 30 years","A candidate must have been born not earlier than the date specified and not later than the date specified in the notification.","3. Educational Qualification","This is a key area for engineering students. The eligibility criteria are inclusive of engineering graduates.","A minimum of 60% marks (50% in case of SC/ST/PwBD) or an equivalent grade in Bachelor’s degree as well as in 12th (or Diploma or equivalent) and 10th Standard examinations.","For Engineering Students: A B.E./B.Tech degree from a recognized university is fully eligible, provided the percentage criteria are met.","Final-Year Students: Candidates in their final year of graduation are not eligible to apply unless their results are declared on or before the cut-off date specified in the notification.","4. Number of Attempts","There is a limit on the number of attempts for the Phase-I examination.","General Category / EWS: 6 attempts .","SC/ST/OBC/PwBD: No restriction on the number of attempts.","Appearing in Phase-I of the examination is counted as an attempt."]},{"heading":"Stages, Exam Pattern & Marking Scheme","body":["The examination structure is designed to test a wide range of skills, from raw aptitude to in-depth knowledge and communication.","Phase-I: Preliminary Examination (Objective & Qualifying)","An online test focused on speed and accuracy. You must clear both the overall and sectional cut-offs to qualify for Phase-II.","Sectional Timings: There are separate time limits for each section, which will be specified in the information handout.","Negative Marking: 0.25 marks will be deducted for each incorrect answer.","Qualifying Nature: Marks obtained in Phase-I are not considered for the final merit list."]},{"heading":"Phase-II: Main Examination (Merit Deciding Stage)","body":["This stage tests your descriptive and analytical skills and forms the basis of the final selection.","Phase-III: Interview","Candidates shortlisted after Phase-II will be called for a personal interview.","Marks: The interview carries 75 marks .","Language: Candidates can opt for the interview in either Hindi or English.","Final Rank Calculation","The final merit list is prepared based on the aggregate marks of the Phase-II Examination and the Interview .","Final Score = Marks in Paper-I (ESI) + Marks in Paper-II (English) + Marks in Paper-III (F&M) + Interview Marks.","Total Marks for Merit = 300 (Phase-II) + 75 (Interview) = 375 Marks.","Application Workflow","The application process for RBI Grade B is entirely online through the official RBI portal."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: Registration & Form Filling","body":["Visit Official Website: Go to the ''Opportunities@RBI'' section on the RBI website.","New Registration: Click on ''New Registration'' and enter your basic details like name, contact number, and email ID. A provisional registration number and password will be generated.","Fill Application Form: Log in and fill in your personal, academic, and other details carefully."]},{"heading":"Stage 2: Document Upload","body":["You will need to upload scanned copies of the following documents as per the specifications mentioned in the notification:","Photograph: Recent passport-size colour photograph (4.5cm × 3.5cm).","Signature: On white paper with Black Ink.","Left Thumb Impression: On white paper with black or blue ink.","Handwritten Declaration: A declaration written by the candidate in English on white paper with black ink. The text is provided in the official notification.","Standard Declaration Text: \"I, _______ (Name of the candidate), hereby declare that all the information submitted by me in the application form is correct, true and valid. I will present the supporting documents as and when required.\""]}]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,5) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.gov_exams (category,name,summary,content,links,display_order) values ('SSC','SBI PO','State Bank of India - Probationary Officer Examination','[{"heading":"Overview","body":["State Bank of India - Probationary Officer Examination"]},{"heading":"About the Examination","body":["The State Bank of India (SBI) Probationary Officer (PO) Examination is one of the most prestigious and competitive banking exams in India. Conducted annually by the SBI, it recruits candidates for the junior management level position of Probationary Officer (Scale-I). An SBI PO career is highly sought-after for its excellent remuneration, job security, rapid growth prospects, and the opportunity to work in India''s largest public sector bank."]},{"heading":"The Philosophy Behind the Examination","body":["The SBI PO exam is designed to select dynamic and capable individuals who can be nurtured into future leaders of the bank. The selection process aims to identify candidates with:","Analytical & Decision-Making Skills: The ability to analyze complex data, interpret financial information, and make sound judgments under pressure.","Problem-Solving Aptitude: Strong logical reasoning and quantitative skills to tackle banking-related challenges.","Communication & Interpersonal Skills: The ability to communicate effectively, manage customer relationships, and work in a team.","General & Economic Awareness: A comprehensive understanding of the banking industry, national economy, and current affairs.","The Role of a Probationary Officer"]},{"heading":"The Three-Phase Selection Process","body":["The SBI PO selection is a rigorous three-stage process designed to thoroughly evaluate a candidate''s suitability.","Phase 1: Preliminary Examination (The Screening Test): This is an online, objective-type screening test. Its purpose is purely to shortlist a reasonable number of candidates for the Main examination. The marks obtained in the Prelims are not counted for the final merit list .","Phase 2: Main Examination (The Merit Decider): This is the most crucial stage. It consists of both an objective test and a descriptive test. The scores from the Main examination are used for shortlisting candidates for the final phase and are also a major component of the final merit list.","Phase 3: Psychometric Test, Group Exercise & Interview (The Personality Assessment): Candidates who clear the Mains undergo a Psychometric Test (to assess personality), followed by a Group Exercise and a personal Interview. This phase evaluates a candidate''s communication skills, leadership qualities, and overall personality."]},{"heading":"The Final Verdict: Merit and Selection","body":["The final merit list is prepared by aggregating the marks obtained in the Main Examination (Phase 2) and the Group Exercise & Interview (Phase 3) . The scores are normalized to 100. Candidates are selected from the top of this merit list based on their category and the number of vacancies."]},{"heading":"Typical Timeline (Indicative)","body":["The SBI PO recruitment cycle typically completes within 6-7 months from notification to the final result. The following is a general, indicative schedule.","Disclaimer: These timelines are based on past trends. Always refer to the official SBI notification for the specific year for exact dates.","Phase 1: Notification & Application (September - October)","September: The official notification for the PO Examination is released by SBI on its careers website (sbi.co.in/web/careers).","September - October: The online application window opens and remains active for about three weeks . Candidates are required to register, fill the form, and pay the fee.","Phase 2: Preliminary Examination (November)","October / November: Approximately 10-15 days before the exam , SBI releases the call letter (admit card) for the Preliminary Examination.","November: The Preliminary Examination is conducted online in multiple shifts over several days.","November / December: The result of the Prelims is declared, usually within 2-3 weeks.","Phase 3: Main Examination (December)","December: Call letters for the Main Examination are released for all candidates shortlisted from the Prelims.","December: The Main Examination (Objective + Descriptive) is conducted online on a single day.","January: The result of the Main Examination is declared.","Phase 4: Psychometric Test, Group Exercise & Interview (January - February)","January / February: Call letters for Phase 3 are issued to candidates who clear the Mains cut-off.","February: The Psychometric Test, Group Exercises, and Interviews are conducted at various centers across the country.","Phase 5: Final Result (February - March)","February / March: The final result, containing the list of selected candidates, is declared by SBI. The joining process begins shortly after."]},{"heading":"Eligibility Criteria","body":["Candidates must ensure they fulfill all the eligibility criteria stipulated by SBI. Eligibility is determined by Nationality, Age Limit, Educational Qualification, and the Number of Attempts.","Note: The details provided are general. Always verify from the official SBI PO notification for the current year, as it is the final authority.","1. Nationality","A candidate must be a citizen of India.","2. Age Limit","The age of the candidate is calculated as of a specific cut-off date, typically the 1st of April of the recruitment year.","Age Limit: Not below 21 years and not above 30 years .","For example, for the 2025 exam, a candidate must have been born not earlier than 02.04.1995 and not later than 01.04.2004 (both days inclusive).","Relaxations in the upper age limit are provided for reserved categories:","3. Educational Qualification","The minimum educational requirement is a graduation degree.","Essential Qualification: A Graduation degree in any discipline from a recognised University or any equivalent qualification recognised as such by the Central Government.","Final-Year Students: Candidates who are in the Final Year/Semester of their Graduation may also apply provisionally, subject to the condition that, if called for an interview, they will have to produce proof of having passed the graduation examination on or before the specified cut-off date.","4. Number of Attempts","SBI places a limit on the number of times a candidate can appear for the PO examination. Appearing in the Preliminary Exam is not counted as an attempt, but appearing in the Main Exam is counted ."]},{"heading":"Stages, Exam Pattern & Marking Scheme","body":["The SBI PO examination consists of three phases. A thorough understanding of the pattern is crucial for success.","Phase-I: Preliminary Examination (Qualifying)","The Prelims is an online objective test to screen candidates for the Main exam. Candidates must clear both the overall and sectional cut-offs.","Sectional Timings: Each section is timed separately for 20 minutes . Candidates cannot switch between sections.","Negative Marking: There is a penalty of 0.25 marks (1/4th of the mark assigned to the question) for each incorrect answer.","Qualifying Nature: Marks are not considered for the final merit list.","Phase-II: Main Examination (Objective + Descriptive)","This is the core stage for merit calculation. It consists of an objective test and a descriptive test, both conducted online.","This test is conducted online immediately after the completion of the objective test. Candidates will have to type their answers on the computer.","Subjects: English Language (Letter Writing & Essay).","Number of Questions: 2","Maximum Marks: 50","Duration: 30 Minutes","Sectional Timings & Marks: Both timings and marks vary for each section in the objective test.","Negative Marking: A penalty of 0.25 marks applies to wrong answers in the objective test.","Phase-III: Psychometric Test, Group Exercise & Interview","Candidates numbering up to 3 times the category-wise vacancies are shortlisted for this phase.","Psychometric Test: Conducted to assess the personality profile of the candidates. There are no marks, but the report may be used by the interview panel.","Group Exercise (GE): 20 Marks","Interview: 30 Marks","Total Marks for Phase III: 50 Marks","Final Rank Calculation","The marks obtained in the Preliminary Exam (Phase-I) will not be added for preparing the final merit list for selection. Only the marks obtained in Main Examination (Phase-II) and Phase-III will be considered.","Marks from Main Exam (Objective + Descriptive) are converted out of 75 .","Marks from Group Exercise & Interview are converted out of 25 .","The final merit list is prepared based on the total score out of 100 .","Application Workflow","The application process for SBI PO is completely online via the official SBI careers website (sbi.co.in/web/careers) ."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: Registration","body":["Candidates need to register themselves on the portal by providing their basic information like name, mobile number, and email ID. A provisional registration number and password will be generated."]},{"heading":"Stage 2: Filling the Application Form & Uploading Documents","body":["Log in using the registration number and password to complete the application form.","Personal & Academic Details: Fill in all required details carefully.","Document Upload: Candidates are required to upload scanned copies of: Photograph: 4.5cm × 3.5cm, 20 KB to 50 KB. Signature (on white paper with black ink): 10 KB to 20 KB. Left Thumb Impression (on white paper with black or blue ink): 20 KB to 50 KB. Handwritten Declaration (on white paper with black ink): A specific text provided in the notification, to be written by the candidate. Size: 50 KB to 100 KB.","Photograph: 4.5cm × 3.5cm, 20 KB to 50 KB.","Signature (on white paper with black ink): 10 KB to 20 KB.","Left Thumb Impression (on white paper with black or blue ink): 20 KB to 50 KB.","Handwritten Declaration (on white paper with black ink): A specific text provided in the notification, to be written by the candidate. Size: 50 KB to 100 KB."]},{"heading":"Stage 3: Fee Payment","body":["Amount: ₹750/- for General, EWS, and OBC candidates. NIL for SC, ST, and PwBD candidates.","₹750/- for General, EWS, and OBC candidates.","NIL for SC, ST, and PwBD candidates.","Payment Modes: The fee can be paid online using Debit Card, Credit Card, or Internet Banking."]},{"heading":"Stage 4: Final Submission","body":["Review and Submit: Preview the application form to verify all details before final submission. Once submitted, no changes can be made.","Print Application & e-Receipt: After successful payment, take a printout of the system-generated application form and the e-receipt for future reference.","Common Mistakes to Avoid","Handwritten Declaration: Do not use capital letters for the entire declaration. Write it exactly as specified in the notification.","Document Specifications: Ensure all scanned documents meet the size and dimension criteria to avoid rejection.","Final Submission: Submitting the application without paying the fee (if applicable) will lead to cancellation.","Email and Mobile Number: Use a personal and active email ID and mobile number, as all communications from SBI will be sent to them."]}]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,6) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.gov_exams (category,name,summary,content,links,display_order) values ('SSC','SEBI Grade A (IT Officer)','Securities and Exchange Board of India - Assistant Manager (Information Technology)','[{"heading":"Overview","body":["Securities and Exchange Board of India - Assistant Manager (Information Technology)"]},{"heading":"The Philosophy Behind the Examination","body":["The SEBI Grade A (IT) exam is designed to select candidates with a strong technical foundation and the ability to apply their skills in a high-stakes regulatory environment. The selection process aims to identify individuals who possess:","Technical Expertise: Deep knowledge of core computer science and IT subjects like networking, databases, cybersecurity, and programming.","Analytical & Problem-Solving Skills: The ability to analyze complex technical issues, design solutions, and troubleshoot problems in a mission-critical infrastructure.","General Aptitude: Foundational skills in quantitative aptitude, reasoning, and English, which are essential for any managerial role.","Awareness of Financial Markets: A basic understanding of the financial sector is beneficial, as the role involves supporting the technological backbone of the market regulator.","The Role of an IT Officer in SEBI","An Assistant Manager in the IT stream at SEBI is responsible for a wide range of critical functions, including:"]},{"heading":"The Three-Stage Selection Process","body":["The selection process is a rigorous three-tiered structure designed to holistically evaluate a candidate''s suitability.","Stage 1: Phase I (Online Screening Test): This is a qualifying examination consisting of two papers. Paper 1 tests general aptitude, while Paper 2 focuses on specialized IT knowledge. Its primary purpose is to screen candidates for the next phase.","Stage 2: Phase II (Online Examination): Candidates who clear Phase I appear for this stage. It also consists of two papers: Paper 1 is a descriptive English test, and Paper 2 is an advanced test of IT knowledge. The marks from Phase II are given high weightage in the final merit list.","Stage 3: Interview: Candidates who clear the Phase II cut-off are shortlisted for a personal interview. The interview assesses technical knowledge, personality, and suitability for a regulatory body.","The Final Verdict: Merit and Allocation","The final merit list is prepared by assigning a weightage of 85% to the marks obtained in Phase II and 15% to the marks obtained in the Interview . Successful candidates are offered the position of Assistant Manager (Grade A) and undergo a probationary period, typically of two years."]},{"heading":"Typical Timeline (Indicative)","body":["The SEBI Grade A recruitment cycle is not fixed annually, but when it occurs, it typically spans 4-6 months from notification to the final result. The following is an example timeline.","Disclaimer: This timeline is indicative. SEBI''s recruitment is not on a fixed annual calendar. Always refer to the official SEBI website for the latest notifications.","Phase 1: Notification & Application (e.g., March - April)","March: The official detailed notification is released on the SEBI website (sebi.gov.in).","March - April: The online application window remains open for approximately 3-4 weeks.","Phase 2: Phase I Examination (e.g., May)","May (Early): Call letters for the Phase I online examination are released.","May (Mid/Late): The Phase I online examination is conducted on a single day across various centers.","Phase 3: Phase I Result & Phase II Examination (e.g., June - July)","June: The results of the Phase I examination are declared, and a list of shortlisted candidates is published.","July (Early): Call letters for the Phase II online examination are released.","July (Mid/Late): The Phase II online examination is conducted.","Phase 4: Interview & Final Result (e.g., August - September)","August: Results of Phase II are declared, and a list of candidates shortlisted for the interview is released. Interview call letters are sent out.","August/September: Interviews are conducted, usually at the SEBI Head Office in Mumbai.","September: The final result, based on combined Phase II and interview scores, is published."]},{"heading":"Eligibility Criteria","body":["Candidates must strictly adhere to the eligibility criteria regarding nationality, age, and educational qualifications as specified in the official notification.","1. Nationality","A candidate must be a citizen of India.","2. Age Limit","A candidate must not have exceeded the age of 30 years as on the date specified in the notification (e.g., March 31, 2025). This means the candidate must have been born on or after the corresponding date (e.g., April 01, 1995).","3. Educational Qualification (for IT Stream)"]},{"heading":"A candidate must possess one of the following qualifications","body":["Bachelor’s Degree in Engineering (Electrical / Electronics / Electronics and Communication / Information Technology / Computer Science) from a recognized University / Institute. OR","Masters in Computers Application (MCA) from a recognized University / Institute. OR","Bachelor’s Degree in any discipline with a post-graduate qualification (minimum 2 years duration) in Computers / Information Technology from a recognized University / Institute.","Note: A candidate appearing for the final year/semester of their degree can also apply, provided they can produce proof of passing the examination by the date specified in the official notification.","4. Number of Attempts","There is no restriction on the number of attempts for the SEBI Grade A examination, provided the candidate fulfills the age and educational criteria."]},{"heading":"Stages, Exam Pattern & Marking Scheme","body":["The selection process for SEBI Grade A (IT) is a three-stage process: Phase I, Phase II, and an Interview. All papers (except English Descriptive) will be available in both English and Hindi.","Phase I: Online Screening Examination","Phase I is an online examination consisting of two objective-type papers. To be shortlisted for Phase II, a candidate must clear the individual cut-offs for each paper and the aggregate cut-off.","Negative Marking: There will be a penalty of 1/4th of the marks assigned to the question for each wrong answer in both papers.","Phase II: Online Examination","Candidates shortlisted from Phase I will appear for Phase II. The marks from this phase are crucial for the final selection.","Negative Marking: There will be a penalty of 1/4th of the marks for wrong answers in Paper 2."]},{"heading":"Stage 3: Interview","body":["Candidates who clear the aggregate cut-off in Phase II will be called for an interview. The interview can be conducted in either Hindi or English, at the candidate''s choice.","Final Rank Calculation","The final merit list is prepared based on the candidate''s performance in Phase II and the Interview. The weightage is as follows:","Marks obtained in Phase II Examination: 85% Weightage","Marks obtained in Interview: 15% Weightage","Application Workflow","The application process is conducted entirely online through the official SEBI website.","Step 1: Registration: Candidates must visit the SEBI careers page and click on the link for new registration. They need to provide a valid email ID and mobile number.","Step 2: Filling the Application Form: Fill in personal, academic, and other details carefully.","Step 3: Document Upload: Upload a recent photograph, signature, left thumb impression, and a handwritten declaration as per the specifications given in the notification.","Step 4: Fee Payment: The application fee (e.g., ₹1000 for General/OBC/EWS and ₹100 for SC/ST/PwBD) must be paid online.","Step 5: Final Submission: After verifying all details, submit the application form and take a printout for future reference."]},{"heading":"Syllabus","body":["The syllabus for the SEBI Grade A (IT) exam covers a broad range of topics from general aptitude to specialized technical subjects."]},{"heading":"Phase I - Paper 1 Syllabus (Common to all streams)","body":["Quantitative Aptitude: Number Series, Simplification/Approximation, Quadratic Equations, Data Interpretation (Bar, Pie, Line, Tabular), Time & Work, Speed Time & Distance, Profit & Loss, Simple & Compound Interest, Ratio & Proportion, Percentage, Averages.","Test of Reasoning: Puzzles, Seating Arrangements, Syllogism, Blood Relations, Direction Sense, Inequality, Coding-Decoding, Logical Reasoning.","English Language: Reading Comprehension, Cloze Test, Para Jumbles, Spotting Errors, Fill in the Blanks, Sentence Improvement, Vocabulary (Synonyms/Antonyms).","General Awareness: Current Affairs (especially related to the financial sector), Static GK, Banking & Insurance Awareness, Economy, Government Schemes, Books & Authors, Important Days."]},{"heading":"Phase II - Paper 1 Syllabus (English Descriptive)","body":["Essay Writing: Topics will often be related to finance, technology, economy, or social issues.","Précis Writing: Summarizing a given passage to about one-third of its length.","Reading Comprehension: Answering questions based on a given passage."]},{"heading":"Phase I & II - Paper 2 Syllabus (Information Technology)","body":["The syllabus for Paper 2 is the same for both phases, but the difficulty level is higher in Phase II.","Database Concepts & SQL: ER-diagrams, normalization, transaction management, concurrency control, SQL queries.","Data Structures & Algorithms: Array, Linked List, Stack, Queue, Tree, Hash Table, Graph. Searching and Sorting algorithms, Time & Space complexity analysis.","Programming Concepts: Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) principles using C++ or Java, basic control structures, functions, pointers (C/C++), exception handling.","Computer Networks: OSI & TCP/IP models, IP addressing (IPv4/IPv6), routing protocols, TCP/UDP, DNS, HTTP, network security basics (firewalls, IDS/IPS).","Operating System: Processes, threads, memory management, file systems, CPU scheduling, deadlocks, concurrency and synchronization.","Software Engineering: Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC) models (Waterfall, Agile), software design principles, testing methodologies.","Information & Cyber Security: Cryptography basics (symmetric/asymmetric), network security protocols (SSL/TLS), common vulnerabilities & attacks (SQL Injection, XSS), security governance.","Data Analytics: Introduction to Data Warehousing, Big Data concepts, basics of languages like R and Python for data analysis.","Shell Programming: Basic Linux/Unix commands and shell scripting.","Frequently Asked Questions"]}]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,7) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.gov_exams (category,name,summary,content,links,display_order) values ('SSC','SSC CGL','The Staff Selection Commission (SSC) Combined Graduate Level (CGL) Examination is a prominent national-level exam conducted to recruit personnel for Group ‘B’ (Non-Gazetted) and Group ‘C’ (Non-Technical) posts in various Ministries, Departm','[{"heading":"About the Examination","body":["The Staff Selection Commission (SSC) Combined Graduate Level (CGL) Examination is a prominent national-level exam conducted to recruit personnel for Group ‘B’ (Non-Gazetted) and Group ‘C’ (Non-Technical) posts in various Ministries, Departments, and Organizations of the Government of India. It is one of the most sought-after examinations for graduates in India, offering a stable and prestigious career in the central government workforce."]},{"heading":"The Philosophy Behind the Examination","body":["The SSC CGL exam is structured to select candidates who possess strong foundational knowledge and aptitude required for administrative and executive roles. The core philosophy is to identify individuals who demonstrate:","Quantitative & Analytical Skills: The ability to work with numbers, interpret data, and solve problems logically.","Reasoning Ability: The capacity for logical deduction, pattern recognition, and critical thinking.","Language Proficiency: Strong comprehension and communication skills, particularly in English.","General Awareness: A good understanding of the static and current events shaping the world, with a focus on India.","Adaptability: Suitability for a wide range of roles, from clerical and fieldwork to audit and analysis."]},{"heading":"The Posts and Services at Stake","body":["Successful candidates are allocated to various posts based on their rank and preferences. These posts are categorized by their Pay Level in the 7th Central Pay Commission."]},{"heading":"The Two-Tiered Selection Process","body":["The CGL is a multi-stage process where each tier serves a specific purpose in filtering and selecting the most competent candidates.","Stage 1: Tier-I Examination (The Qualifying Filter): This is an objective-type, computer-based screening test. With lakhs of applicants, its primary role is to shortlist candidates for the next stage. It tests the breadth of a candidate''s aptitude across four key areas: Reasoning, General Awareness, Quantitative Aptitude, and English. Crucially, the marks from this stage are not counted for the final merit list. It is only qualifying in nature.","Stage 2: Tier-II Examination (The Merit Decider): Candidates who clear Tier-I undertake the Tier-II Examination, which is the decisive stage. It is also a computer-based test that evaluates a candidate''s subject-specific knowledge and skills in greater depth. The marks from the Tier-II Examination are the sole determinant of the final score and rank . This stage also includes qualifying tests for computer knowledge and data entry speed.","The Final Verdict: Merit and Allocation","The final merit list is prepared based on the overall performance in the Tier-II Examination . Based on their final rank, the post preferences submitted by them, their category, and the available vacancies, candidates are allocated to a specific post and department. This final allocation marks the successful entry into a coveted central government job."]},{"heading":"Typical Timeline (Indicative)","body":["The SSC CGL examination cycle typically spans 8-10 months from notification to the final result. Aspirants should be prepared for a sustained period of preparation. The following is a general, indicative schedule.","Disclaimer: These timelines are based on past trends. Always refer to the official SSC calendar and notification for the specific year for exact dates.","Phase 1: Notification & Application (June - July)","June: The official notification for the CGL Examination is released by SSC on its website (ssc.nic.in). This document contains all vital information: post details, vacancies, eligibility, syllabus, and exam pattern.","June - July: The online application window opens on the day of notification and remains active for about one month . This involves using the One-Time Registration (OTR) portal to fill out the application form.","Phase 2: Tier-I Examination (August - September)","August: Approximately 1-2 weeks before the exam date , SSC releases the Application Status and e-Admit Card for the Tier-I Examination on its regional websites.","August / September: The Tier-I computer-based examination is conducted in multiple shifts over several days across the country.","Phase 3: Tier-I Result & Answer Key (October - November)","September: A few days after the exam concludes, the tentative Answer Key is released, and candidates can raise objections.","October / November: The results of the Tier-I Examination are declared, along with the category-wise cut-off marks. A list of qualified candidates is published.","Phase 4: Tier-II Examination (December)","November / December: Admit cards for the Tier-II Examination are released for all shortlisted candidates.","December: The Tier-II Examination is conducted. This is also a computer-based test and is typically held on a single day for most candidates.","Phase 5: Final Result and Post Allocation (February - March of next year)","January: The tentative Answer Key for Tier-II is released.","February: The final result, based on Tier-II scores, is declared. This includes a merit list of all successful candidates.","March onwards: The process of document verification and allocation of posts and departments to the selected candidates is carried out by the user departments."]},{"heading":"Eligibility Criteria","body":["Aspirants must carefully check their eligibility for the SSC CGL examination. The criteria set by the Staff Selection Commission are mandatory. Eligibility is determined by Nationality, Age Limit, and Educational Qualification.","Note: The details provided here are based on prevailing rules. These are subject to change, and the official SSC notification for the current examination year is the ultimate authority.","1. Nationality"]},{"heading":"A candidate must be either","body":["(a) a citizen of India, or","(b) a subject of Nepal, or","(c) a subject of Bhutan, or","(d) a Tibetan refugee who came over to India, before the 1st January 1962 with the intention of permanently settling in India, or","(e) a person of Indian origin who has migrated from Pakistan, Burma, Sri Lanka, East African Countries of Kenya, Uganda, the United Republic of Tanzania, Zambia, Malawi, Zaire, Ethiopia and Vietnam with the intention of permanently settling in India.","Candidates belonging to categories (b), (c), (d) and (e) must possess a certificate of eligibility issued by the Government of India.","2. Age Limit","The age limit for SSC CGL varies for different posts. The age is calculated as of a specific cut-off date, typically the 1st of August of the examination year.","For posts with age limit 18-27 years: Candidate must have been born not earlier than 02-08-1998 and not later than 01-08-2007 (for CGL 2025, as an example).","For posts with age limit 20-30 years: Candidate must have been born not earlier than 02-08-1995 and not later than 01-08-2005.","For posts with age limit 18-30 years: Candidate must have been born not earlier than 02-08-1995 and not later than 01-08-2007.","For posts with age limit 18-32 years: Candidate must have been born not earlier than 02-08-1993 and not later than 01-08-2007.","SSC provides relaxations in the upper age limit for reserved categories:","3. Educational Qualification","The minimum educational qualification for most posts is a Bachelor''s Degree.","For All Posts (except AAO and JSO): A Bachelor''s Degree from a recognized University or equivalent.","For Assistant Audit Officer/Assistant Accounts Officer (AAO): Essential: Bachelor''s Degree from a recognized University. Desirable: Chartered Accountant or Cost & Management Accountant or Company Secretary or Masters in Commerce or Masters in Business Studies or Masters in Business Administration (Finance) or Masters in Business Economics.","Essential: Bachelor''s Degree from a recognized University.","Desirable: Chartered Accountant or Cost & Management Accountant or Company Secretary or Masters in Commerce or Masters in Business Studies or Masters in Business Administration (Finance) or Masters in Business Economics.","For Junior Statistical Officer (JSO): Bachelor''s Degree in any subject from a recognized University or Institute with at least 60% Marks in Mathematics at 12th standard level; OR Bachelor''s Degree in any subject with Statistics as one of the subjects at degree level.","Bachelor''s Degree in any subject from a recognized University or Institute with at least 60% Marks in Mathematics at 12th standard level; OR","Bachelor''s Degree in any subject with Statistics as one of the subjects at degree level.","Final-Year Students: Candidates who are in the final year of their graduation can also apply, however, they must possess the essential qualification on or before the cut-off date specified in the official notice.","4. Number of Attempts","There is no limit on the number of attempts a candidate can take for the SSC CGL examination. A candidate may appear for the examination as many times as they wish, provided they satisfy all other eligibility conditions, including the age limit for their desired post(s)."]},{"heading":"Stages, Exam Pattern & Marking Scheme","body":["The SSC CGL examination is conducted in two tiers. A thorough understanding of the pattern and marking scheme of both tiers is essential for a focused preparation strategy.","Tier-I Examination (Qualifying Nature)","The Tier-I exam is a computer-based objective test designed to screen candidates for the next stage. Its marks are not counted for the final merit list, but candidates must clear the category-wise cut-off to be eligible for Tier-II.","Total Duration: 1 hour (60 minutes). 80 minutes for PwBD candidates.","Question Type: Objective Type, Multiple Choice Questions (MCQs).","Language: The questions will be set in both English & Hindi, except for the English Comprehension part.","Negative Marking: There is a penalty of 0.50 marks for each incorrect answer."]},{"heading":"Tier-II Examination (Merit Deciding Stage)","body":["This is the final and most crucial stage. The final merit list and post allocation depend entirely on the scores obtained in Tier-II. It consists of three separate papers.","Negative Marking: For Paper-I: 1 mark is deducted for each wrong answer. For Paper-II & Paper-III: 0.50 marks are deducted for each wrong answer.","For Paper-I: 1 mark is deducted for each wrong answer.","For Paper-II & Paper-III: 0.50 marks are deducted for each wrong answer.","Qualifying Sections: The Computer Knowledge Test (CKT) and Data Entry Speed Test (DEST) are mandatory but qualifying in nature. Candidates must clear the minimum cut-off in these to be considered for the final merit list.","Final Rank Calculation","The final merit list is prepared based on the overall performance in Tier-II. The scores are calculated as follows:","For posts other than JSO & AAO: Marks scored in Section-I & Section-II of Paper-I. (Total 390 Marks)","For JSO: Marks from Paper-I + Marks from Paper-II. (Total 590 Marks)","For AAO: Marks from Paper-I + Marks from Paper-III. (Total 590 Marks)","Application Workflow","The application process for SSC CGL is entirely online through the new official portal (ssc.gov.in) . The process requires careful attention to detail to avoid rejection."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: One-Time Registration (OTR)","body":["This is a mandatory, one-time activity for all aspirants. Once completed, your basic details are saved for all future SSC examinations.","Purpose: OTR saves a candidate''s personal details, contact information, and declaration, which are automatically fetched when applying for an exam.","Information Required: You will need personal details (Aadhaar, Name, Father''s Name, Mother''s Name, DOB), contact details (Mobile Number, Email ID), and Class 10 Board details.","Verification: Mobile number and email ID are verified via OTP. Use personal and active contact details for all SSC communications."]},{"heading":"Stage 2: Filling the Examination Application Form","body":["After OTR, log in to the SSC portal, navigate to the live examinations, and select the Combined Graduate Level Examination.","Personal Information: Verify the data auto-filled from your OTR profile.","Educational Qualification: Fill in the details of your highest qualification and your graduation degree.","Preference for Examination Centres: Select three examination centres in order of preference within the same region.","Fee Payment: Amount: A non-refundable fee of ₹100 (Rupees One Hundred only). Exemptions: Women candidates and candidates belonging to SC, ST, PwBD, and Ex-Servicemen (ESM) categories are exempted from payment of the fee. Payment Modes: The fee can be paid online through BHIM UPI, Net Banking, or by using Visa, Mastercard, Maestro, RuPay Credit or Debit cards.","Amount: A non-refundable fee of ₹100 (Rupees One Hundred only).","Exemptions: Women candidates and candidates belonging to SC, ST, PwBD, and Ex-Servicemen (ESM) categories are exempted from payment of the fee.","Payment Modes: The fee can be paid online through BHIM UPI, Net Banking, or by using Visa, Mastercard, Maestro, RuPay Credit or Debit cards.","Photograph and Signature Upload: Scanned Photograph: Must be a recent passport-size colour picture (not more than 3 months old). Must be without cap and spectacles, with both ears clearly visible. Format: JPEG/JPG . Size: 20 KB to 50 KB . Scanned Signature: Must not be blurred. Format: JPEG/JPG . Size: 10 KB to 20 KB .","Scanned Photograph: Must be a recent passport-size colour picture (not more than 3 months old). Must be without cap and spectacles, with both ears clearly visible. Format: JPEG/JPG . Size: 20 KB to 50 KB .","Must be a recent passport-size colour picture (not more than 3 months old).","Must be without cap and spectacles, with both ears clearly visible.","Format: JPEG/JPG . Size: 20 KB to 50 KB .","Scanned Signature: Must not be blurred. Format: JPEG/JPG . Size: 10 KB to 20 KB .","Must not be blurred.","Format: JPEG/JPG . Size: 10 KB to 20 KB ."]},{"heading":"Stage 3: Final Submission and Post-Submission Actions","body":["Review and Submit: Before final submission, carefully review the entire application form in the preview section. After submission, no changes are possible outside the correction window.","Download Application PDF: After submission, download the filled application form and keep a copy for your records.","Application Form Correction Window: SSC provides a specific window (usually 2-3 days) after the application deadline closes to allow candidates to correct their forms by paying a correction charge.","Common Mistakes to Avoid","Last-Minute Rush: Applying on the last day can lead to server issues and payment failures.","Incorrect Photo/Signature: Uploading an old photograph, a photo with a cap/goggles, or a blurred signature is a common reason for rejection.","Mismatch in Details: Ensure your name, date of birth, and parents'' names match your matriculation (Class 10) certificate exactly.","Incomplete Application: Failing to complete the payment (if applicable) or final submission will render the application incomplete."]}]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,8) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.gov_exams (category,name,summary,content,links,display_order) values ('SSC','SSC CHSL','Combined Higher Secondary (10+2) Level Examination','[{"heading":"Overview","body":["Combined Higher Secondary (10+2) Level Examination"]},{"heading":"The Philosophy Behind the Examination","body":["The CHSL exam is meticulously designed to select individuals with strong clerical and operational aptitude, who form the foundational support system of the government machinery. The examination''s core philosophy is to assess a candidate''s suitability for roles that require precision, speed, and adherence to procedures. It aims to identify individuals who demonstrate:","Clerical Aptitude & Procedural Clarity: The ability to efficiently handle routine office tasks, manage files and data, and comprehend and follow official procedures.","Speed & Accuracy: A high degree of proficiency in typing and data entry, which is a critical, non-negotiable skill for the posts offered.","Basic Computer Proficiency: Foundational knowledge of computer operations, common office software (like MS Office), and internet usage.","Foundational Knowledge & Aptitude: A solid grasp of basic quantitative skills, logical reasoning, English language comprehension, and an awareness of the world around them."]},{"heading":"The Posts and Services at Stake","body":["Successful candidates are recruited for essential clerical, secretarial, and data management roles that are the backbone of government administration. The primary posts are:"]},{"heading":"The Two-Tiered Selection Process","body":["The CHSL selection is a two-stage process, with each tier serving a distinct purpose in filtering and selecting the most suitable candidates.","Stage 1: Tier-I Examination (The Great Filter): This is an objective, computer-based test that acts as a screening mechanism. Due to the massive number of applicants, its primary role is to shortlist a manageable number of candidates for the next, more intensive stage. It tests general aptitude across four core areas. Crucially, the marks from this stage are not counted for the final merit list; it is purely qualifying in nature.","Stage 2: Tier-II Examination (The Merit and Skill Decider): This is the final and most crucial stage. It is conducted in a single day and has multiple components. It includes an objective, computer-based test (whose marks determine the final merit) and mandatory qualifying tests for computer knowledge and data entry/typing skills. A candidate''s final rank and selection depend entirely on their performance in the objective section of Tier-II.","The Final Verdict: Merit and Allocation","The final merit list is prepared based on the overall performance in the objective sections (Section-I and Section-II) of the Tier-II Examination . Based on their final rank, the post preferences submitted by them, their category, and the available vacancies, candidates are allocated to a specific post and department, marking their successful entry into the central government services."]},{"heading":"Typical Timeline (Indicative)","body":["The SSC CHSL examination cycle generally spans 8 to 10 months from notification to the final result. Aspirants must plan for a sustained period of preparation.","Disclaimer: These timelines are based on past trends. Always refer to the official SSC calendar and notification for the specific year for exact dates.","Phase 1: Notification & Application (April - May)","Early April: The official notification for the CHSL Examination is released by SSC on its website (ssc.gov.in), containing all crucial details like vacancies, eligibility, syllabus, and exam dates.","April - May: The online application window opens on the day of notification and remains active for about one month .","Phase 2: Tier-I Examination (July - August)","Early July: Approximately two weeks before the exam date, SSC releases the Application Status and e-Admit Cards for the Tier-I Examination on its regional websites.","July / August: The Tier-I computer-based examination is conducted in multiple shifts over several days across the country.","Phase 3: Tier-I Result & Answer Key (September - October)","Late August: A few days after the conclusion of the exam, the tentative Answer Key is released, and candidates are given a window to raise objections.","September / October: The results of the Tier-I Examination are declared. SSC publishes lists of candidates who have qualified for Tier-II.","Phase 4: Tier-II Examination (November - December)","Early November: Admit cards for the Tier-II Examination are released for all shortlisted candidates.","Late November / Early December: The Tier-II Examination (Objective Test + Skill/Typing Test) is conducted, typically on a single day for all candidates.","Phase 5: Final Result and Post Allocation (February - March of next year)","January: After the Tier-II exam, SSC opens a window for qualified candidates to submit their detailed Post Preferences online. This is a mandatory step for final consideration.","February: The final result, based on the performance in Tier-II, is declared. This includes the final merit list of all selected candidates."]},{"heading":"Eligibility Criteria","body":["Aspirants must carefully check their eligibility for the SSC CHSL examination. The criteria set by the Staff Selection Commission are mandatory and are based on Nationality, Age Limit, and Educational Qualification.","Note: The official SSC notification for the current examination year is the ultimate authority. The cut-off date for age and educational qualifications mentioned in it is final.","1. Nationality","A candidate must be either: (a) a citizen of India, or (b) a subject of Nepal, or (c) a subject of Bhutan, or a person under other specified categories who possesses a certificate of eligibility issued by the Government of India.","2. Age Limit","The age limit for all posts under SSC CHSL is standard. The age is calculated as of a specific cut-off date, typically the 1st of August of the examination year.","Age Bracket: A candidate must be between 18 and 27 years of age.","This means a candidate must have been born not earlier than 02-08-1998 and not later than 01-08-2007 (for the 2025 exam, as an example).","SSC provides relaxations in the upper age limit for reserved categories as per government norms:","3. Educational Qualification","The minimum educational qualification for the CHSL exam is passing the 12th standard, with a specific requirement for one type of post.","For Lower Division Clerk (LDC) / Junior Secretariat Assistant (JSA) and Data Entry Operator (DEO): Candidates must have passed the 12th Standard or an equivalent examination from a recognized Board or University.","For Data Entry Operator (DEO) Grade ‘A’ in the Office of the Comptroller and Auditor General of India (C&AG), Ministry of Consumer Affairs, Food & Public Distribution, and Ministry of Culture: Candidates must have passed the 12th Standard in the Science stream with Mathematics as a subject from a recognized Board or equivalent.","Final-Year Students: Candidates who have appeared for their 12th Standard or equivalent examination can also apply. However, they must possess the essential qualification on or before the cut-off date specified in the official notice."]},{"heading":"Stages, Exam Pattern & Marking Scheme","body":["The SSC CHSL examination is a two-tier process. The final merit list is prepared based exclusively on the scores from the objective section of Tier-II, provided the candidate qualifies in all other mandatory tests.","Tier-I Examination (Qualifying Nature)","This is a computer-based screening test. Its marks are not counted for the final merit, and it serves only to shortlist candidates for Tier-II.","Total Duration: 1 hour (60 minutes). 80 minutes for PwBD candidates.","Question Type: Objective Type, Multiple Choice Questions (MCQs).","Language: Questions are set in both English & Hindi, except for the English Language part.","Negative Marking: There is a penalty of 0.50 marks for each incorrect answer."]},{"heading":"Tier-II Examination (Merit Deciding Stage)","body":["This crucial stage consists of multiple sections and modules, including merit-deciding objective tests and mandatory qualifying skill tests, conducted in two separate sessions on the same day.","Merit Sections: Final merit is based *only* on the total marks of Section-I and Section-II.","Marking Scheme: For Section-I, Section-II, and Module-I of Section-III, each correct answer fetches +3 marks .","Negative Marking: There is a penalty of 1 mark for each incorrect answer in the objective tests of Tier-II.","Qualifying Sections: The Computer Knowledge Test and the Skill/Typing Test are mandatory for all candidates but are qualifying in nature. Failure to clear these will lead to disqualification, regardless of the score in merit sections.","Final Rank Calculation","The final merit list is prepared based on the cumulative score of the two merit-deciding sections of Tier-II.","Total Marks in Section-I (out of 180) + Total Marks in Section-II (out of 180) = Grand Total for Merit: 360 Marks","Application Workflow","The application process for SSC CHSL is conducted exclusively online through the official SSC portal (ssc.gov.in) . The process requires careful attention to detail to avoid rejection."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: One-Time Registration (OTR)","body":["This is a mandatory, one-time activity for all aspirants. Once completed, your basic details are saved for all future SSC examinations, simplifying the application process.","Information Required: You will need personal details (Aadhaar, Name, Father''s Name, Mother''s Name, DOB), contact details (Mobile Number, Email ID), and Class 10 Board details.","Verification: Mobile number and email ID are verified via OTP. Use personal and active contact details for all SSC communications."]},{"heading":"Stage 2: Filling the Examination Application Form","body":["After OTR, log in to the SSC portal, navigate to the live examinations, and select the Combined Higher Secondary (10+2) Level Examination.","Personal Information: Verify the data auto-filled from your OTR profile.","Educational Qualification: Fill in the details of your 12th standard examination.","Preference for Examination Centres: Select three examination centres in order of preference.","Fee Payment: Amount: A non-refundable fee of ₹100 . Exemptions: Women candidates and candidates belonging to SC, ST, PwBD, and Ex-Servicemen (ESM) categories are exempted . Payment Modes: The fee can be paid online through BHIM UPI, Net Banking, or by using Visa, Mastercard, Maestro, RuPay Credit or Debit cards.","Amount: A non-refundable fee of ₹100 .","Exemptions: Women candidates and candidates belonging to SC, ST, PwBD, and Ex-Servicemen (ESM) categories are exempted .","Payment Modes: The fee can be paid online through BHIM UPI, Net Banking, or by using Visa, Mastercard, Maestro, RuPay Credit or Debit cards.","Photograph and Signature Upload: Scanned Photograph: Must be a recent passport-size colour picture (not more than 3 months old), without cap and spectacles, with both ears clearly visible. Format: JPEG/JPG. Size: 20 KB to 50 KB. Scanned Signature: Must not be blurred. Format: JPEG/JPG. Size: 10 KB to 20 KB.","Scanned Photograph: Must be a recent passport-size colour picture (not more than 3 months old), without cap and spectacles, with both ears clearly visible. Format: JPEG/JPG. Size: 20 KB to 50 KB.","Scanned Signature: Must not be blurred. Format: JPEG/JPG. Size: 10 KB to 20 KB."]},{"heading":"Stage 3: Final Submission and Post-Submission Actions","body":["Review and Submit: Before final submission, carefully review the entire application form in the preview section. After submission, no changes are possible outside the correction window.","Download Application PDF: After submission, download the filled application form and keep a copy for your records.","Application Form Correction Window: SSC provides a specific window (usually 2-3 days) after the application deadline closes to allow candidates to correct their forms by paying a correction charge."]},{"heading":"Syllabus","body":["The syllabus for the CHSL exam is based on the curriculum up to the 10th and 12th standards. The Tier-II syllabus is an extension of Tier-I with a slightly higher difficulty level."]}]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,9) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.gov_exams (category,name,summary,content,links,display_order) values ('SSC','SSC JE','The Staff Selection Commission Junior Engineer (SSC JE) Examination is a major national-level exam for recruiting Junior Engineers, who are Group ‘B’ (Non-Gazetted) technical staff. This exam is a fantastic opportunity for both Diploma hold','[{"heading":"About the Examination","body":["The Staff Selection Commission Junior Engineer (SSC JE) Examination is a major national-level exam for recruiting Junior Engineers, who are Group ‘B’ (Non-Gazetted) technical staff. This exam is a fantastic opportunity for both Diploma holders and Engineering Graduates to secure a government job that''s central to building and maintaining India''s infrastructure. It''s a role that puts you on the ground, applying your technical skills every day.","What SSC Looks For","The SSC JE exam isn''t just about formulas; it''s about finding practical-minded engineers. It''s designed to test your:","Core Engineering Knowledge: A solid grasp of the fundamentals of Civil, Mechanical, or Electrical engineering.","General Intelligence & Awareness: Strong reasoning skills and a good understanding of current events and general knowledge.","Practical Application: The ability to use your technical knowledge to solve real-world problems you''d face on a worksite.","The Departments and Disciplines","Acing the SSC JE exam can land you a position in some of the most vital government organizations. Recruitment happens for three core engineering streams."]},{"heading":"The Selection Process","body":["The selection process is straightforward and entirely based on written tests. There is no interview , making it a purely merit-based recruitment.","Paper-I (Computer Based Test): This is the first hurdle. It''s an objective test that checks your reasoning, general awareness, and basic technical knowledge. Your score here is crucial for getting shortlisted for the next stage.","Paper-II (Computer Based Test): If you clear Paper-I, you''ll take this test. It''s also an objective CBT but focuses entirely on your specific engineering discipline. The questions are more in-depth and carry higher marks.","Document Verification (DV): Candidates are shortlisted based on their combined scores in Paper-I and Paper-II. If your name is on the list, you''ll be called to verify your documents. That''s the final step before you get your posting!","The Final Merit","Your final rank depends on the combined score of both written papers.","Normalized Marks in Paper-I (out of 200) + Normalized Marks in Paper-II (out of 300) = Final Merit Score (out of 500)"]},{"heading":"Typical Timeline (Indicative)","body":["The SSC JE Exam follows a fairly regular annual schedule. As it''s currently October 2025, the process for the 2025 exam would be in its final stages.","Disclaimer: This is a typical schedule. Always check the official SSC calendar for exact dates.","Phase 1: Notification & Application (February - March)","The official notification usually comes out in late February, with the application window open for about a month.","Phase 2: Paper-I Exam (May - June)","The first computer-based test is typically held in late May or early June. Admit cards are available a week or two before the exam.","Phase 3: Paper-I Result (July - August)","SSC declares the Paper-I results and the cut-offs for each stream, shortlisting candidates for the next stage.","Phase 4: Paper-II Exam (September - October)","Paper-II is usually conducted a couple of months after the Paper-I results. Given the current date, this exam would have just concluded or be happening right now for the 2025 cycle.","Phase 5: Final Result & DV (December - January)","The final combined result is announced, and successful candidates are called for Document Verification, followed by the final allocation of departments."]},{"heading":"Eligibility Criteria","body":["Eligibility for SSC JE is very specific and can differ from one department to another. Always check the official notification for your chosen post.","1. Nationality","You must be a citizen of India (or meet other specific criteria for subjects of Nepal/Bhutan, etc., as per government rules).","2. Educational Qualification","You need either a B.E./B.Tech OR a 3-year Diploma in Civil, Mechanical, or Electrical Engineering. Some departments may also require a specific amount of work experience, especially for Diploma holders. This is a crucial detail to check in the official advertisement.","3. Age Limit","The age limit varies by department. The cut-off date is usually the 1st of August of the notification year. Standard age relaxations apply for reserved categories (SC/ST: 5 years, OBC: 3 years)."]},{"heading":"Exam Pattern & Marking Scheme","body":["The SSC JE exam has two objective computer-based tests.","Paper-I (Computer Based Test)","Duration: 2 Hours.","Negative Marking: Yes, -0.25 marks for each wrong answer.","Paper-II (Computer Based Test)","Negative Marking: Yes, -1 mark for each wrong answer.","Calculator: A virtual scientific calculator will be available on your screen.","Final Rank Calculation","Your final selection depends on your combined performance in both papers after marks are normalized.","Paper-I Normalized Score (out of 200) + Paper-II Normalized Score (out of 300) = Final Merit Score (out of 500)","Application Workflow","The application process for the SSC JE examination is conducted entirely online through the new official SSC website (ssc.gov.in). It''s a systematic process that requires careful attention to detail to avoid rejection. The workflow can be broken down into key stages."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: One-Time Registration (OTR)","body":["Before you can apply for any SSC exam, you must complete the One-Time Registration. This is a one-time activity that simplifies future applications.","Purpose: OTR saves your basic personal information (name, address, educational qualifications) on the SSC portal. This data is auto-filled whenever you apply for an exam, saving time and preventing errors.","Information Required: You will need your Aadhaar card, Class 10th and 12th details, a valid mobile number, a valid email ID, and scanned copies of your photograph and signature.","Verification: Your mobile number and email ID will be verified using OTPs. Ensure these are personal and active, as all communications from SSC will be sent to them."]},{"heading":"Stage 2: Filling the Application Form","body":["Once your OTR is complete, you can log in to the SSC portal and apply for the \"Junior Engineer (Civil, Mechanical & Electrical) Examination.\"","Personal Details: Most of your details will be automatically populated from your OTR profile. You will need to verify them.","Educational Qualifications: Provide the details of your Engineering Diploma or Degree.","Post Preferences: This is a critical step. You must carefully select and order your preferences for various departments (like CPWD, MES, BRO, etc.) that you are eligible for. This preference is final and is used for allocation after the results.","Examination Centre Selection: You will be asked to choose your preferred cities for the examination from a list of available centers."]},{"heading":"1. Fee Payment","body":["Amount: A non-refundable application fee of ₹100 (Rupees One Hundred only).","Exemptions: Women candidates and candidates belonging to Scheduled Caste (SC), Scheduled Tribe (ST), Persons with Benchmark Disabilities (PwBD), and Ex-Servicemen (ESM) are exempted from paying the fee.","Payment Modes: The fee can be paid online through BHIM UPI, Net Banking, or by using Visa, Mastercard, Maestro, or RuPay Credit/Debit cards."]},{"heading":"2. Uploading of Photograph and Signature","body":["SSC has very strict specifications for the photograph and signature. Failure to comply can lead to application rejection.","Live Photograph: The new application process requires candidates to capture a live photograph through the webcam on their computer or mobile device. Ensure good lighting and a plain background.","Scanned Signature: The signature must be scanned clearly in the specified format (typically JPG/JPEG , with a size between 10 KB to 20 KB )."]},{"heading":"Stage 4: Final Submission and Post-Submission Actions","body":["Review and Submit: Before the final submission, you will be shown a preview of your application. Review every detail for accuracy. Once submitted, changes are only possible during the ''Correction Window''.","Download Application PDF: After successful submission, download and print a copy of your application form for your records.","Application Correction Window: SSC provides a brief window (usually 2-3 days) after the application period closes to allow candidates to make corrections to their forms upon payment of a correction fee.","Common Mistakes to Avoid","Last-Minute Rush: Applying on the last day can lead to server issues, payment failures, and mistakes. Apply early.","Incorrect Photo/Signature: Failing to meet the specific requirements for the live photograph or scanned signature is a common reason for rejection.","Wrong Post Preferences: Not filling the post preferences carefully can lead to you being allocated to a department you didn''t want, even with a good rank.","Ignoring Eligibility: Applying for a post for which you do not meet the specific age or educational criteria mentioned in the notification."]},{"heading":"Syllabus","body":["The official notification from SSC is the final authority on the syllabus. The following is a detailed breakdown."]},{"heading":"Paper-I Syllabus","body":["This section tests your logical thinking. Expect questions on analogies, similarities, differences, problem-solving, analysis, judgment, decision making, visual memory, and arithmetical reasoning.","This covers a broad range of topics including current events, India and its neighboring countries (History, Culture, Geography, Economy), General Polity, and Scientific Research."]}]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,10) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.gov_exams (category,name,summary,content,links,display_order) values ('UPPSC','UPPSC PCS','The UPPSC PCS exam is designed to assess a candidate''s comprehensive abilities beyond mere academic knowledge. Its core philosophy is to identify individuals who possess a potent combination of intellectual depth, analytical acuity, ethical','[{"heading":"The Philosophy Behind the Examination","body":["The UPPSC PCS exam is designed to assess a candidate''s comprehensive abilities beyond mere academic knowledge. Its core philosophy is to identify individuals who possess a potent combination of intellectual depth, analytical acuity, ethical integrity, and a profound understanding of the state''s unique socio-economic landscape . The examination seeks to evaluate a candidate''s:","Critical Assimilation: The ability to understand complex information from diverse fields and grasp the core issues relevant to Uttar Pradesh and the nation.","Balance of Judgment: The capacity to form reasoned opinions, consider multiple perspectives, and make sound, unbiased judgments.","Intellectual and Moral Integrity: A strong ethical compass and the courage to uphold constitutional and administrative values.","Leadership and Social Cohesion: The potential to lead and work collaboratively to solve pressing societal problems at the state and local levels.","Awareness and Curiosity: A deep interest in local, state, national, and international affairs."]},{"heading":"The Services at Stake","body":["Successful candidates are allocated to various state-level services based on their rank and preferences. These services are the backbone of the state''s administrative framework."]},{"heading":"Key Group ''A'' & ''B'' Services","body":["and many others, including Naib Tehsildar, District Social Welfare Officer, etc."]},{"heading":"The Three-Tiered Selection Gauntlet","body":["The PCS exam is a multi-layered process where each stage serves as a progressively finer filter to identify the most suitable candidates. The entire cycle, from notification to the final result, spans over a year.","Stage 1: Preliminary Examination (The Filter): This is an objective-type (Multiple Choice Questions) screening test. Its primary role is to select a manageable number of candidates for the Mains exam. It tests the breadth of a candidate''s knowledge. Crucially, the marks from this stage are not counted for the final merit list. It is a pass/fail gateway to the next level.","Stage 2: Main Examination (The Crucible): Candidates who clear the Prelims undertake the Main Examination, a descriptive (written) exam that is the real test of intellectual depth and analytical skill. Spread over eight papers, it evaluates a candidate''s ability to articulate nuanced arguments and structure comprehensive answers, with a special focus on Uttar Pradesh. The marks from the Main Examination are a significant component of the final score.","Stage 3: Personality Test (The Final Appraisal): The final stage is an interview conducted by a board of distinguished experts at the UPPSC headquarters in Prayagraj. The board assesses the candidate''s personality, mental alertness, leadership qualities, and suitability for a career in the state''s public administration.","The Final Verdict: Merit and Allocation","The final merit list is prepared based on the total marks obtained in the Main Examination (1500 marks) and the Personality Test (100 marks) , for a grand total of 1600 marks. Based on their final rank, the preferences for services submitted by them, their category, and the vacancies available, candidates are allocated a service."]},{"heading":"Typical Timeline (Indicative)","body":["The UPPSC PCS schedule can vary, but it generally follows a year-long cycle. Understanding this timeline is crucial for strategic planning. The following is a typical, indicative schedule based on recent trends.","Disclaimer: These timelines are based on past trends. Always refer to the official UPPSC calendar and notifications for the specific year for exact dates.","Phase 1: Notification & Application (January - February)","January: The official notification for the Combined State/Upper Subordinate Services Examination is released by UPPSC on its website (uppsc.up.nic.in). This document contains all essential details.","January - February: The online application window opens and typically remains active for about a month. This process requires a one-time registration (OTR) before filling out the application form.","Phase 2: Preliminary Examination (May - July)","May/June: Admit Cards are released 2-3 weeks before the exam.","June/July: The Preliminary Exam is conducted on a single day, in two shifts.","Phase 3: Prelims Result & Mains Application (August - September)","August: The results of the Preliminary Examination are typically declared within 4-6 weeks.","August - September: Qualified candidates are required to fill out the Detailed Application Form (DAF) for the Mains exam.","Phase 4: Main Examination (October - November)","September/October: Admit cards for the Main Examination are released.","October/November: The Main Examination is a descriptive (written) test conducted over 5-6 days.","Phase 5: Mains Result & Interview Schedule (February - March)","February/March: The results of the Main Examination are usually announced about 3 to 4 months after the exam, and a list of candidates qualified for the interview is published.","Phase 6: Personality Tests (Interviews) (April - May)","The Personality Tests (Interviews) typically commence a few weeks after the Mains results are declared.","Candidates are summoned to the UPPSC office in Prayagraj in batches.","Phase 7: Final Result Declaration (May - June)","The final merit list, based on the combined scores of the Main Examination and the Personality Test, is declared, usually within a few weeks of the last interview."]},{"heading":"Eligibility Criteria (for Bihar Residents)","body":["Before starting your UPPSC preparation, it''s crucial to verify your eligibility. The criteria set by the Uttar Pradesh Public Service Commission are strict. For candidates from Bihar, it''s important to understand how reservation policies apply.","Note: The official UPPSC notification is the ultimate authority. The details here are based on prevailing rules.","1. Nationality","A candidate must be a citizen of India .","2. Age Limit","The age of a candidate is calculated as of July 1st of the examination year.","Minimum Age: A candidate must have attained the age of 21 years .","Maximum Age: A candidate must not have attained the age of more than 40 years .","Candidates from other states, including Bihar, are treated as General Category candidates in Uttar Pradesh for the purpose of reservation benefits. This means:","Important: Age relaxation benefits for categories like SC, ST, and OBC are available only to the domiciles of Uttar Pradesh. Bihar residents applying under these categories will compete in the General/Unreserved category in UP.","3. Educational Qualification"]},{"heading":"The minimum educational requirement is","body":["A candidate must hold a Bachelor''s Degree from any recognised University or an equivalent qualification up to the last date for receipt of application.","Some posts have specific educational requirements (e.g., a Law degree for Sub Registrar, a Commerce degree for District Audit Officer), which will be detailed in the official notification.","4. Number of Attempts","There is no limit on the number of attempts for the UPPSC PCS exam. Candidates can appear for the exam as long as they fulfill the age eligibility criteria."]},{"heading":"Stages, Exam Pattern & Marking Scheme","body":["The UPPSC PCS Examination is a three-stage process. Understanding this pattern is the first step toward a successful preparation strategy."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: Preliminary Examination (Objective Type)","body":["The primary purpose of the Prelims is to screen candidates for the Main Examination. The marks obtained in this stage are not counted for the final merit list.","Objective Format: Both papers consist of Multiple Choice Questions (MCQs).","Negative Marking: There is a penalty for incorrect answers. For each wrong answer, one-third (0.33%) of the marks assigned to that question will be deducted.","CSAT is Qualifying: Paper II (CSAT) is a qualifying paper. A candidate must score a minimum of 33% . If a candidate fails to score 33% in CSAT, their Paper I (GS) will not be evaluated.","Merit Calculation: The cut-off for the Main Examination is determined solely based on the marks scored in General Studies Paper I ."]},{"heading":"Stage 2: Main Examination (Descriptive Type)","body":["This stage is the core of the selection process. It consists of 8 descriptive papers. The optional subject has been removed since 2023.","The sum of marks from these eight papers determines the written score for the final merit list."]},{"heading":"Stage 3: Personality Test (Interview)","body":["The final stage is a face-to-face interview with the UPPSC board to assess personality and suitability for public service.","Maximum Marks: 100 Marks","Qualities Assessed: The board evaluates mental alertness, critical powers of assimilation, clear and logical exposition, balance of judgment, variety and depth of interest, ability for social cohesion and leadership, and intellectual and moral integrity.","Final Rank Calculation","The final merit list is prepared based on the cumulative score of the Main Examination and the Personality Test.","Main Exam (Written) Marks: 1500 + Personality Test (Interview) Marks: 100 = Grand Total: 1600 Marks","Application Workflow","The application process for the UPPSC PCS is conducted exclusively online through the official UPPSC website. It is a meticulous process that requires careful attention to detail."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: One-Time Registration (OTR)","body":["Before filling out the application form for any UPPSC examination, a candidate must complete the One-Time Registration. This is a mandatory, one-time activity.","Purpose: The OTR platform saves a candidate''s basic personal information, which can then be automatically populated for any UPPSC exam application, saving time and reducing errors.","Process: Visit the official UPPSC website and complete the OTR process by providing your basic personal, communication, and educational details. You will receive an OTR number upon completion."]}]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,11) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.gov_exams (category,name,summary,content,links,display_order) values ('UPSC','BARC OCES/DGFS','Scientific Officer Recruitment Examination','[{"heading":"Overview","body":["Scientific Officer Recruitment Examination"]},{"heading":"About the Examination","body":["The Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC) conducts its nationwide examination for the recruitment of Scientific Officers (Group ''A'' post) for various units of the Department of Atomic Energy (DAE). This is one of the most prestigious gateways for engineering graduates to embark on a career at the forefront of nuclear science, research, and technology in India. The recruitment is done through two main schemes: OCES and DGFS."]},{"heading":"The Schemes: OCES & DGFS","body":["Selected candidates are admitted into one of the two training programs, which are the cornerstone for grooming the next generation of scientists and engineers for the DAE.","OCES (Orientation Course for Engineering Graduates and Science Postgraduates): This is a one-year training program conducted at various BARC Training Schools located across India. OCES Trainee Scientific Officers (TSOs) who successfully complete the program are appointed as Scientific Officers ''C'' in one of the DAE units.","DGFS (DAE Graduate Fellowship Scheme for Engineering Graduates): This is a two-year program for candidates who want to pursue an M.Tech. degree while receiving a stipend from DAE. Fellows complete their M.Tech. at select premier institutes (like certain IITs/NITIE) and are then posted as Scientific Officers in DAE."]},{"heading":"The Philosophy Behind the Selection","body":["Unlike many other PSU exams that test a wide range of general and technical subjects, the BARC selection process is intensely focused on evaluating the depth of fundamental knowledge in a candidate''s core engineering discipline. It seeks individuals who possess:","Strong Conceptual Clarity: The ability to explain core engineering principles from the ground up.","Analytical & Problem-Solving Skills: The capacity to apply theoretical knowledge to solve complex, practical problems.","Research Aptitude: A curious and innovative mindset suitable for a research and development organization.","Sustained Academic Excellence: A consistent and strong academic record, reflecting a solid foundation."]},{"heading":"The Two-Stage Selection Process","body":["The BARC selection process is a two-step funnel designed to identify candidates with genuine technical prowess.","Stage 1: Screening Test (The Gateway): This is a shortlisting stage. Candidates can be shortlisted for the final interview based on their performance in either an online, discipline-specific examination conducted by BARC, or through their valid GATE (Graduate Aptitude Test in Engineering) score . This stage serves only to filter the candidates for the crucial next step.","Stage 2: Selection Interview (The Ultimate Test): This is the final and most decisive stage. Candidates shortlisted from Stage 1 are called for a personal interview in Mumbai. The interview, conducted by a panel of senior DAE scientists, is a purely technical and rigorous assessment of the candidate''s subject knowledge. The final selection is based solely on performance in this interview."]},{"heading":"Typical Timeline (Indicative)","body":["The BARC recruitment cycle is relatively short and efficient, typically concluding within 6-7 months from notification to the commencement of the course. Understanding this schedule is vital for aspirants.","Disclaimer: These are approximate timelines based on past trends. Always refer to the official BARC recruitment portal for the exact dates of the current cycle.","Phase 1: Notification & Application (December - January)","Late December / Early January: The official notification is released on the BARC online examination portal (barconlineexam.in). It contains details on eligible disciplines, vacancies, eligibility, and important dates.","January - Early February: The online application window opens and typically remains active for about a month. Candidates must fill the form, choose their shortlisting method (Online Exam, GATE Score, or both), and pay the fee.","Phase 2: Admit Card Release (Late February)","Approximately 2-3 weeks before the exam window , BARC releases the admit cards for candidates who opted for the online examination.","Phase 3: Online Examination (March)","The BARC online computer-based test is conducted over a window of a few days (e.g., a weekend) at various centers across India. Each candidate is assigned a specific date and time slot.","Phase 4: Shortlisting for Interview (March - April)","Late March: Results of the online examination are declared.","Early April: A consolidated list of candidates shortlisted for the interview is released. This list includes candidates selected via the online exam and those selected based on their GATE scores.","Phase 5: Interview Slot Booking (April)","Shortlisted candidates are required to log in to the portal and book their preferred date for the selection interview from the available slots."]},{"heading":"Phase 6: Selection Interviews (May - June)","body":["The personal interviews are conducted over several weeks, primarily at the BARC campus in Mumbai.","Phase 7: Final Result Declaration (Late June / Early July)","A final merit list of selected candidates is published, based entirely on their performance in the interview. Selected candidates are notified about their allotted scheme (OCES or DGFS).","Phase 8: Commencement of Program (August)","The Orientation Course (OCES) typically begins in the first week of August at the BARC Training Schools. DGFS fellows join their respective M.Tech. programs."]},{"heading":"Eligibility Criteria","body":["Candidates must strictly fulfill the eligibility criteria related to nationality, age, and educational qualifications to be considered for the BARC Scientific Officer position.","Note: The official notification for the current examination year is the final authority on all eligibility criteria.","1. Nationality","The candidate must be a citizen of India .","2. Age Limit","The age is calculated as of the 1st of August of the examination year. The general age limits are:","Note: Physically Challenged persons of all categories are eligible for age relaxation of 10 years.","3. Educational Qualification","Qualifying Degree: A B.E. / B.Tech. / B.Sc. (Engineering) / 5-year Integrated M.Tech. degree is required.","Minimum Marks: Candidates must have a minimum of 60% aggregate marks (or equivalent CGPA) in their qualifying degree.","Final Year Students: Students in the final year of their B.E./B.Tech. or Integrated M.Tech. are eligible to apply, provided they can produce their final results by the specified date in the notification.","4. Eligible Engineering Disciplines","Candidates must have a degree in one of the following (or equivalent) engineering disciplines:","The list of eligible disciplines and their equivalent branches is detailed in the official notification."]},{"heading":"GATE Score Eligibility","body":["Candidates can also be shortlisted based on their GATE score. The requirements are:","A valid GATE score from the current or previous year, as specified in the notification.","The GATE paper must be in the same engineering discipline as the qualifying degree."]},{"heading":"Selection Process & Exam Pattern","body":["The BARC selection is a two-stage process: a screening stage to shortlist candidates, followed by a selection interview which is the final determinant of merit."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: Screening for Interview","body":["Shortlisting for the interview is done through two independent channels. Candidates can opt for either or both.","BARC conducts its own discipline-specific computer-based test.","Candidates can also apply on the basis of their valid GATE score .","BARC sets a separate cut-off for each engineering discipline to shortlist candidates directly for the interview.","Applying through GATE is a separate shortlisting channel and does not depend on the online exam.","Note: The screening exam score or GATE score is only for shortlisting and has NO weightage in the final selection."]},{"heading":"Stage 2: Selection Interview","body":["This is the final and most crucial stage. The interview is a rigorous test of a candidate''s technical acumen.","Nature of Interview: It is purely technical, focusing on the fundamental concepts of the candidate''s engineering discipline. There are typically no HR or general knowledge questions.","Interview Panel: The board consists of senior scientists and experts from DAE.","Evaluation Criteria: The panel assesses the depth of understanding, analytical ability, and problem-solving skills. Candidates are expected to derive equations and explain concepts from first principles.","Location: Interviews are primarily conducted at the BARC campus in Mumbai.","Final Merit List","Final selection is based solely on performance in the personal interview .","Application Workflow","The application for BARC OCES/DGFS is a completely online process. Candidates must be careful while filling out the form as details submitted are final."]}]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,12) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.gov_exams (category,name,summary,content,links,display_order) values ('UPSC','BSSC JE','Bihar Junior Engineer Examination','[{"heading":"Overview","body":["Bihar Junior Engineer Examination"]},{"heading":"About the Examination","body":["The Bihar Staff Selection Commission (BSSC) Junior Engineer (JE) Examination is a state-level competitive exam conducted for the recruitment of Junior Engineers in various technical departments of the Government of Bihar. This examination is a significant opportunity for engineering diploma holders to secure a government job that plays a pivotal role in the state''s infrastructure development, public works, and maintenance of essential services.","The Role and Importance of a Junior Engineer","Junior Engineers form the backbone of the state''s technical workforce. They are responsible for the execution, supervision, and management of various government projects at the ground level. The BSSC JE exam aims to select candidates who possess:","Technical Proficiency: A strong foundational knowledge in their respective engineering discipline (Civil, Mechanical, or Electrical).","Practical Application Skills: The ability to apply engineering principles to real-world problems and projects.","Managerial Acumen: The capacity to manage resources, supervise work, and ensure quality standards are met within deadlines.","Integrity and Public Service Spirit: A commitment to ethical practices and serving the public by contributing to the state''s progress.","Departments and Posts","Successful candidates are allocated to various departments under the Bihar Government based on their rank, preferences, and the vacancies available. Key departments include:","and several others, depending on the specific recruitment notification."]},{"heading":"The Selection Process","body":["The selection process for BSSC JE is typically a single-stage competitive examination. It is designed to be a comprehensive test of both general awareness and deep technical knowledge.","Written Examination: This is the sole stage for selection. It consists of objective-type (Multiple Choice Questions) papers. The examination is divided into two main parts: one testing general knowledge, reasoning, and mental ability, and the other testing the candidate''s core technical knowledge in their specific engineering branch (e.g., Civil, Mechanical, Electrical).","Counselling & Document Verification: Candidates who qualify in the written examination based on the cut-off marks are called for counselling. During this stage, their original documents, eligibility, and credentials are verified."]},{"heading":"The Final Merit: Direct Path to Service","body":["The final merit list is prepared based on the total marks obtained in the written examination . There is typically no interview for this post. Based on their rank in the merit list and the departmental vacancies, candidates are recommended for appointment as Junior Engineers, marking their entry into the technical services of the Bihar Government."]},{"heading":"Typical Timeline (Indicative)","body":["Unlike the fixed annual schedule of UPSC, the BSSC JE examination timeline is not fixed and is based on the requirement of personnel in various departments. The process begins with the release of an official notification. The following is a generic, indicative schedule based on past trends.","Disclaimer: This timeline is highly variable. Candidates must always refer to the official BSSC website (bssc.bihar.gov.in) for the latest updates and exact dates for any specific recruitment cycle.","Phase 1: Notification & Application","Notification Release: The official notification is published on the BSSC website, detailing the number of vacancies, department-wise breakup, eligibility criteria, syllabus, exam pattern, and important dates.","Application Period: The online application window typically remains open for about 30 to 45 days . This involves online registration, filling the application form, payment of fees, and uploading of required documents.","Phase 2: Pre-Examination Formalities","Application Scrutiny: BSSC scrutinizes the applications for eligibility and correctness.","Admit Card Release: Approximately 2-3 weeks before the exam date , BSSC releases the e-Admit Card on its official website. Candidates must download and print it, as it is mandatory for entering the examination hall.","Phase 3: Written Examination","The written exam is conducted on the date(s) announced by the commission. It usually consists of two papers held on the same day or over different shifts. Paper I: General Studies Paper II: Technical Subject (Civil/Mechanical/Electrical)","Paper I: General Studies","Paper II: Technical Subject (Civil/Mechanical/Electrical)","Phase 4: Result Declaration & Counselling","Answer Key Release: A few days after the exam, a provisional answer key is released, and candidates are given a window to raise objections.","Written Exam Result: After considering the objections, the final result is prepared and published. This usually takes a few months. BSSC releases a list of roll numbers of candidates who have qualified for the next stage.","Counselling Schedule: Qualified candidates are called for Counselling and Document Verification. The schedule is published on the official website."]},{"heading":"Phase 5: Final Selection and Joining","body":["Final Merit List: Based on the written exam marks and successful document verification, a final merit list is published.","Department Allocation: BSSC recommends the names of the selected candidates to the concerned government departments for appointment.","Appointment: The respective departments issue appointment letters to the candidates, after which they can join the service."]},{"heading":"Eligibility Criteria","body":["Aspirants must carefully check their eligibility before applying for the BSSC JE examination. The criteria set by the Bihar Staff Selection Commission are strict, and any discrepancy can lead to cancellation of candidature at any stage. Eligibility is determined by Nationality, Age Limit, and Educational Qualification.","Note: The details provided are based on general rules for Bihar government jobs. The official notification for the specific examination year is the final authority.","1. Nationality","The candidate must be a citizen of India .","Candidates from other states are generally eligible to apply, but they will be considered under the Unreserved (General) category and will not be eligible for reservation benefits (like age relaxation) applicable to Bihar domicile candidates.","2. Age Limit","The age of a candidate is calculated as of a specific cut-off date mentioned in the official notification (usually 1st August or 1st January of the exam year).","Minimum Age: A candidate must have attained the age of 18 years .","Maximum Age: The upper age limit varies by category for candidates who are domiciles of Bihar."]},{"heading":"The upper age limits for various categories are as follows","body":["3. Educational Qualification","The minimum educational requirement is the cornerstone of eligibility for this technical post:","A candidate must possess a Diploma in Engineering in the relevant discipline (i.e., Civil, Mechanical, or Electrical) from an institution recognized by the All India Council for Technical Education (AICTE), New Delhi or a State Board of Technical Education.","Relevant Disciplines: Diploma in Civil Engineering Diploma in Mechanical Engineering Diploma in Electrical Engineering","Higher Qualifications: Candidates with higher technical qualifications like B.E./ B.Tech in the relevant engineering stream are also generally considered eligible to apply, provided the notification does not explicitly bar them.","Final-Year Students: Typically, candidates must have passed the qualifying diploma examination and possess the certificate as of the cut-off date mentioned in the notification. Appearing/final-year students are usually not eligible unless specified otherwise.","4. Number of Attempts","There is no specified limit on the number of attempts a candidate can take for the BSSC JE examination. A candidate can appear for the exam as many times as they wish, provided they fulfill the age and other eligibility criteria for each recruitment cycle."]},{"heading":"Exam Pattern & Marking Scheme","body":["The BSSC JE selection is based on a single-stage written examination. This exam is meticulously structured to evaluate both the general aptitude and the core technical expertise of the candidates. Understanding this pattern is fundamental to crafting an effective preparation strategy.","Written Examination (Objective Type)","The examination consists of two compulsory papers. The final merit list is prepared based on the total marks obtained in these papers.","Objective Format: All questions in both papers will be Multiple Choice Questions (MCQs).","Bilingual Paper: The question papers will be set in both Hindi and English . In case of any discrepancy, the English version will be considered final.","Negative Marking: There is a penalty for incorrect answers. For each wrong answer, 1 mark will be deducted , and for each correct answer, 4 marks will be awarded .","Merit Calculation: The final merit list for selection will be prepared based on the aggregate marks obtained by the candidates in both Paper I and Paper II.","Qualifying Nature: While there isn''t a separate qualifying paper like CSAT in UPSC, candidates are required to score a minimum qualifying percentage as per their category to be considered for the merit list. General Category: 40% BC Category: 36.5% EBC Category: 34% SC/ST, PwD, and all Female candidates: 32%","General Category: 40%","BC Category: 36.5%","EBC Category: 34%","SC/ST, PwD, and all Female candidates: 32%","Counselling and Document Verification","This is not an evaluation stage but a mandatory verification process. Candidates who are shortlisted based on their written exam performance are called for counselling.","Purpose: To verify the authenticity of the documents submitted during the application, such as educational certificates, caste certificates, domicile certificates, etc.","Process: Candidates must present their original documents for verification. Failure to produce the required documents can lead to disqualification.","Final Selection: After successful document verification, a final merit list is published, and candidates are recommended for appointment.","Application Workflow","The application process for the BSSC JE exam is conducted entirely online through the commission''s official portal. Aspirants must be extremely careful while filling out the form, as errors can lead to the rejection of their application."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: Online Registration","body":["The first step is to register on the BSSC online portal to create a user profile.","Go to the Official Website: Visit bssc.bihar.gov.in and find the link for the relevant JE recruitment.","Provide Basic Details: You will need to provide your name, father''s name, date of birth, a valid mobile number, and a valid email ID.","Verification: The mobile number and email ID are crucial as all communication, including registration ID and password, will be sent to them."]},{"heading":"Stage 2: Filling the Application Form","body":["After registration, log in with your credentials and fill out the detailed application form.","Personal Information: Fill in your personal details, correspondence and permanent address, category (General, BC, EBC, SC, ST), domicile information, etc.","Educational Qualification: Provide detailed information about your Diploma/Degree in Engineering, including the university/board, passing year, and marks obtained."]},{"heading":"Stage 3: Fee Payment","body":["After filling in the details, you will be directed to the payment gateway to pay the application fee.","Fee Structure (Indicative): General / BC / EBC / Other State Candidates: ₹750 SC / ST / PwD / All Female Candidates (of Bihar Domicile): ₹200","General / BC / EBC / Other State Candidates: ₹750","SC / ST / PwD / All Female Candidates (of Bihar Domicile): ₹200","Payment Modes: The fee can usually be paid online via Net Banking, Credit Card, Debit Card, or UPI."]}]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,13) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.gov_exams (category,name,summary,content,links,display_order) values ('UPSC','DRDO SET','DRDO Scientist Entry Test','[{"heading":"Overview","body":["DRDO Scientist Entry Test"]},{"heading":"About the Examination","body":["The Defence Research and Development Organisation (DRDO) is the premier R&D wing of the Ministry of Defence, Govt. of India, with a vision to empower India with cutting-edge defence technologies. The Scientist Entry Test (SET) is a highly competitive recruitment process conducted by DRDO''s Recruitment & Assessment Centre (RAC) to induct the nation''s brightest engineering and science graduates as Scientist ''B'' , a Group ''A'' Gazetted technical service of the Union."]},{"heading":"The Philosophy Behind the Recruitment","body":["Unlike conventional exams, the DRDO selection process is designed to identify individuals with a strong aptitude for research, development, and innovation. The core philosophy is to assess a candidate''s:","Fundamental Knowledge: A deep and clear understanding of core engineering principles.","Practical Application: The ability to apply theoretical knowledge to solve real-world, complex problems in defence technology.","Research Aptitude: A curious and analytical mind with the potential to contribute to novel research and development projects.","Commitment to National Security: A dedication to serving the nation by contributing to its technological self-reliance in defence.","The Role of a Scientist ''B''","A career as a DRDO Scientist is one of the most prestigious technical jobs in the country. New recruits are involved in the design, development, testing, and evaluation of state-of-the-art systems across a wide spectrum of technologies, including:"]},{"heading":"The Two-Stage Selection Process","body":["The selection is a rigorous two-stage process designed to filter for exceptional technical talent.","Stage 1: Shortlisting (The Technical Screen): This is the initial filtering stage. For most engineering disciplines, candidates are shortlisted based on their valid GATE (Graduate Aptitude Test in Engineering) scores . For some specific roles, a separate descriptive written examination may be conducted. The goal is to identify a pool of candidates with proven proficiency in their engineering domain.","Stage 2: Personal Interview (The Final Assessment): Shortlisted candidates are called for a final, in-depth personal interview. This is a crucial stage conducted by a panel of senior DRDO scientists and subject matter experts. It aims to assess the candidate''s technical knowledge, practical understanding, problem-solving skills, and suitability for a research and development environment.","The Final Verdict: Merit and Appointment","The final merit list is prepared based on a weighted score combining the performance in GATE and the Personal Interview , or the Written Exam and the Interview. Successful candidates receive an offer of appointment as Scientist ''B'' and undergo an induction program before being posted to one of DRDO''s many laboratories or establishments across India."]},{"heading":"Typical Timeline (Indicative)","body":["Unlike the fixed calendar of UPSC, the DRDO recruitment timeline is dynamic and depends on the vacancies advertised. The entire process, from notification to the final result, typically spans 6-8 months.","Disclaimer: This timeline is indicative. Always refer to the official RAC website for the exact schedule of a specific recruitment advertisement.","Phase 1: Notification & Application (Variable)","DRDO releases advertisements on the RAC website (rac.gov.in) and in the Employment News as per requirement. There is no fixed annual schedule.","The online application window typically remains open for 21-30 days from the date of notification.","Phase 2: Scrutiny and Shortlisting (1-2 Months Post Application)","After the application window closes, RAC scrutinizes all applications.","Candidates are shortlisted for the next stage (Interview or Written Exam) based on their GATE scores or other criteria mentioned in the advertisement.","A list of shortlisted candidates is published on the RAC website.","Phase 3: Written Examination (If Applicable)","If a written exam is part of the selection process for a particular discipline, it is conducted after the initial shortlisting. Admit cards are released about 2-3 weeks before the exam date.","Phase 4: Personal Interviews (2-4 Months Post Application)","Interview call letters are issued to shortlisted candidates via email and the RAC portal.","Interviews are typically conducted in person at the DRDO headquarters in Delhi or other designated centers. The interview schedule is spread over several weeks.","Phase 5: Final Result Declaration (1-2 Months Post Interview)","The final merit list, based on the combined weighted scores, is published on the RAC website."]},{"heading":"Eligibility Criteria","body":["Aspirants must strictly fulfill the eligibility criteria mentioned in the specific recruitment advertisement. The key parameters are Nationality, Age Limit, and Educational Qualification.","Note: The details below are general criteria. Always verify against the specific official notification you are applying for.","1. Nationality","The candidate must be a citizen of India .","2. Age Limit","The age limit is calculated as on the closing date of the application. The typical upper age limits are:","3. Educational Qualification","The educational qualification is highly specific to the discipline advertised. The fundamental requirements are:","Essential Qualification: A First Class Bachelor''s Degree in Engineering or Technology in the relevant discipline from a recognized university. A minimum of 60% marks or equivalent CGPA is required.","GATE Qualification: A valid GATE (Graduate Aptitude Test in Engineering) score in the specified GATE paper is mandatory for most engineering disciplines. The advertisement will specify the eligible GATE papers (e.g., EC for Electronics, ME for Mechanical, CS for Computer Science).","Final-Year Students: Candidates in the final year of their qualifying degree are generally eligible to apply, provided they can produce the final degree certificate by a specified date."]},{"heading":"Stages, Exam Pattern & Marking Scheme","body":["The DRDO selection process is designed to evaluate deep technical knowledge and research potential. It is primarily based on GATE scores and a Personal Interview."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: Shortlisting based on GATE Score","body":["This is the first and most critical filter.","Basis: Candidates are shortlisted for the interview based on their valid GATE scores in the relevant paper.","Ratio: The shortlisting is done in a ratio of approximately 1:10 (10 candidates for every 1 vacancy) for each discipline and category.","Cut-off: RAC releases the category-wise GATE score cut-offs for shortlisting after the process is complete. These cut-offs vary significantly each year based on the number of vacancies and the performance of applicants."]},{"heading":"Stage 2: Descriptive Examination (Not for all disciplines)","body":["In some specific cases or for certain advertisements where GATE is not the criterion, DRDO may conduct a descriptive written exam.","Pattern: Typically consists of two papers, each of 300 marks and 3 hours duration.","Syllabus: The questions are descriptive and cover the entire syllabus of the relevant engineering discipline, similar to the GATE and Engineering Services Examination (ESE) syllabus.","Purpose: To test the in-depth theoretical knowledge and problem-solving ability of the candidates."]},{"heading":"Stage 3: Personal Interview","body":["This is the final and decisive stage for all shortlisted candidates.","Maximum Marks: 100 Marks","Duration: Typically lasts for 15-30 minutes.","Focus: The interview panel, comprising senior DRDO scientists, assesses: Core subject knowledge and fundamental concepts. Practical application of engineering principles. Knowledge of final year project/thesis. Familiarity with recent technological advancements in their field. Communication skills, confidence, and suitability for an R&D career.","Core subject knowledge and fundamental concepts.","Practical application of engineering principles.","Knowledge of final year project/thesis.","Familiarity with recent technological advancements in their field.","Communication skills, confidence, and suitability for an R&D career.","Final Merit Calculation","The final selection is based on a weighted score. The scheme is clearly mentioned in the advertisement. A common pattern is:","80% weightage to GATE Score + 20% weightage to Personal Interview Marks","If a written exam is conducted, the weightage is usually 80% for the written exam score and 20% for the interview score. The GATE score is not considered in such cases. The final merit list is prepared based on this combined score.","Application Workflow","The application process for DRDO Scientist ''B'' recruitment is entirely online through the RAC portal. It is crucial to fill out the form accurately and upload documents in the prescribed format.","Step 1: Registration on RAC Portal","Visit the official RAC website: rac.gov.in .","Find the relevant advertisement for Scientist ''B'' recruitment and click on the application link.","Register as a new user by providing a valid email ID and mobile number, which will be verified via OTP.","Step 2: Filling the Online Application Form"]},{"heading":"After logging in, you will need to fill in several sections","body":["Personal Details: Name, parents'' names, date of birth, category, address, etc.","Educational Qualifications: Details of Class 10, Class 12, and your Engineering Degree (University, marks/CGPA, year of passing).","GATE Score Details: Registration number, year of appearance, score, marks out of 100, and All India Rank.","Step 3: Uploading Documents","You need to upload scanned copies of your documents. The specifications are strict.","Scanned Photograph: Recent passport-size colour photo. Format: JPG/JPEG. Size: Typically between 15 KB to 50 KB.","Scanned Signature: On white paper with a black pen. Format: JPG/JPEG. Size: Typically between 15 KB to 50 KB.","Certificates (in PDF format): Qualifying Degree Certificate. Valid GATE Scorecard. Caste/Category Certificate (for EWS/OBC/SC/ST candidates). Class 10 Certificate (for Date of Birth proof).","Qualifying Degree Certificate.","Valid GATE Scorecard.","Caste/Category Certificate (for EWS/OBC/SC/ST candidates).","Class 10 Certificate (for Date of Birth proof).","Step 4: Fee Payment","Amount: A non-refundable application fee of ₹100 (Rupees One Hundred only).","Exemptions: There is no fee for candidates belonging to SC, ST, PwD, and Women categories.","Payment Mode: The fee must be paid online through Net Banking, UPI, or Credit/Debit cards.","Step 5: Final Submission","After completing all steps, preview the entire application form carefully to check for any errors.","Once you are sure all details are correct, submit the form.","After final submission, download and take a printout of your application form for future reference. No changes can be made after the final submission."]},{"heading":"Syllabus","body":["The syllabus for the DRDO Scientist ''B'' recruitment is directly linked to the Graduate Aptitude Test in Engineering (GATE) syllabus for the respective disciplines.","For Shortlisting (GATE-based Entry)","The primary syllabus that candidates need to master is the official GATE syllabus for their specific engineering paper. DRDO uses the GATE score as a benchmark for a candidate''s technical proficiency. The syllabus covers all core subjects of the undergraduate engineering curriculum.","Candidates must thoroughly prepare for subjects under these common GATE papers:","Engineering Mathematics: Common to all branches.","General Aptitude: Common to all branches.","Core Discipline Subjects: As prescribed in the official GATE syllabus for papers like: Computer Science and Information Technology (CS) Electronics and Communication Engineering (EC) Mechanical Engineering (ME) Electrical Engineering (EE) Civil Engineering (CE) Aerospace Engineering (AE) Chemical Engineering (CH) And others as specified in the advertisement.","Computer Science and Information Technology (CS)","Electronics and Communication Engineering (EC)","Mechanical Engineering (ME)","Electrical Engineering (EE)","Civil Engineering (CE)","Aerospace Engineering (AE)","Chemical Engineering (CH)","And others as specified in the advertisement.","For Written Examination (If Applicable)","In the rare instance that a descriptive written test is conducted, the syllabus remains largely the same as the GATE and Engineering Services Examination (ESE) syllabus for that particular branch. It requires a more in-depth, theoretical understanding suitable for descriptive answers, derivations, and numerical problems. Aspirants should refer to the standard B.E./B.Tech curriculum for a comprehensive preparation.","Frequently Asked Questions","Here are answers to some common questions about the DRDO Scientist ''B'' recruitment."]}]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,14) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.gov_exams (category,name,summary,content,links,display_order) values ('UPSC','ISRO Scientist/Engineer ''SC''','Indian Space Research Organisation Recruitment','[{"heading":"Overview","body":["Indian Space Research Organisation Recruitment"]},{"heading":"The Philosophy Behind the Examination","body":["The selection process is designed to identify engineers with a strong foundation in their core discipline, exceptional problem-solving abilities, and a passion for innovation. ISRO seeks individuals who possess:","Deep Conceptual Clarity: A thorough understanding of fundamental engineering principles and their practical applications.","Analytical and Critical Thinking: The ability to analyze complex technical problems and devise logical, efficient solutions.","Passion for Space Technology: A genuine interest in and motivation for contributing to India''s space missions.","Adaptability and Learning Agility: The potential to work in a dynamic, multi-disciplinary R&D environment and continuously learn new technologies.","Disciplines and Opportunities","ISRO conducts recruitment for the Scientist/Engineer ''SC'' post across several engineering disciplines based on its requirements. The primary streams include:","Other specialized fields like Civil, Refrigeration & Air Conditioning, and Architecture are also recruited as per specific vacancies."]},{"heading":"The Two-Stage Selection Process","body":["The selection is a rigorous two-step process designed to filter for both theoretical knowledge and practical aptitude.","Stage 1: Written Test: This is an objective-type (MCQ) screening test focused entirely on the specific engineering discipline of the candidate. It is a comprehensive test of the core B.E./B.Tech curriculum. The primary role of this test is to shortlist a manageable number of candidates for the next stage.","Stage 2: Personal Interview: Candidates who clear the cut-off in the written test are called for a personal interview. This is a crucial and in-depth technical interview conducted by a panel of senior ISRO scientists and experts. It assesses the candidate''s grasp of fundamental concepts, practical knowledge, and overall suitability for a research and development role.","The Final Merit: A Balanced Assessment","The final merit list is prepared with a balanced weightage given to both stages. The final selection is based on the combined scores of the Written Test and the Personal Interview . Typically, a 50:50 weightage is assigned to both, making the interview as important as the written exam for securing a position."]},{"heading":"Typical Timeline (Indicative)","body":["Unlike the fixed annual calendar of UPSC, the ISRO Scientist/Engineer ''SC'' recruitment timeline is vacancy-driven and can be irregular . Notifications are released as and when vacancies arise in different centres. The following is a generalized timeline of events once a notification is published.","Disclaimer: This is a hypothetical timeline. Always refer to the specific, official ISRO notification for exact dates.","Phase 1: Notification & Application (Variable, e.g., May - June)","Notification Release: The official notification is published on the ISRO Careers portal (isro.gov.in/careers) and in the Employment News. This document contains all crucial details: number of vacancies per discipline, eligibility criteria, age cut-off date, syllabus, and exam centers.","Application Window: The online application window typically remains open for about 20-25 days . Candidates must apply through the designated portal.","Phase 2: Scrutiny and Admit Card (e.g., July - August)","Application Scrutiny: ISRO scrutinizes applications to ensure they meet all eligibility criteria.","Admit Card Release: E-Admit Cards for the written test are made available for download on the ISRO portal, usually 2-3 weeks before the exam date . An email or SMS alert is typically sent to registered candidates.","Phase 3: Written Examination (e.g., September)","The Written Test is conducted on a single day across various centres in India. It is a computer-based test (CBT) or offline OMR-based test for the specific engineering discipline.","Phase 4: Written Test Results & Interview Call Letters (e.g., October - November)","Result Declaration: The results of the written test, along with the cut-off marks, are published on the ISRO website. A list of shortlisted candidates for the interview is released.","Interview Call Letters: Shortlisted candidates can download their call letters for the personal interview, which will specify the date, time, and venue (usually a major ISRO centre).","Phase 5: Personal Interviews (e.g., November - December)","Interviews are conducted in batches over several days or weeks, depending on the number of candidates. Candidates must attend in person at the designated ISRO centre. Document verification is also done at this stage.","Phase 6: Final Result Declaration (e.g., January)","The final merit list, based on the combined scores (50% from written test + 50% from interview), is published on the ISRO website. This list contains the names of the provisionally selected candidates.","Phase 7: Joining Formalities (e.g., February - March)","Selected candidates receive their offer letters and are required to complete pre-employment formalities, including medical fitness tests. The joining date and initial posting location are communicated, marking the start of their journey at ISRO."]},{"heading":"Eligibility Criteria","body":["Aspirants must strictly meet the eligibility criteria specified in the official notification. These are non-negotiable and are verified at the time of the interview. The key parameters are Nationality, Educational Qualification, and Age Limit.","Note: Always verify the criteria from the specific recruitment notification, as cut-off dates and specific requirements can vary.","1. Nationality","The candidate must be a Citizen of India .","2. Educational Qualification","The minimum educational requirement is a first-class Bachelor''s degree in Engineering/Technology.","A candidate must have a B.E./B.Tech or equivalent qualification in the relevant discipline from a recognized University/Institution.","Minimum Marks: An aggregate minimum of 65% marks or a CGPA of 6.84 on a 10-point scale is required. This is a mandatory, non-negotiable requirement for all candidates.","Final-Year Students: Candidates who are in their final year of B.E./B.Tech are also eligible to apply, provided they can produce their final degree certificate by the date specified in the notification and have secured the required aggregate percentage.","3. Age Limit","The age of a candidate is calculated as of the closing date of the online application mentioned in the notification.","Maximum Age (General/UR): 28 years .","ISRO provides relaxations in the upper age limit for candidates from reserved categories:","Note on Attempts: Unlike UPSC, there is no limit on the number of attempts for the ISRO examination, as long as the candidate meets the age and educational criteria."]},{"heading":"Selection Process & Marking Scheme","body":["The selection process for the Scientist/Engineer ''SC'' post is a two-stage merit-based system. Each stage is designed to rigorously evaluate the candidate''s technical prowess and suitability for a scientific role at ISRO."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: Written Test (Objective Type)","body":["This is the screening stage designed to test the depth of a candidate''s knowledge in their specific engineering discipline.","Correct Answer: The marking scheme can vary slightly per notification, but a common pattern is +3 marks for each correct answer.","Incorrect Answer (Negative Marking): There is a penalty for wrong answers. Typically, -1 mark (or one-third of the marks for a correct answer) is deducted.","Unattempted Question: No marks are awarded or deducted.","Candidates are shortlisted for the interview in a ratio of approximately 1:5 (i.e., for every 1 vacancy, 5 candidates are called) based on their performance in the written test."]},{"heading":"Stage 2: Personal Interview","body":["The final stage is a comprehensive technical interview conducted by a panel of senior ISRO scientists. It is a true test of a candidate''s fundamental understanding and practical application of engineering concepts.","Maximum Marks: 100 Marks","Minimum Qualifying Marks: Candidates must score a minimum of 60 out of 100 marks in the interview to be considered for the final merit list.","Nature of Interview: It is highly technical and concept-driven. The panel probes the candidate''s knowledge of core subjects, their final year project, and their ability to apply principles to solve real-world problems. The focus is on clarity of thought and depth of understanding, not rote memorization.","Qualities Assessed: The board evaluates fundamental knowledge, problem-solving skills, analytical ability, practical orientation, and passion for the field.","Final Merit Calculation","The final selection is based on the aggregate score from both stages, with equal importance given to each.","50% Weightage from Written Test Score + 50% Weightage from Interview Score = Final Merit Score","The final merit list is generated based on this combined score, and candidates are selected in order of merit against the available vacancies for their discipline.","Application Workflow","The application process for the ISRO Scientist/Engineer ''SC'' examination is conducted entirely online through the official ISRO or affiliated centre''s portal. Candidates must ensure they have all necessary documents and details ready before starting the application.","Step 1: Find the Active Advertisement","Regularly check the ISRO Careers page for active recruitment notifications. Once a relevant advertisement (for your discipline) is found, read the notification document thoroughly before applying.","Step 2: Online Registration and Form Filling","Visit the Portal: Click on the application link provided in the advertisement.","Registration: Register on the portal using a valid email ID and mobile number. These will be used for all future communications.","Fill Application Form: Log in and fill in the online application form with accurate details, including: Personal information (Name, DOB, Category, etc.). Educational qualifications (University, Year of Passing, Percentage/CGPA for all semesters). Mailing address and contact details. Choice of written test city from the available list.","Personal information (Name, DOB, Category, etc.).","Educational qualifications (University, Year of Passing, Percentage/CGPA for all semesters).","Mailing address and contact details.","Choice of written test city from the available list.","Step 3: Uploading Documents","Candidates are required to upload scanned copies of their recent photograph and signature as per the specifications mentioned in the notification.","Scanned Photograph: A recent passport-size colour photograph with a light background (Format: JPG/JPEG, Size: typically under 50 KB).","Scanned Signature: Signature on white paper with a black pen (Format: JPG/JPEG, Size: typically under 50 KB).","Step 4: Payment of Application Fee","Fee Amount: A non-refundable application fee, typically ₹250 (Rupees Two Hundred and Fifty only). However, initially, all candidates have to uniformly pay the processing fee.","Refund Policy: The processing fee is refunded to candidates who are exempted (Women, SC/ST, Ex-Servicemen, PwBD) and to others who appear for the written test.","Payment Modes: The fee can be paid online using Net Banking, Debit/Credit Cards, or UPI.","Step 5: Final Submission and Printout","Review: Before final submission, carefully review the entire application form for any errors.","Submit: Once you are sure all details are correct, submit the application.","Printout: After successful submission and payment, a unique Registration Number is generated. Download and take a printout of the application form and the payment receipt for your records and future reference.","Common Mistakes to Avoid","Incorrect Percentage/CGPA: Ensure you calculate your aggregate percentage exactly as per your university''s formula. Incorrect entry can lead to disqualification.","Applying for the Wrong Discipline: Double-check that you are applying for the post corresponding to your B.E./B.Tech degree.","Last-Minute Rush: Avoid applying on the last day to prevent issues with server load or payment failures.","Blurry Photo/Signature: Upload clear, legible images as per the required dimensions and size to avoid rejection."]},{"heading":"Detailed Syllabus","body":["The syllabus for the ISRO Scientist/Engineer ''SC'' written test is based on the fundamental curriculum of a standard B.E./B.Tech degree in the respective discipline. The questions are designed to test a deep, conceptual understanding of these core subjects.","Computer Science & Engineering (CSE)","Number Systems & Boolean Algebra: Logic gates, K-Maps, simplification of expressions.","Combinational & Sequential Circuits: Adders, Multiplexers, Decoders, Flip-Flops, Counters, Shift Registers.","Data Representation: Integer and floating-point number representation (IEEE 754).","CPU Architecture: ALU, data-path, control unit design (hardwired, micro-programmed).","Instruction Pipelining & Addressing Modes: Hazards, instruction formats.","Memory Hierarchy: Cache memory (mapping techniques), main memory, I/O interface (DMA, Interrupts).","Core Concepts: Programming in C, Recursion, Pointers, Structures.","Linear Data Structures: Arrays, Stacks, Queues, Linked Lists.","Non-Linear Data Structures: Trees (BST, AVL, B-trees), Heaps, Graphs, Hashing.","Analysis: Asymptotic notations (Big-O, Omega, Theta).","Techniques: Greedy, Dynamic Programming, Divide and Conquer.","Core Algorithms: Searching, Sorting, Graph algorithms (Shortest Paths, MST).","Automata Theory: Regular Expressions, Finite Automata, Context-Free Grammars, Turing Machines.","Compiler Phases: Lexical analysis, Parsing (LL, LR), Syntax-directed translation, Code optimization.","Core Concepts: Processes, Threads, CPU Scheduling algorithms, Deadlocks.","Synchronization & Memory: Semaphores, Monitors, Paging, Segmentation, Virtual Memory, Page Replacement.","Models & Languages: ER-Model, Relational Algebra, SQL.","Design & Transactions: Normalization (1NF, 2NF, 3NF, BCNF), ACID properties, Concurrency Control, Indexing (B+ Trees).","Layered Models: OSI and TCP/IP models.","Protocols: IP (v4/v6), TCP, UDP, HTTP, DNS, SMTP.","Concepts: Routing algorithms, Subnetting, MAC protocols (CSMA/CD), Congestion Control, Network Security basics.","Electronics & Communication Engineering (ECE)","Network Analysis: KCL, KVL, Thevenin''s, Norton''s, Maximum Power Transfer theorems. Transient analysis.","Signals & Systems: Continuous and discrete-time signals, LTI systems, Fourier, Laplace, and Z-transforms.","Semiconductor Physics: Energy bands, carrier transport.","Diodes & Transistors: P-N Junction diodes, Zener diodes, BJT, JFET, MOSFET, MOS capacitor.","Diode Circuits & Amplifiers: Clipping, clamping, rectifiers. BJT and MOS amplifiers (biasing, small signal analysis).","Operational Amplifiers: Characteristics and applications (inverting, non-inverting, integrator, differentiator).","Oscillators and Feedback Amplifiers.","Combinational & Sequential Circuits: Multiplexers, Decoders, Flip-Flops, Counters.","Data Converters & Logic Families: ADC, DAC, TTL, CMOS logic.","System Modeling: Transfer function, block diagrams, signal flow graphs.","Stability Analysis: Routh-Hurwitz and Nyquist criteria, Bode plots, root loci.","Controllers: PID controllers, state-space analysis.","Analog Communication: Amplitude Modulation (AM), Frequency Modulation (FM), Phase Modulation (PM).","Digital Communication: PCM, DPCM, Digital modulation schemes (ASK, PSK, FSK, QAM), Error control coding.","Information Theory: Entropy, channel capacity theorem.","Maxwell''s Equations & Wave Propagation.","Transmission Lines: Characteristic impedance, impedance matching, Smith chart.","Antennas and Waveguides.","Mechanical Engineering (ME)","Statics & Dynamics: Free-body diagrams, friction, trusses, virtual work, kinematics.","Strength of Materials: Stress, strain, elastic constants, bending of beams, torsion of circular shafts, columns and struts.","Kinematics: Mechanisms, velocity and acceleration analysis.","Dynamics: Gears, governors, balancing of rotating masses, gyroscopes.","Vibrations: Free and forced vibrations of single-degree-of-freedom systems, damping.","Laws of Thermodynamics: First and Second laws, entropy, availability.","Properties of Pure Substances & Gas Mixtures.","Power Cycles: Otto, Diesel, Dual, Rankine, Brayton cycles.","Applications: IC Engines, Refrigeration and Air-conditioning.","Fluid Properties & Statics: Manometry, buoyancy.","Fluid Dynamics: Bernoulli''s equation, flow through pipes, boundary layer concept.","Modes of Heat Transfer: Conduction, convection (free and forced), radiation.","Heat Exchangers.","Engineering Materials: Structure and properties, heat treatment.","Metal Casting, Forming, and Joining Processes: Forging, rolling, welding, brazing.","Machining and Machine Tool Operations.","Operations Research: Linear programming, PERT and CPM.","Electrical Engineering (EE)","Circuit Analysis: KCL, KVL, Node and Mesh analysis, Network theorems.","Transient & Steady-state Analysis: RL, RC, and RLC circuits, AC analysis.","Electromagnetic Fields: Maxwell’s equations, Poynting vector.","Signal Representation: Continuous and discrete-time signals.","Transforms: Fourier, Laplace, and Z-transforms.","System Analysis: LTI systems, causality, stability.","Transformers: Single phase and three-phase transformers, auto-transformer.","DC Machines: DC motors and generators.","AC Machines: Three-phase induction motors, synchronous motors, and generators.","Generation, Transmission & Distribution: Power plants, transmission line models, economic load dispatch.","System Analysis: Load flow studies, fault analysis (symmetrical components), stability analysis.","Protection: Circuit breakers, relays.","System Modeling: Transfer function, block diagrams.","Time & Frequency Domain Analysis: Routh-Hurwitz, Nyquist criteria, Bode plots, Root Locus.","Controllers & Compensators: Lag, lead, lead-lag compensators, PID controllers.","Analog: Diode circuits, BJT & FET amplifiers, operational amplifiers.","Digital: Combinational and sequential circuits, logic gates.","Microprocessors: 8085 architecture, programming, and interfacing.","Semiconductor Devices: Thyristors, MOSFETs, IGBTs.","Converters: AC-DC (rectifiers), DC-DC (choppers), DC-AC (inverters).","Frequently Asked Questions","Here are answers to some common queries regarding the ISRO Scientist/Engineer ''SC'' examination."]},{"heading":"Regarding Eligibility & Application","body":["Can I apply if I have less than 65% in my B.Tech? No. The requirement of an aggregate minimum of 65% marks (or 6.84 CGPA) is a strict, mandatory eligibility criterion. Applications not meeting this will be rejected.","Is a GATE score required or considered for this exam? No. For the Scientist/Engineer ''SC'' recruitment through this exam, a GATE score is not required or given any weightage. Selection is based purely on ISRO''s own Written Test and Interview.","I am in my 7th semester. Can I apply? Yes, usually. Most notifications allow final-year students to apply. However, you must have completed your degree and obtained the provisional/final certificate with the required percentage by a specified cut-off date (usually before the interview stage).","Regarding the Written Exam","Is there a sectional cut-off in the written test? No. There are no sectional cut-offs. The shortlisting for the interview is based on the total score obtained in the single-paper written test.","Can I use a calculator in the exam? No. Physical calculators are not allowed. For Computer Based Tests (CBT), a virtual scientific calculator is typically provided on the screen.","What is the level of difficulty of the written test? The difficulty level is generally considered to be between GATE (moderate) and ESE (difficult). The questions are highly conceptual and test the core fundamentals of engineering subjects. Speed and accuracy are key."]},{"heading":"Regarding the Interview & Final Selection","body":["What kind of questions are asked in the ISRO interview? The interview is almost entirely technical. The panel will ask in-depth questions from your favourite subjects (which they''ll ask you to list), your final year project, and practical applications of concepts. They are looking for clarity and a strong grasp of fundamentals.","What is the weightage of the interview in the final selection? The interview carries a very high weightage. The final merit list is prepared with 50% weightage given to the written test score and 50% to the interview score. A strong interview performance is crucial for selection.","Is it mandatory to score 60% in the interview? Yes. To be considered for empanelment in the final merit list, a candidate must secure a minimum of 60 out of 100 marks in the interview, regardless of how high their written test score is.","What happens after the final result? Selected candidates receive an offer of appointment. They undergo an induction training program upon joining and are then posted as Scientist/Engineer ''SC'' at one of the many ISRO centres or units across India.","Disclaimer","This content is provided for general informational purposes only and does not constitute legal, academic, or professional advice; users should verify details from official sources before acting on the information.","Information Usage","All information is presented “as is,” without warranties of completeness, accuracy, or fitness for a particular purpose.","Policies, rules, formats, fees, schedules, eligibility, and procedures are subject to change; always refer to the official ISRO recruitment notification for the active cycle.","Liability","No liability is assumed for any loss, damage, or consequences arising from reliance on the information, including but not limited to academic, financial, or professional decisions.","Users are responsible for ensuring compliance with the current guidelines, eligibility norms, and deadlines published by the official authorities.","Contact for Corrections","For corrections, concerns, or takedown requests regarding content or assets, please reach out through the contact options provided in the app."]}]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,15) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.gov_exams (category,name,summary,content,links,display_order) values ('UPSC','RRB JE / SSE','Railway Recruitment Board - Junior / Senior Section Engineer','[{"heading":"Overview","body":["Railway Recruitment Board - Junior / Senior Section Engineer"]},{"heading":"About the Examination","body":["The Railway Recruitment Board (RRB) Junior Engineer (JE) and Senior Section Engineer (SSE) examinations are highly sought-after national-level recruitment drives conducted to select technical supervisors for the Indian Railways. These roles form the backbone of the railway''s technical workforce, ensuring the safe and efficient operation of one of the world''s largest rail networks.","Note: While JE recruitment is conducted periodically based on vacancies, the direct recruitment for the SSE post has been paused in recent years, with these positions being filled through internal promotions. This guide covers the process generally applicable to both, with a focus on the current JE recruitment pattern."]},{"heading":"The Philosophy Behind the Examination","body":["Unlike administrative exams, the RRB JE/SSE selection process is engineered to identify candidates with strong technical foundations, problem-solving skills, and a steadfast commitment to safety and precision . The examination seeks to evaluate a candidate''s:","Technical Proficiency: A deep and practical understanding of core engineering principles relevant to their specific discipline.","Analytical and Logical Reasoning: The ability to quickly analyze technical problems and arrive at logical solutions under pressure.","General Awareness & Scientific Temper: A solid understanding of basic science and an awareness of current affairs, crucial for a well-rounded technical supervisor.","Physical and Medical Fitness: The capacity to meet the stringent medical standards required for jobs that are critical to railway safety and operations.","The Posts and Departments","Successful candidates are allocated to various technical departments within the Indian Railways based on their engineering discipline, merit, and medical fitness. These roles are pivotal in the construction, maintenance, and operation of railway infrastructure."]},{"heading":"The Multi-Stage Selection Process","body":["The RRB JE selection is a rigorous multi-stage process designed to filter candidates based on technical knowledge, aptitude, and medical fitness.","Stage 1: First Stage Computer Based Test (CBT-1): This is a common screening test for all candidates, regardless of their engineering discipline. It is an objective-type (MCQ) exam designed to test general aptitude, reasoning, and scientific knowledge. Its primary purpose is to shortlist a large number of applicants for the next stage. Marks from this stage are not counted for the final merit list.","Stage 2: Second Stage Computer Based Test (CBT-2): This is the main technical examination and the real test of a candidate''s domain knowledge. Candidates who clear CBT-1 are eligible for this stage. It consists of both common subjects and a large section dedicated to the specific technical discipline of the candidate. The score in this stage is the sole determinant of a candidate''s position in the final merit list.","Stage 3: Document Verification (DV) and Medical Examination: Candidates shortlisted based on their CBT-2 performance are called for Document Verification. Here, all original academic and category certificates are verified. Following a successful DV, candidates are sent for a mandatory and stringent medical examination at a designated Railway Hospital. Clearing the medical test according to the standards prescribed for the post is non-negotiable.","The Final Verdict: Panel and Appointment","A final panel of selected candidates is prepared purely based on the marks obtained in the Second Stage CBT (CBT-2) , provided they clear the Document Verification and the required Medical Examination. Candidates are then allocated a zone and post, marking the beginning of their career as a Junior Engineer in the Indian Railways."]},{"heading":"Typical Timeline (Indicative)","body":["Unlike the fixed annual calendar of UPSC, RRB recruitments are not conducted every year. They are announced based on the vacancies reported by various railway zones. The entire process, from notification to the final panel, can take anywhere from 1 to 2 years.","Disclaimer: This is a generic timeline based on past recruitment drives. Always refer to the official Centralised Employment Notification (CEN) for exact dates.","Phase 1: Notification & Application (Month 1-2)","Notification Release: The official Centralised Employment Notification (CEN) is published in the Employment News and on all official RRB websites, detailing vacancies, eligibility, syllabus, and dates.","Application Period: The online application window opens and typically remains active for about 30 days ."]},{"heading":"Phase 2: First Stage CBT (Month 4-6)","body":["Application Status & City Intimation: RRBs release the application status and inform candidates of their exam city and date about 10-15 days before the exam.","Admit Card Release: The e-Admit Card (Hall Ticket) can be downloaded 4 days prior to the candidate''s specific exam date.","CBT-1 Examination: The exam is conducted online in multiple shifts over several days or weeks across the country.","Phase 3: CBT-1 Result & CBT-2 Shortlisting (Month 7-8)","Answer Key & Objections: A provisional answer key is released, and candidates are given a window to raise objections.","CBT-1 Result: The results, including the scores (raw and normalized) and the list of candidates shortlisted for CBT-2, are published. The number of candidates shortlisted is typically 15 times the number of vacancies."]},{"heading":"Phase 4: Second Stage CBT (Month 9-10)","body":["Admit Card for CBT-2: Similar to CBT-1, admit cards are released 4 days before the exam date.","CBT-2 Examination: The technical stage of the exam is conducted, also in multiple shifts.","Phase 5: CBT-2 Result & DV Shortlisting (Month 12-14)","Answer Key & Result: The answer key and final result for CBT-2 are declared.","DV Shortlisting: A list of candidates shortlisted for Document Verification is published, usually in a 1:1 ratio (or slightly more) to the number of vacancies.","Phase 6: Document Verification & Medical Exam (Month 15-18)","DV Schedule: Shortlisted candidates are called for DV at the respective RRB headquarters on specific dates.","Medical Examination: Immediately after DV, candidates are issued a memo and sent to the designated Railway Hospital for the medical examination, which may take 2-4 days.","Phase 7: Final Panel & Joining (Month 19 onwards)","Final Result (Panel): After compiling the results of DV and the medical exam, the RRB publishes the final panel of selected candidates.","Appointment: The respective Zonal Railways then handle the process of issuing appointment letters and conducting training."]},{"heading":"Eligibility Criteria","body":["Aspirants must ensure they meet all the eligibility criteria mentioned in the official notification regarding Nationality, Age, and Educational Qualifications. These criteria are strictly enforced.","Note: The details provided here are based on the latest recruitment patterns. The official CEN for the examination year is the final authority.","1. Nationality"]},{"heading":"A candidate must be","body":["A citizen of India, or","A subject of Nepal, or","A subject of Bhutan, or","A Tibetan refugee who came over to India before January 1, 1962, with the intention of permanently settling in India, or","A person of Indian origin (PIO) who has migrated from Pakistan, Burma, Sri Lanka, East African countries, etc., with the intention of permanently settling in India.","2. Age Limit (for RRB JE)","The age is calculated as of a cut-off date mentioned in the notification (e.g., 1st January of the recruitment year).","Minimum Age: 18 years","Maximum Age: 33 years (for the General/UR category)","Relaxations in the upper age limit are provided for reserved categories:","3. Educational Qualification (as on closing date of application)","This is the most critical criterion. The required qualification must be from a recognized University/Institute.","For Junior Engineer (JE): A candidate must possess either: A three-year Diploma in the relevant engineering discipline specified for the post. A B.E. / B.Tech / B.Sc (Engineering) degree in the relevant engineering discipline.","A three-year Diploma in the relevant engineering discipline specified for the post.","A B.E. / B.Tech / B.Sc (Engineering) degree in the relevant engineering discipline.","For Depot Material Superintendent (DMS): A three-year Diploma or a Degree in Engineering from any discipline.","For Chemical & Metallurgical Assistant (CMA): Bachelor''s Degree in Science with Physics and Chemistry with a minimum of 45% marks.","Important: Candidates who are awaiting the results of their final examinations are NOT eligible to apply. The educational qualification must be fully completed on or before the closing date of the application."]},{"heading":"Stages, Exam Pattern & Marking Scheme","body":["The RRB JE selection process is composed of two stages of Computer Based Tests (CBTs), followed by Document Verification and a mandatory Medical Examination."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: First Stage CBT (Common Screening Test)","body":["This is a qualifying test to screen candidates for the second stage. The marks from this stage are not used for the final merit list. The level of questions is generally of the 10th/12th standard.","Duration: 90 minutes (120 minutes for eligible PwBD candidates).","Negative Marking: Yes. 1/3rd of a mark is deducted for every incorrect answer.","Normalization: Marks are normalized across different shifts to account for variations in difficulty levels.","Minimum Qualifying Marks: UR-40%, OBC-30%, SC-30%, ST-25%."]},{"heading":"Stage 2: Second Stage CBT (Technical & Merit-Deciding Stage)","body":["This stage decides the final merit of the candidates. The exam is divided into two parts, conducted in a single session.","Duration: 120 minutes (160 minutes for eligible PwBD candidates).","Virtual Calculator: An on-screen scientific calculator will be provided for calculations. Physical calculators are not allowed.","Merit Calculation: The final panel is prepared solely based on the normalized marks obtained in this stage."]}]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,16) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.gov_exams (category,name,summary,content,links,display_order) values ('UPSC','UPSC CAPF (AC)','UPSC Central Armed Police Forces (Assistant Commandants) Examination','[{"heading":"Overview","body":["UPSC Central Armed Police Forces (Assistant Commandants) Examination"]},{"heading":"About the Examination","body":["The Union Public Service Commission (UPSC) Central Armed Police Forces (CAPF) Examination is a national-level competitive exam for the recruitment of Assistant Commandants (AC) , who are Group ''A'' officers. This examination serves as the unified gateway for aspiring candidates to join the officer cadre of India''s premier internal security forces. It is a highly respected career path that offers a unique blend of adventure, responsibility, and service to the nation."]},{"heading":"The Philosophy Behind the Examination","body":["The CAPF (AC) examination is designed to select young, dynamic, and resilient leaders. Beyond academic knowledge, the selection process rigorously assesses a candidate''s physical and mental fortitude, integrity, and suitability for command roles in challenging operational environments. The exam seeks individuals who possess:","Physical and Mental Robustness: The ability to endure tough physical conditions and maintain composure under extreme pressure.","Leadership and Command Quality: The potential to lead a company of armed personnel, make critical decisions, and inspire confidence.","Intellectual Acuity: A strong grasp of general awareness, analytical reasoning, and security-related issues.","Unwavering Integrity: A strong moral compass and a deep sense of patriotism and duty.","The Forces at Stake","Successful candidates are appointed as Assistant Commandants in one of the five Central Armed Police Forces under the Ministry of Home Affairs. These forces are the pillars of India''s internal security architecture."]},{"heading":"The Three-Stage Selection Process","body":["The CAPF (AC) selection is a multi-stage process designed to comprehensively evaluate a candidate''s fitness for the role. The entire cycle spans over a year.","Stage 1: Written Examination: This is the first filter and consists of two papers conducted on the same day. Paper I is an objective test of General Ability and Intelligence, while Paper II is a descriptive test of General Studies, Essay, and Comprehension. This stage assesses the academic and analytical capabilities of the candidates.","Stage 2: Physical Standards Test (PST) / Physical Efficiency Test (PET) & Medical Standards Test: Candidates who qualify the written exam are called for this stage. The PST involves checking physical measurements like height and chest. The PET includes a series of athletic events to test physical endurance and agility. This is followed by a detailed medical examination to ensure candidates meet the stringent health requirements. This stage is qualifying in nature .","Stage 3: Interview / Personality Test: The final stage is an interview conducted by a board of senior officers and experts at the UPSC. This test assesses the candidate''s personality, leadership potential, communication skills, and overall suitability for a command position in the armed police forces.","The Final Verdict: Merit and Allocation","The final merit list is prepared based on the total marks obtained in the Written Examination (450 marks) and the Interview/Personality Test (150 marks) , for a grand total of 600 marks. Based on their final rank and the preferences submitted by them, candidates are allocated to one of the five CAPFs."]},{"heading":"Typical Timeline (Indicative)","body":["The UPSC CAPF (AC) Examination follows a relatively predictable annual schedule. Understanding this timeline is essential for planning your preparation effectively.","Disclaimer: These timelines are based on past trends. Always refer to the official UPSC calendar for the specific year for exact dates.","Phase 1: Notification & Application (April - May)","Late April: The official notification for the CAPF (AC) Examination is released by UPSC on its website (upsc.gov.in).","April - May: The online application window opens and typically remains active for about 20-25 days.","Phase 2: Written Examination (August)","July: The e-Admit Card for the Written Examination is released approximately 3 weeks before the exam date.","First Sunday of August: The Written Examination (Paper I & Paper II) is conducted on a single day across the country.","Phase 3: Written Exam Result (September - October)","The results of the Written Examination are typically declared within 40-50 days. A list of qualified candidates is published, who must then fill a Detailed Application Form (DAF).","Phase 4: PST / PET & Medical Tests (December - February)","Qualified candidates are called for the Physical and Medical tests. These are conducted in batches at various centers designated by the Nodal Authority (like ITBP, BSF, etc.).","Phase 5: Interview / Personality Test (May - June of next year)","Candidates who clear the physical and medical tests are summoned for the final interview at the UPSC office in New Delhi.","Phase 6: Final Result Declaration (July - August of next year)","The final merit list, based on the combined scores of the Written Examination and Interview, is declared, marking the end of the selection cycle."]},{"heading":"Eligibility Criteria","body":["The eligibility criteria for the CAPF (AC) exam are very strict, especially regarding age and physical standards. Candidates must verify their eligibility meticulously.","Note: The official UPSC notification is the ultimate authority on all eligibility criteria.","1. Nationality","A candidate must be a citizen of India . (Subjects of Nepal and Bhutan are not eligible).","2. Age Limit","The age is calculated as of the 1st of August of the examination year.","Minimum Age: 20 years .","Maximum Age: 25 years . (A candidate must not have attained the age of 25 years).","3. Educational Qualification","A candidate must hold a Bachelor''s degree from a University incorporated by an Act of the Central or State Legislature in India or other educational institutions established by an Act of Parliament or declared to be deemed as a University.","4. Physical and Medical Standards","This is a critical, non-negotiable criterion. Candidates must meet the prescribed physical and medical standards.","Distant Vision (Corrected): Better Eye (6/6 or 6/9) & Worse Eye (6/12 or 6/9).","Near Vision: Better Eye (N6) & Worse Eye (N9).","Candidates must not have knock knees, flat feet, varicose veins, or squint in their eyes. They should be in good mental and bodily health."]},{"heading":"Stages, Exam Pattern & Marking Scheme","body":["The CAPF (AC) selection process is a comprehensive three-stage journey."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: Written Examination (Total 450 Marks)","body":["Conducted on a single day in two sessions.","Negative Marking: There is a penalty of one-third (1/3rd) of the marks for each wrong answer in Paper I only.","Evaluation Rule: Paper II is only evaluated for those candidates who secure a minimum qualifying mark in Paper I, as decided by the commission."]},{"heading":"Stage 2: Physical Efficiency Test (PET) - Qualifying","body":["Candidates must qualify in all the following events. Failure in any one event leads to disqualification."]},{"heading":"Stage 3: Interview / Personality Test (150 Marks)","body":["Candidates who clear the PET and are declared medically fit are called for the interview. The board assesses their suitability for a command role, evaluating their mental alertness, critical thinking, leadership qualities, and overall personality.","Final Rank Calculation","The final merit list is prepared based on the marks from the Written Exam and the Interview.","Written Exam (Paper I + II): 450 Marks + Interview / Personality Test: 150 Marks = Grand Total: 600 Marks","Application Workflow","The application for the UPSC CAPF (ACs) exam is a completely online process conducted through the official UPSC website. It requires careful attention to detail to avoid rejection."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: One-Time Registration (OTR)","body":["This is a mandatory, one-time process for all UPSC exams. Candidates must register on the OTR platform (upsconline.nic.in), providing basic details like name, date of birth, mobile number, and email ID. This information is saved and auto-populated for future applications, and it can be updated."]},{"heading":"Stage 2: Filling the Examination Application Form","body":["After completing OTR, candidates can log in and apply for the specific CAPF (ACs) Examination. This involves:","Part-I Registration: Confirming personal and educational details, and crucially, selecting preferences for the forces (e.g., BSF, CRPF, CISF, ITBP, SSB).","Part-II Registration: This involves paying the application fee, selecting the examination center, and uploading a recent photograph, signature, and a valid Photo ID document (like Aadhar Card, Voter ID, etc.) as per the specified format.","Amount: A non-refundable fee of ₹200 (Rupees Two Hundred only).","Exemptions: Female candidates and candidates belonging to Scheduled Caste (SC) and Scheduled Tribe (ST) categories are exempted from paying the fee.","Payment Modes: The fee can be paid online via Net Banking, UPI, or Credit/Debit cards, or offline by depositing cash at an SBI branch.","Centre Selection: Exam centers are allotted on a \"first-apply-first-allot\" basis for the Written Exam. Apply early to get your preferred center.","Document Specifications: Ensure your photograph, signature, and Photo ID upload meet the exact size, format, and clarity requirements mentioned in the official notification to avoid rejection."]}]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,17) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.gov_exams (category,name,summary,content,links,display_order) values ('UPSC','UPSC CDS','The Union Public Service Commission (UPSC) Combined Defence Services (CDS) Examination is a prestigious national-level exam conducted twice a year to recruit Commissioned Officers into the Indian Armed Forces. Unlike civilian services, the ','[{"heading":"About the Examination","body":["The Union Public Service Commission (UPSC) Combined Defence Services (CDS) Examination is a prestigious national-level exam conducted twice a year to recruit Commissioned Officers into the Indian Armed Forces. Unlike civilian services, the CDS exam is the primary gateway for university graduates to embark on a challenging and honourable career leading the men and women of the Indian Army, Navy, and Air Force."]},{"heading":"The Philosophy Behind the Examination","body":["The CDS selection process is designed to identify candidates who possess the unique blend of attributes required for military leadership, often summarized as \"Officer Like Qualities\" (OLQs). The exam assesses more than academic knowledge; it seeks individuals with:","Mental Robustness & Aptitude: The ability to think clearly, solve problems logically, and perform under pressure.","Physical Fitness & Courage: A high standard of physical and mental endurance to meet the demanding nature of military life.","Leadership & Integrity: The potential to lead, inspire confidence, and uphold the highest standards of honour and integrity.","Patriotism & Sense of Duty: A deep-seated commitment to the nation and an unwavering sense of duty and responsibility.","The Academies at Stake","Successful candidates are admitted to different defence academies based on their preference and merit. The CDS exam serves as the entry point for the following prestigious institutions:"]},{"heading":"The Two-Stage Selection Process","body":["The CDS selection is a comprehensive two-stage process designed to thoroughly evaluate a candidate''s suitability for a commission in the Armed Forces.","Stage 1: Written Examination: This is an objective-type (MCQ) examination designed to test the candidate''s knowledge of English, General Knowledge, and Elementary Mathematics. It serves as a screening test to select candidates for the next stage. Candidates applying only for the Officers'' Training Academy (OTA) are exempted from the Mathematics paper.","Stage 2: Services Selection Board (SSB) Interview: Candidates who qualify in the written exam are called for the SSB Interview. This is an intensive 5-day personality and intelligence assessment. It includes a battery of psychological tests, group tasks, and a personal interview to evaluate a candidate''s \"Officer Like Qualities\" (OLQs). This stage is the ultimate test of one''s character, leadership potential, and mental fortitude.","The Final Verdict: Merit and Joining Instructions","A final merit list is prepared based on the total marks obtained in the Written Examination and the SSB Interview . Candidates who clear the medical examination are issued joining instructions for their respective academies based on their rank, preferences, and the number of vacancies available."]},{"heading":"Typical Timeline (Indicative)","body":["The CDS Examination is conducted biannually, providing two opportunities each year for aspiring candidates. The two cycles are referred to as CDS (I) and CDS (II) . The timeline for each cycle spans approximately 9-10 months from notification to the commencement of the course.","Disclaimer: These timelines are illustrative. Always refer to the official UPSC calendar for exact dates."]},{"heading":"Typical Timeline for a Single CDS Cycle","body":["Phase 1: Notification & Application (e.g., Oct-Nov for CDS I / May-Jun for CDS II) The official notification is released by UPSC, and the online application window opens for about 20-25 days.","Phase 2: Admit Card Release (e.g., Jan-Feb for CDS I / Aug for CDS II) E-Admit cards are made available for download from the UPSC website around 3 weeks before the exam date.","Phase 3: Written Examination (e.g., Feb or Apr for CDS I / Sep for CDS II) The objective-type written exam is conducted on a single day across various centers in India.","Phase 4: Written Examination Result (e.g., Mar-May for CDS I / Oct-Nov for CDS II) UPSC declares the results of the written exam, publishing a list of qualified candidates'' roll numbers.","Phase 5: SSB Interview Process (Spans Several Months) Qualified candidates are allotted dates for their 5-day SSB interviews. This phase is conducted by the respective services (Army, Navy, Air Force) and can take place over several months following the written result.","Phase 6: Final Result Declaration (e.g., Jul-Aug for CDS I / Feb-Mar for CDS II) A final merit list is published, containing the names of candidates recommended after the SSB interviews.","Phase 7: Course Commencement (e.g., Jan for CDS I / Jul for CDS II) Following medical examinations and other formalities, the course commences at the respective training academies."]},{"heading":"Eligibility Criteria","body":["Eligibility for the CDS Examination is highly specific and varies for each academy. Key criteria include nationality, age, marital status, and educational qualifications.","Note: The official UPSC notification for the specific examination cycle is the final authority on all eligibility conditions.","1. Nationality"]},{"heading":"A candidate must be unmarried and must either be","body":["a Citizen of India, or","a subject of Nepal, or","a person of Indian origin who has migrated from Pakistan, Burma, Sri Lanka and East African Countries of Kenya, Uganda, the United Republic of Tanzania, Zambia, Malawi, Zaire and Ethiopia or Vietnam with the intention of permanently settling in India.","2. Marital Status","For IMA, INA, and AFA: Candidates must be unmarried males .","For OTA (Men & Women): Candidates (both male and female) must be unmarried .","Candidates must not marry during the period of training. Marriage during training will result in discharge from the academy.","3. Age Limit & Educational Qualification","Age and educational requirements are distinct for each academy and are strictly enforced.","Note for Final-Year Students: Candidates who are in their final year/semester of a degree course are also eligible to apply, provided they can produce proof of passing the degree examination by the specified date mentioned in the official notification."]},{"heading":"Stages & Exam Pattern","body":["The CDS selection process is a two-stage affair: a written exam followed by an SSB interview for qualified candidates."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: Written Examination (Objective Type)","body":["The pattern of the written exam differs for candidates aspiring for IMA, INA, and AFA versus those for the OTA.","Objective Format: All papers consist of Multiple Choice Questions (MCQs).","Negative Marking: There is a penalty for wrong answers. One-third (0.33) of the marks assigned to a question will be deducted for each incorrect answer.","Language of Paper: The question papers for General Knowledge and Elementary Mathematics will be set bilingually in Hindi as well as English."]},{"heading":"Stage 2: Services Selection Board (SSB) Interview","body":["This is a comprehensive personality and intelligence test conducted over 5 days. It is not an academic knowledge test but an evaluation of \"Officer Like Qualities\" (OLQs). The SSB process is divided into two stages.","Stage I (Screening): This is a one-day screening process involving an Officer Intelligence Rating (OIR) test and a Picture Perception & Description Test (PPDT). Candidates who clear Stage I proceed to Stage II.","Psychological Tests: Thematic Apperception Test (TAT), Word Association Test (WAT), Situation Reaction Test (SRT), and Self Description Test (SD).","Group Testing Officer (GTO) Tasks: A series of indoor and outdoor group exercises, including group discussions, group planning exercises, and command tasks.","Personal Interview: A one-on-one conversation with a senior officer (the Interviewing Officer).","Conference: On the final day, all assessors jointly discuss each candidate''s performance before finalizing their recommendation.","Final Merit Calculation","The final merit list is prepared based on the combined marks of the Written Exam and the SSB Interview.","For IMA, INA, AFA: Written Exam (300) + SSB Interview (300) = Total 600 Marks For OTA: Written Exam (200) + SSB Interview (200) = Total 400 Marks","Application Workflow","The application for the UPSC CDS exam is a completely online process conducted through the official UPSC website. It requires careful attention to detail to avoid rejection."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: One-Time Registration (OTR)","body":["This is a mandatory, one-time process for all UPSC exams. Candidates must register on the OTR platform, providing basic details like name, date of birth, mobile number, and email ID. This information is saved and auto-populated for future applications."]},{"heading":"Stage 2: Filling the Examination Application Form","body":["After OTR, candidates can log in and apply for the specific CDS Examination. This involves:","Part-I Registration: Confirming personal and educational details, and crucially, selecting preferences for the academies (IMA, INA, AFA, OTA).","Part-II Registration: This involves paying the application fee, selecting the examination center, and uploading a recent photograph and signature as per the specified format.","Amount: A non-refundable fee of ₹200 (Rupees Two Hundred only).","Exemptions: Female candidates and candidates belonging to Scheduled Caste (SC) and Scheduled Tribe (ST) categories are exempted from paying the fee.","Payment Modes: The fee can be paid online via Net Banking, UPI, or Credit/Debit cards, or offline via cash deposit at an SBI branch.","Centre Selection: Exam centers are allotted on a \"first-apply-first-allot\" basis. Apply early to get your preferred center.","Document Specifications: Ensure your photograph and signature uploads meet the exact size and format requirements mentioned in the notification."]}]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,18) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.gov_exams (category,name,summary,content,links,display_order) values ('UPSC','UPSC CSE','The CSE is engineered to assess more than just rote memorization. Its core philosophy is to identify individuals who possess a potent combination of intellectual depth, analytical acuity, ethical integrity, and a profound sense of social co','[{"heading":"The Philosophy Behind the Examination","body":["The CSE is engineered to assess more than just rote memorization. Its core philosophy is to identify individuals who possess a potent combination of intellectual depth, analytical acuity, ethical integrity, and a profound sense of social commitment . The examination seeks to evaluate a candidate''s:","Critical Assimilation: The ability to understand complex information from diverse fields, filter out noise, and grasp the core issues.","Balance of Judgment: The capacity to form reasoned opinions, consider multiple perspectives, and make sound, unbiased judgments under pressure.","Intellectual and Moral Integrity: A strong ethical compass, honesty, and the courage to uphold constitutional values.","Leadership and Social Cohesion: The potential to lead, inspire, and work collaboratively to solve pressing societal problems.","Awareness and Curiosity: A deep and abiding interest in the world around them, from local village issues to global geopolitical shifts."]},{"heading":"The Services at Stake","body":["Successful candidates are allocated to various services based on their rank and preferences. These services are broadly classified into two categories:","1. All India Services: These services are common to both the Union and State governments. Officers are recruited by the Centre but their services are placed under various State cadres, and they are liable to serve both the Centre and the States.","2. Central Civil Services (Group ''A'' and ''B''): These services function directly under the Government of India and are involved in the administration of Union subjects."]},{"heading":"Key Group ''A'' Services","body":["and several others."]},{"heading":"The Three-Tiered Selection Gauntlet","body":["The CSE is a multi-layered process where each stage serves as a progressively finer filter to identify the most suitable candidates. The entire cycle, from notification to the final result, spans over a year.","Stage 1: Preliminary Examination (The Great Filter): This is an objective-type (Multiple Choice Questions) screening test. With an application pool often exceeding a million candidates for roughly 10,000-15,000 Mains spots, its primary role is elimination. It tests the breadth of a candidate''s knowledge and their aptitude for logical reasoning. Crucially, the marks from this stage are not counted for the final merit list. It is a pass/fail gateway to the next level.","Stage 2: Main Examination (The Crucible of Knowledge): Candidates who clear the Prelims undertake the Main Examination, a descriptive (written) exam that is the real test of intellectual depth and analytical skill. Spread over nine papers, it evaluates a candidate''s ability to articulate nuanced arguments, structure comprehensive answers, and demonstrate mastery over their chosen optional subject. The marks from the Main Examination are the most significant component of the final score.","Stage 3: Personality Test (The Final Appraisal): The final stage is an interview conducted by a board of distinguished experts at the UPSC headquarters in New Delhi. It is explicitly described as a \"purposive conversation\" rather than a strict cross-examination. The board, having access to the candidate''s Detailed Application Form (DAF), assesses their personality, mental alertness, leadership qualities, and suitability for a high-stakes career in public administration.","The Final Verdict: Merit and Allocation"]},{"heading":"Typical Timeline (Indicative)","body":["The UPSC Civil Services Examination is a marathon that unfolds over a period of 14-15 months, from the date of notification to the start of the Foundation Course. Understanding this timeline is crucial for strategic planning, sustained preparation, and mental conditioning. The following is a typical, indicative schedule.","Disclaimer: These timelines are based on past trends. Always refer to the official UPSC calendar for the specific year for exact dates.","Phase 1: Notification & Application (February - March)","Early February: The official notification for the Civil Services Examination is released by UPSC on its website (upsc.gov.in). This document is the bible for aspirants, containing all essential details: vacancy numbers, eligibility criteria, syllabus, exam rules, and the list of examination centers.","February - March: The online application window opens on the day of the notification and typically remains active for about 20-25 days . This process involves a one-time registration (OTR) and filling out the application form in two parts. Aspirants must be extremely careful while filling in details, selecting centers (which are allotted on a first-apply-first-allot basis), and uploading documents.","Phase 2: Prelims Preparation & Admit Card (April - May)","April - May: This is a period of intense, focused preparation and revision for the Preliminary Examination.","Late April / Early May: Approximately 3-4 weeks before the exam date , UPSC releases the e-Admit Card for the Preliminary Examination. Candidates must download and print it. It is mandatory to carry the admit card along with a valid, original Photo ID to the examination hall.","Phase 3: Preliminary Examination (Late May / Early June)","The Preliminary Exam is conducted on a single day (usually a Sunday). It consists of two compulsory papers with a break in between. Paper I: General Studies (9:30 AM to 11:30 AM) Paper II: CSAT (2:30 PM to 4:30 PM)","Paper I: General Studies (9:30 AM to 11:30 AM)","Paper II: CSAT (2:30 PM to 4:30 PM)","Phase 4: Mains Qualification & DAF-I (July - August)","Mid to Late July: The results of the Preliminary Examination are typically declared within 30-40 days of the exam date. UPSC releases a list of the roll numbers of qualified candidates.","July - August: Shortly after the Prelims result, the qualified candidates are required to fill out the Detailed Application Form-I (DAF-I) . This is an exhaustive form that collects detailed information about the candidate''s personal background, educational qualifications, parental details, and employment history. It is also where candidates must indicate their optional subject for the Main Exam and provide preferences for services and cadres. This form is the basis for the interview later on.","Phase 5: Main Examination (September / October)","Late August / Early September: Admit cards for the Main Examination are released for candidates who have successfully submitted their DAF-I.","September / October: The Main Examination is a descriptive (written) test conducted over 5 days , usually spread across two weekends. It consists of 9 papers, testing the depth of a candidate''s knowledge and analytical ability.","Phase 6: Personality Test Qualification & DAF-II (December - January)","December / Early January: The results of the Main Examination are usually announced about 2.5 to 3 months after the exam. A list of candidates who have qualified for the Personality Test (Interview) is published.","January: Qualified candidates are then required to fill the Detailed Application Form-II (DAF-II) . This form often requires them to reconfirm their service preferences and provide additional details which might be used by the interview board.","Phase 7: Personality Tests (Interviews) (February - April)","The Personality Tests (Interviews) typically commence a few weeks after the Mains results are declared.","Candidates are summoned to the UPSC office (Dholpur House) in New Delhi in batches. The interview schedule is spread over several weeks, often concluding by mid or late April.","Phase 8: Final Result Declaration (Late April / May)","This is the culmination of the entire process. The final merit list, based on the combined scores of the Main Examination and the Personality Test, is declared. This usually happens within 5-10 days of the conclusion of the last interview.","Phase 9: Post-Result Formalities (June - August)","The Department of Personnel and Training (DoPT) undertakes the crucial task of service and cadre allocation based on ranks, preferences, category, and vacancies.","Late August / Early September: The Foundation Course for all newly recruited officers begins at the Lal Bahadur Shastri National Academy of Administration (LBSNAA) in Mussoorie, officially starting their journey as civil servants."]},{"heading":"Eligibility Criteria","body":["Before embarking on the rigorous journey of UPSC preparation, every aspirant must meticulously verify their eligibility. The criteria set by the Union Public Service Commission are strict and non-negotiable. Eligibility is primarily determined by four key parameters: Nationality, Age Limit, Educational Qualification, and Number of Attempts.","Note: The details provided here are based on prevailing rules. These are subject to change, and the official UPSC notification for the current examination year is the ultimate authority.","1. Nationality","The nationality requirement varies depending on the service an individual aspires to join.","For Indian Administrative Service (IAS), Indian Police Service (IPS), and Indian Foreign Service (IFS): The candidate must be a citizen of India . There are no exceptions for these premier services.","A citizen of India, or","A subject of Nepal, or","A subject of Bhutan, or","A Tibetan refugee who came over to India before the 1st of January, 1962, with the intention of permanently settling in India, or","A person of Indian origin (PIO) who has migrated from Pakistan, Burma, Sri Lanka, East African countries of Kenya, Uganda, the United Republic of Tanzania, Zambia, Malawi, Zaire, Ethiopia and Vietnam with the intention of permanently settling in India.","Candidates belonging to the latter categories must possess a certificate of eligibility issued by the Government of India.","2. Age Limit","The age of a candidate is calculated as of a specific cut-off date, which is typically the 1st of August of the examination year. The general age limits are as follows:","Minimum Age: A candidate must have attained the age of 21 years .","Maximum Age: A candidate must not have attained the age of 32 years (for the General category).","This means a candidate from the General category must have been born not earlier than 2nd August, 1993 and not later than 1st August, 2004 (for the 2025 exam, as an example).","UPSC provides relaxations in the upper age limit for candidates belonging to reserved categories:","Note: Cumulative relaxations are generally not allowed. For instance, an OBC candidate with a benchmark disability can claim a relaxation of 10 years, not 10 + 3 years.","3. Educational Qualification"]},{"heading":"The minimum educational requirement is straightforward","body":["A candidate must hold a Bachelor''s degree from any university incorporated by an Act of the Central or State Legislature in India or other educational institutions established by an Act of Parliament or declared to be deemed as a University under Section-3 of the UGC Act, 1956.","Alternatively, a candidate can possess an equivalent qualification.","Final-Year Students: Candidates who have appeared or intend to appear for their final degree examination and are awaiting results are also eligible to apply. However, they must produce proof of passing the said examination when they apply for the Main Examination.","Professional and technical qualifications recognized by the Government as equivalent to a professional and technical degree would also be sufficient.","4. Number of Attempts","The number of times a candidate can appear for the Preliminary Examination is limited. Appearing in even one paper of the Preliminary Exam is counted as an attempt.","An attempt is counted only if a candidate physically appears for at least one paper in the Preliminary Examination. Filling out the application form but not showing up for the exam does not count as an attempt."]},{"heading":"Stages, Exam Pattern & Marking Scheme","body":["The Civil Services Examination is a meticulously designed three-stage process. Each stage has a distinct purpose, format, and evaluation methodology. A thorough understanding of this pattern is the first step toward building a successful preparation strategy."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: Preliminary Examination (Objective Type)","body":["The primary purpose of the Prelims is to screen candidates for the Main Examination. It is a test of wide-ranging general knowledge and mental aptitude. The marks obtained in this stage are not counted for the final merit list.","Objective Format: Both papers consist of Multiple Choice Questions (MCQs).","Negative Marking: There is a penalty for incorrect answers. For each wrong answer, one-third (1/3rd) of the marks assigned to that question will be deducted. For a 2-mark question, this means a deduction of 0.66 marks.","CSAT is Qualifying: Paper II (CSAT) is a qualifying paper. A candidate must score a minimum of 33% (i.e., 66 out of 200 marks) in this paper. If a candidate fails to score 33% in CSAT, their Paper I (GS) will not be evaluated, and they will be disqualified.","Merit Calculation: The cut-off for qualifying for the Main Examination is determined solely based on the marks scored in General Studies Paper I , provided the candidate has scored the minimum qualifying marks in CSAT."]},{"heading":"Stage 2: Main Examination (Descriptive Type)","body":["This stage is the core of the selection process and is designed to assess the intellectual depth and understanding of a candidate rather than just their memory. The marks obtained here are the primary determinant of a candidate''s final rank. It consists of 9 descriptive papers.","Candidates must clear these language papers to have their other Mains papers evaluated.","These papers are of matriculation or equivalent standard. Candidates from certain North-Eastern states may be exempted from Paper A.","The sum of marks from these seven papers determines the written score for the final merit list.","Candidates choose one optional subject from a list provided by UPSC, which has two papers."]},{"heading":"Stage 3: Personality Test (Interview)","body":["The final stage is a face-to-face interview with the UPSC board. It is not a test of knowledge, which has already been evaluated in the Mains, but an assessment of personality, suitability for public service, and mental calibre.","Maximum Marks: 275 Marks","Basis of Interview: The conversation is largely guided by the candidate''s Detailed Application Form (DAF), covering their hobbies, academic background, work experience, and service preferences.","Qualities Assessed: The board evaluates mental alertness, critical powers of assimilation, clear and logical exposition, balance of judgment, variety and depth of interest, ability for social cohesion and leadership, and intellectual and moral integrity.","Final Rank Calculation","The final merit list is prepared based on the cumulative score of the Main Examination and the Personality Test.","Main Exam (Written) Marks: 1750 + Personality Test (Interview) Marks: 275 = Grand Total: 2025 Marks","Application Workflow","The application process for the UPSC CSE is conducted exclusively online through the official UPSC website. It is a meticulous process that requires careful attention to detail. Any error can lead to the rejection of the application. The process can be broken down into several key stages."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: One-Time Registration (OTR)","body":["Before filling out the application form for any UPSC examination, a candidate must complete the One-Time Registration. As the name suggests, this is a one-time activity.","Purpose: The OTR platform saves a candidate''s basic personal information, which can then be automatically populated whenever they apply for a UPSC exam. This saves time and reduces the chance of errors in subsequent applications.","Information Required: You will need to provide your name, parents'' names, date of birth, gender, minority status, a valid mobile number, a valid email ID, and details of your Class 10 board examination roll number.","Verification: The mobile number and email ID are verified through OTPs. It is crucial to use an active and personal mobile number and email, as all future communication from UPSC will be sent to them.","Permanence: The information submitted in the OTR profile is stored securely and can be updated, with some limitations, at any time."]}]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,19) on conflict do nothing;
insert into public.gov_exams (category,name,summary,content,links,display_order) values ('UPSC','UPSC ESE','The ESE is engineered to assess more than just theoretical knowledge. Its core philosophy is to identify individuals who possess a potent combination of deep technical expertise, robust analytical and problem-solving skills, and strong lead','[{"heading":"The Philosophy Behind the Examination","body":["The ESE is engineered to assess more than just theoretical knowledge. Its core philosophy is to identify individuals who possess a potent combination of deep technical expertise, robust analytical and problem-solving skills, and strong leadership potential . The examination seeks to evaluate a candidate''s:","Technical Competence: A thorough and profound understanding of the fundamental principles of their engineering discipline.","Problem-Solving Ability: The capacity to apply engineering concepts to solve complex, real-world problems efficiently and innovatively.","Managerial Acumen: The potential to lead teams, manage large-scale projects, and make sound technical and administrative decisions under pressure.","Ethical Foundation: A strong sense of professional ethics, integrity, and a commitment to public service and national development.","Aptitude and General Awareness: A broad awareness of current affairs, general studies, and logical reasoning, which are essential for a well-rounded technocrat."]},{"heading":"The Services and Disciplines","body":["The ESE is conducted for recruitment into four main engineering disciplines. Successful candidates are allocated to various Group ''A'' and Group ''B'' services based on their rank, preferences, and the vacancies in their respective disciplines."]},{"heading":"Some of the key services under these categories include","body":["and many others across various ministries and departments."]},{"heading":"The Three-Tiered Selection Gauntlet","body":["The ESE is a multi-layered process where each stage is designed to filter candidates based on specific skills and knowledge. The entire cycle, from notification to the final result, spans over a year.","Stage 1: Preliminary Examination (The Technical Screen): This is an objective-type (Multiple Choice Questions) screening test comprising two papers. Paper-I tests General Studies and Engineering Aptitude, while Paper-II is discipline-specific. Its primary role is to select candidates with a solid foundation in both general and technical areas for the next stage. Crucially, the marks from this stage are not counted for the final merit list.","Stage 3: Personality Test (The Professional Appraisal): The final stage is an interview conducted by a board of experts at the UPSC headquarters in New Delhi. This interview assesses the candidate''s suitability for a career in public service from a technical and managerial perspective. The board evaluates leadership qualities, initiative, intellectual curiosity, mental and physical energy, and other traits required for a successful career as an engineer in a government service.","The Final Verdict: Merit and Allocation","The final merit list is prepared based on the total marks obtained in the Main Examination (600 marks) and the Personality Test (200 marks) , for a grand total of 800 marks. Based on their final rank in their specific engineering discipline, the preferences for services submitted by them, their category, and the vacancies available, candidates are recommended for appointment to various engineering services under the Government of India."]},{"heading":"Typical Timeline (Indicative)","body":["The UPSC Engineering Services Examination is a marathon that unfolds over a period of 12-14 months, from the date of notification to the declaration of the final result. Understanding this timeline is crucial for strategic planning and sustained preparation. The following is a typical, indicative schedule based on past trends.","Disclaimer: These timelines are based on past trends. Always refer to the official UPSC calendar for the specific year for exact dates.","Phase 1: Notification & Application (September - October)","Mid to Late September: The official notification for the Engineering Services Examination is released by UPSC on its website (upsc.gov.in). This document contains all essential details: vacancy numbers, eligibility criteria, syllabus, exam rules, and the list of examination centers.","September - October: The online application window opens on the day of the notification and typically remains active for about 20-25 days . This process involves a one-time registration (OTR) and filling out the application form. Aspirants must be extremely careful while filling in their engineering discipline, selecting centers, and uploading documents.","Phase 2: Prelims Preparation & Admit Card (January - February)","November - January: This is a period of intense, focused preparation and revision for the Preliminary Examination.","Late January / Early February: Approximately 3 weeks before the exam date , UPSC releases the e-Admit Card for the Preliminary Examination. Candidates must download and print it.","Phase 3: Preliminary Examination (February)","The Preliminary Exam is conducted on a single day (usually a Sunday in February). It consists of two compulsory papers with a break in between. Paper I: General Studies & Engineering Aptitude (10:00 AM to 12:00 PM) Paper II: Engineering Discipline Specific (2:00 PM to 5:00 PM)","Paper I: General Studies & Engineering Aptitude (10:00 AM to 12:00 PM)","Paper II: Engineering Discipline Specific (2:00 PM to 5:00 PM)","Phase 4: Mains Qualification & DAF-I (April - May)","Late March / Early April: The results of the Preliminary Examination are typically declared within 30-40 days of the exam date. UPSC releases a list of the roll numbers of qualified candidates.","April - May: Shortly after the Prelims result, the qualified candidates are required to fill out the Detailed Application Form-I (DAF-I) . This is an exhaustive form that collects detailed information about the candidate''s personal background, educational qualifications, and service preferences. This form is the basis for the interview later on.","Phase 5: Main Examination (June / July)","May / June: Admit cards for the Main Examination are released for candidates who have successfully submitted their DAF-I.","June / July: The Main Examination is a descriptive (written) test conducted on a single day, with two sessions for the two discipline-specific papers. It tests the depth of a candidate''s technical knowledge and problem-solving ability.","Phase 6: Personality Test Qualification (September / October)","September / October: The results of the Main Examination are usually announced about 2 to 3 months after the exam. A list of candidates who have qualified for the Personality Test (Interview) is published.","Phase 7: Personality Tests (Interviews) (October - December)","The Personality Tests (Interviews) typically commence a few weeks after the Mains results are declared.","Candidates are summoned to the UPSC office (Dholpur House) in New Delhi in batches. The interview schedule is spread over several weeks.","Phase 8: Final Result Declaration (December / January)","This is the culmination of the entire process. The final merit list, based on the combined scores of the Main Examination and the Personality Test, is declared. This usually happens within 10-15 days of the conclusion of the last interview."]},{"heading":"Eligibility Criteria","body":["Before embarking on the rigorous journey of UPSC ESE preparation, every aspirant must meticulously verify their eligibility. The criteria set by the Union Public Service Commission are strict and non-negotiable. Eligibility is primarily determined by three key parameters: Nationality, Age Limit, and Educational Qualification.","Note: The details provided here are based on prevailing rules. These are subject to change, and the official UPSC notification for the current examination year is the ultimate authority.","1. Nationality","A candidate for the Engineering Services Examination must be one of the following:","A citizen of India, or","A subject of Nepal, or","A subject of Bhutan, or","A Tibetan refugee who came over to India before the 1st of January, 1962, with the intention of permanently settling in India, or","A person of Indian origin (PIO) who has migrated from Pakistan, Burma, Sri Lanka, East African countries of Kenya, Uganda, the United Republic of Tanzania, Zambia, Malawi, Zaire, Ethiopia and Vietnam with the intention of permanently settling in India.","Candidates belonging to the latter categories must possess a certificate of eligibility issued by the Government of India.","2. Age Limit","The age of a candidate is calculated as of a specific cut-off date, which is typically the 1st of January of the examination year. The general age limits are as follows:","Minimum Age: A candidate must have attained the age of 21 years .","Maximum Age: A candidate must not have attained the age of 30 years (for the General category).","This means a candidate from the General category must have been born not earlier than 2nd January, 1996 and not later than 1st January, 2005 (for the 2026 exam, as an example).","UPSC provides relaxations in the upper age limit for candidates belonging to reserved categories:","3. Educational Qualification","This is the most critical eligibility criterion for ESE. The minimum educational requirement is:","A candidate must have obtained an Engineering Degree from a University incorporated by an Act of the Central or State Legislature in India or other Educational Institutions established by an Act of Parliament or declared to be deemed as Universities under Section 3 of the University Grants Commission Act, 1956.","Alternatively, a candidate must have passed Sections A and B of the Institution Examinations of the Institution of Engineers (India).","Obtained a degree/diploma in Engineering from such foreign University/College/Institution and under such conditions as may be recognised by the Government for the purpose from time to time.","Passed Graduate Membership Examination of the Institution of Electronics and Telecommunication Engineers (India).","Passed Associate Membership Examination Parts II and III/Sections A and B of the Aeronautical Society of India.","Final-Year Students: Candidates who have appeared or intend to appear for their final degree examination and are awaiting results are also eligible to apply. However, they must produce proof of passing the said examination along with the Detailed Application Form (DAF-I) for the Main Examination.","The degree must be in one of the four eligible disciplines: Civil, Mechanical, Electrical, or Electronics & Telecommunication Engineering .","4. Number of Attempts","There is no restriction on the number of times a candidate can appear for the ESE. Candidates can attempt the examination as many times as they wish, provided they fulfill the age limit and other eligibility criteria specified in the examination notification."]},{"heading":"Stages, Exam Pattern & Marking Scheme","body":["The Engineering Services Examination is a meticulously designed three-stage process. Each stage has a distinct purpose, format, and evaluation methodology. A thorough understanding of this pattern is the first step toward building a successful preparation strategy."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: Preliminary Examination (Objective Type)","body":["The primary purpose of the Prelims is to screen candidates for the Main Examination. It tests both general aptitude and core engineering knowledge. The marks obtained in this stage are not counted for the final merit list.","Objective Format: Both papers consist of Multiple Choice Questions (MCQs).","Negative Marking: There is a penalty for incorrect answers. For each wrong answer, one-third (1/3rd) of the marks assigned to that question will be deducted.","Cut-off Calculation: The cut-off for qualifying for the Main Examination is determined based on the total marks scored in both Paper I and Paper II. There are minimum qualifying marks for each paper as decided by the commission."]},{"heading":"Stage 2: Main Examination (Descriptive Type)","body":["This stage is the core of the selection process and is designed to assess the in-depth technical knowledge and problem-solving skills of a candidate. The marks obtained here are the primary determinant of a candidate''s final rank. It consists of 2 descriptive papers specific to the candidate''s engineering stream.","Both papers are subjective and require detailed, written answers. The use of non-programmable scientific calculators is permitted in this stage."]},{"heading":"Stage 3: Personality Test (Interview)","body":["The final stage is a face-to-face interview with the UPSC board. It is not just a test of technical knowledge but an assessment of the candidate''s overall personality, leadership potential, and suitability for a high-responsibility technical career in the government.","Maximum Marks: 200 Marks","Basis of Interview: The conversation is largely guided by the candidate''s Detailed Application Form (DAF), covering their academic and project background, work experience, and interests.","Qualities Assessed: The board evaluates leadership, initiative, intellectual curiosity, judgement, mental alertness, and the ability to apply technical knowledge to practical situations.","Final Rank Calculation","The final merit list is prepared based on the cumulative score of the Main Examination and the Personality Test.","Main Exam (Written) Marks: 600 + Personality Test (Interview) Marks: 200 = Grand Total: 800 Marks","Application Workflow","The application process for the UPSC ESE is conducted exclusively online through the official UPSC website. It is a meticulous process that requires careful attention to detail. Any error can lead to the rejection of the application. The process can be broken down into several key stages."]},{"heading":"Stage 1: One-Time Registration (OTR)","body":["Before filling out the application form for any UPSC examination, a candidate must complete the One-Time Registration. As the name suggests, this is a one-time activity.","Purpose: The OTR platform saves a candidate''s basic personal information, which can then be automatically populated whenever they apply for a UPSC exam. This saves time and reduces the chance of errors in subsequent applications.","Information Required: You will need to provide your name, parents'' names, date of birth, gender, minority status, a valid mobile number, a valid email ID, and details of your Class 10 board examination roll number.","Verification: The mobile number and email ID are verified through OTPs. It is crucial to use an active and personal mobile number and email, as all future communication from UPSC will be sent to them.","Permanence: The information submitted in the OTR profile is stored securely and can be updated, with some limitations, at any time."]},{"heading":"Stage 2: Filling the Examination Application Form","body":["Once your OTR is complete, you can log in and apply for the Engineering Services (Preliminary) Examination. This is typically a two-part process."]}]'::jsonb,'[]'::jsonb,20) on conflict do nothing;
